-- ---- Music ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('11111111-0001-0004-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'The Tritone: The Devil''s Interval',
   'Medieval musicians called the tritone — the interval spanning three whole tones — "diabolus in musica" (the devil in music) and avoided it in sacred composition. Today it is the foundation of blues and jazz harmony.',
   'The tritone divides the octave exactly in half, creating maximum harmonic tension. Its instability comes from acoustics: the frequency ratios involved create beating and dissonance that the ear strongly wants to resolve. Church authorities in the medieval period discouraged its use in plainchant, though historians debate whether any formal ban existed. Composers like Wagner exploited its unresolved tension deliberately — the opening chord of "Tristan und Isolde" uses a tritone to create yearning that lasts four hours. In blues, the flattened fifth (a tritone) is the defining note of the genre.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('11111111-0001-0005-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Why Music Triggers Autobiographical Memory',
   'Music is uniquely powerful at retrieving autobiographical memories — often more vividly than any other cue. The mechanism involves the hippocampus and amygdala interacting in a way that makes musical memories unusually resistant to decay.',
   'Neurologist Oliver Sacks documented cases of severe amnesia patients who could recall decades-old songs perfectly while unable to recognize family members. Research shows that the brain processes music through regions deeply interconnected with emotional and autobiographical memory systems. The dorsomedial prefrontal cortex — one of the last regions to atrophy in Alzheimer''s disease — is activated during music-evoked autobiographical memory, which explains why music therapy reaches dementia patients when other stimuli cannot. The "reminiscence bump" (strongest memories from ages 10–30) is particularly pronounced for music.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('11111111-0001-0006-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Timbre: The Color of Sound',
   'Timbre is what makes a violin and a flute sound different even when playing the same pitch at the same volume. It is the sonic fingerprint of an instrument — shaped by harmonics, attack, and resonance.',
   'When any instrument plays a note, it produces not just the fundamental frequency but a series of overtones (harmonics) at integer multiples of that frequency. The relative loudness of these harmonics determines timbre. A clarinet''s cylindrical bore suppresses even harmonics, giving it a hollow, reedy sound. A violin''s irregular wooden resonator emphasizes different harmonics, producing warmth. Modern synthesizers manipulate timbre through additive synthesis (layering harmonics) or subtractive synthesis (filtering them). Timbre research has revealed that the first 50 milliseconds of a note — the attack transient — contain most of the information the ear uses to identify an instrument.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('11111111-0001-0007-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'The 27 Club Is a Statistical Myth',
   'The "27 Club" — the idea that musicians disproportionately die at age 27 — is a compelling narrative but statistically unsupported. Studies find no spike in musician mortality at 27 compared to adjacent ages.',
   'A 2011 study in the British Medical Journal analyzed death rates among top musicians from 1956–2007 and found no elevated risk at age 27 specifically. The list of famous 27-year-old deaths (Jimi Hendrix, Janis Joplin, Jim Morrison, Kurt Cobain, Amy Winehouse) is real but small. The phenomenon is a textbook case of the availability heuristic combined with confirmation bias: we remember the famous cases, ignore the many musicians who died at 26, 28, or 34, and the narrative takes on a life of its own. What is true is that musicians as a group have elevated mortality throughout their careers due to lifestyle factors.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('11111111-0001-0008-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Absolute Pitch and Tonal Languages',
   'Absolute pitch (perfect pitch) — the ability to identify or reproduce a musical note without a reference — is roughly six times more common among speakers of tonal languages like Mandarin, Cantonese, and Vietnamese.',
   'In tonal languages, pitch is phonemic: the word "ma" in Mandarin means mother, hemp, horse, or scold depending on tone. Speakers must process pitch analytically from infancy. Research by Diana Deutsch at UC San Diego showed this early analytical pitch processing predisposes tonal language speakers to absolute pitch. The critical period for absolute pitch acquisition appears to close around age 6–7. Studies of conservatory students in China vs. the US show the gap: roughly 60% of Chinese students have absolute pitch vs. 14% of Americans, even controlling for musical training intensity.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('11111111-0001-0009-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Modulation: The Art of the Key Change',
   'Modulation — moving from one musical key to another within a piece — is one of the most powerful tools for creating emotional lift, surprise, or drama. A well-executed key change feels like a door opening into a larger room.',
   'The most common modulation in pop music is the "truck driver''s gear change": simply shifting the entire song up a half or whole step for the final chorus. It creates instant emotional elevation through sheer novelty — the same melody suddenly feels higher, brighter, more urgent. Classical modulation is more sophisticated: composers navigate through the circle of fifths, using pivot chords (chords that function in both the old and new key) to make the transition seamless. Beethoven''s "Moonlight Sonata" uses abrupt modulation to jarring effect; Schubert''s modulations are so smooth they can pass unnoticed for bars.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('11111111-0001-0010-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'The Oldest Recording of a Human Voice',
   'The oldest playable recording of a human voice is not Edison''s 1877 phonograph but a phonautograph recording made in Paris on April 9, 1860 — seventeen years earlier — by Édouard-Léon Scott de Martinville.',
   'Scott''s phonautograph traced sound waves onto smoke-blackened paper as a visual record — intended for study, not playback. He never imagined playing it back. In 2008, audio historians at the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory used optical scanning and digital processing to play the recording for the first time: a 10-second fragment of the French folk song "Au Clair de la Lune." The voice was initially thought to be a woman''s but was later identified as Scott himself singing in falsetto. The recording predates Edison''s "Mary Had a Little Lamb" by 17 years and challenges popular history''s attribution of recorded sound.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('11111111-0001-0011-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Why Minor Keys Sound Sad',
   'Minor keys are widely perceived as sad or tense across cultures, but the association is not purely cultural — it has a basis in acoustics and the physics of harmonic series.',
   'The minor third interval that defines minor keys appears lower in the natural harmonic series than the major third. In the overtone series produced by virtually any vibrating object, the fifth and octave appear first, followed by the major third — the minor third appears much later. This means minor intervals are acoustically less "natural" or stable, creating a subtle tension the ear perceives as unresolved. Cross-cultural studies show the major/minor sadness distinction holds across cultures with limited Western music exposure, though the intensity of the association varies. Tempo interacts with mode: a fast minor-key piece can feel exciting, not sad.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('11111111-0001-0012-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Ostinato: The Repeating Figure',
   'An ostinato is a persistently repeated musical phrase or rhythm — from the Italian for "obstinate." It is the structural backbone of minimalism, electronic dance music, and much of the Western classical tradition.',
   'Pachelbel''s Canon uses a repeating eight-note bass ostinato over which the upper voices weave increasingly elaborate variations. Ravel''s "Bolero" is essentially one long crescendo built on a relentless rhythmic ostinato. In electronic music, the loop is an ostinato by definition — the entire architecture of techno, house, and drum and bass is built on layering and modifying ostinatos. Minimalist composers like Steve Reich and Philip Glass built entire aesthetic movements from the hypnotic power of repetition with subtle variation. Neuroscience suggests ostinatos activate the brain''s reward circuitry through a mechanism called "groove" — anticipation and micro-violation of expectation.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('11111111-0001-0013-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Music Boosts Athletic Performance by 10–15%',
   'Listening to music during exercise consistently improves performance: athletes work harder, feel less fatigue, and maintain effort longer. The effect size is roughly 10–15% in endurance tasks.',
   'Costas Karageorghis at Brunel University has spent three decades studying music and exercise, finding that music at tempos matching or slightly exceeding heart rate is most effective. The mechanism is partly attentional — music competes with fatigue signals for conscious awareness — and partly motivational, through rhythmic entrainment (the body''s tendency to synchronize movement to a beat). The effect is stronger at moderate intensities and diminishes near maximal effort, where physiological signals overwhelm the attentional buffer. World Athletics banned headphones in elite competition in 2007 on the grounds that music provides an unfair advantage.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('11111111-0001-0014-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'The A440 Standard Was Only Set in 1955',
   'The pitch A above middle C was only internationally standardized at 440 Hz by the ISO in 1955. Before that, concert pitch varied by era, country, and even individual orchestra — making historical performance a complex puzzle.',
   'In Baroque Europe, pitch varied by up to a minor third across regions — a full step lower than today''s standard in some cities. Handel''s tuning fork (preserved at the Foundling Hospital, London) is tuned to approximately 422.5 Hz. 19th-century orchestras crept upward in pitch as players sought a brighter, more brilliant sound, reaching 452 Hz in some Viennese orchestras. This "pitch inflation" meant singers had to transpose up over time. Verdi campaigned for a lower standard (432 Hz) to protect singers'' voices. The 1955 ISO standard of 440 Hz was a compromise and is still not universally observed — many European orchestras tune to 442 or 444 Hz.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('11111111-0001-0015-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Syncopation: Rhythm Against the Beat',
   'Syncopation places rhythmic accents on weak beats or between beats — creating tension against the underlying pulse. It is the defining rhythmic feature of jazz, funk, reggae, and much of African-derived music.',
   'In standard 4/4 time, beats 1 and 3 are strong; beats 2 and 4 are weak. Syncopation shifts emphasis to the offbeats or between them. Reggae''s defining feature is the "skank" — a guitar or keyboard chord landing precisely on the offbeat (the "and" of each beat). Funk''s dense syncopation creates "the pocket" — a groove that feels both delayed and urgent. James Brown''s "The One" is a concept built on syncopation: everything leads to the downbeat, but only by elaborately avoiding it. Research shows syncopation activates motor planning regions of the brain even when listeners remain still — the body wants to move.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('11111111-0001-0016-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'The Theremin: Music Without Touch',
   'The theremin is the only instrument played without physical contact. Two antennas sense the player''s hand positions — one controlling pitch, one volume — producing an eerie, singing tone used in early science fiction films.',
   'Leon Theremin invented the instrument in 1920 while researching proximity sensors for the Soviet government. Performers move one hand near a vertical antenna (closer = higher pitch) and the other near a horizontal loop antenna (closer = softer). The instrument is famously difficult to play in tune because there is no physical feedback — no frets, keys, or stops. Clara Rockmore, widely considered the greatest theremin virtuoso, reportedly practiced 8 hours daily for years. The instrument''s sound — produced by interference between two radio-frequency oscillators — is similar to the human voice in its continuous pitch and vibrato, giving it its haunting quality.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('11111111-0001-0017-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Call and Response: Musical Conversation',
   'Call and response is a musical structure where a phrase (call) is answered by a complementary phrase (response) — creating a dialogue between performers, sections of an orchestra, or performer and audience.',
   'The structure is found across virtually every musical culture: West African drumming traditions, antiphonal Gregorian chant (choir answering choir), the blues (vocalist and guitar trading phrases), gospel, jazz improvisation (soloist and rhythm section), and electronic music (drop and response). Ethnomusicologists believe call and response may be among humanity''s oldest musical forms, rooted in the social dynamics of coordinated group labor. Field hollers — work songs of enslaved African Americans — used it for practical coordination and coded communication. The form directly shaped rock and roll through its blues ancestry.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('11111111-0001-0018-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'How the Piano Works',
   'The piano is a hybrid instrument: a string instrument (steel strings under tension) triggered by a percussion mechanism (felt hammers) controlled by a keyboard. Its mechanism — the double escapement action — took 300 years to perfect.',
   'Bartolomeo Cristofori invented the first true piano around 1700, solving the key problem of the harpsichord: players couldn''t vary volume by touch. His "escapement" mechanism allows the hammer to rebound immediately after striking, so the string can vibrate freely. The double escapement (perfected by Sébastien Érard in 1821) allows rapid repetition of notes — impossible on earlier designs. A modern concert grand has approximately 12,000 individual parts. Each string is under roughly 90 kg of tension; the entire instrument''s frame bears about 20 tonnes of combined string tension, requiring a cast iron plate to hold it.',
   'fact', '2026-07-15'),
  ('11111111-0001-0019-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Binaural Beats and Brainwave Entrainment',
   'When slightly different frequencies are played in each ear, the brain perceives a third, phantom beat — the difference between the two. Proponents claim this entrains brainwaves; evidence for therapeutic use is mixed.',
   'If the left ear hears 200 Hz and the right ear hears 210 Hz, the brain perceives a 10 Hz "beat" — generated internally in the superior olivary nucleus, not in the environment. EEG studies confirm this influences brainwave activity; the brain does partially entrain to the binaural frequency. But the leap from entrainment to cognitive or therapeutic effects is where the evidence thins. A 2017 Cochrane review found insufficient evidence for anxiety reduction. Small studies suggest modest effects on focus and relaxation, but effect sizes are generally small and methodologies weak. The phenomenon is real; the marketing claims around it are mostly overblown.',
   'insight', '2026-07-16'),
  ('11111111-0001-0020-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'The Circle of Fifths',
   'The circle of fifths is a visual diagram arranging all 12 musical keys in a circle based on their relationships. Adjacent keys share six of seven notes, making them harmonically close; opposite keys share almost nothing.',
   'Moving clockwise around the circle, each key is a perfect fifth above the previous (C → G → D → A → E → B → F# → C# → Ab → Eb → Bb → F → C). Keys adjacent on the circle have one note difference between their scales, making modulation between them smooth and natural. The circle encodes the entire system of Western tonal harmony in one diagram: key signatures, chord relationships, common modulations, and the structure of the major/minor system all emerge from it. Composers use it intuitively; music theory teachers use it explicitly. Bach''s "Well-Tempered Clavier" systematically explored all 24 major and minor keys as the circle was being formalized in the early 18th century.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-17'),
  ('11111111-0001-0021-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'How Auto-Tune Changed Pop Music',
   'Auto-Tune was invented in 1997 as an invisible correction tool. T-Pain and Cher''s producers pushed the effect to maximum, creating a new aesthetic — a robotic vocal texture that became a defining sound of 21st-century pop.',
   'Andy Hildebrand, a geophysicist who used autocorrelation algorithms to analyze seismic data, repurposed the math for pitch detection and correction, founding Antares Audio Technologies. Cher''s "Believe" (1998) used hard pitch quantization deliberately to create the robotic effect. T-Pain popularized it as a signature aesthetic in the 2000s, and Kanye West''s "808s and Heartbreak" (2008) brought the sound to mainstream hip-hop. The technology created a cultural split: purists decried it as dishonest; proponents argued it was simply a new instrument. Today, some form of pitch correction appears on virtually every commercially released vocal recording.',
   'fact', '2026-07-18'),
  ('11111111-0001-0022-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Microtonal Music: Notes Between the Notes',
   'Western music divides the octave into 12 equal semitones — but this is a convention, not a law. Microtonal music uses intervals smaller than a semitone, accessing the vast harmonic territory between standard pitches.',
   'Arabic maqam music, Turkish makam, and Persian dastgah traditions all use quarter-tones (half a semitone) as structural intervals. Indian classical music uses 22 shrutis per octave. Harry Partch built custom instruments to play 43 tones per octave. The physics rationale is that many microtonal intervals occur naturally in the harmonic series but were "tempered out" (approximated away) in equal temperament for the practical advantage of allowing fixed-pitch instruments to play in any key. Modern electronic instruments and software make any tuning system trivially playable, prompting composers like La Monte Young and Ben Johnston to revisit just intonation and other historical systems.',
   'insight', '2026-07-19'),
  ('11111111-0001-0023-0001-000000000001', 'music', 'Tempo, Emotion, and the Autonomic Nervous System',
   'Music tempo directly affects heart rate, breathing, and anxiety levels — not through conscious interpretation but through the autonomic nervous system''s tendency to entrain to rhythmic external stimuli.',
   'Research by Lucanne Magill and others shows that slow music (below 60 BPM) lowers heart rate and promotes parasympathetic (rest-and-digest) activation. Fast music (above 120 BPM) increases arousal, heart rate, and stress hormone levels. The mechanism appears to be respiratory-cardiovascular coupling: breathing tends to synchronize to musical rhythm, and breathing rate directly influences heart rate through the vagus nerve. This is why slow music is used in pain management, pre-operative anxiety reduction, and intensive care units. The effect is measurable even in premature infants, suggesting it does not require learned cultural associations.',
   'fact', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- News ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('22222222-0002-0004-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'The First Daily Newspaper (1702)',
   'The Daily Courant, launched in London on March 11, 1702 by Elizabeth Mallet, is widely considered the first daily newspaper in the English language. It ran for 30 years and established norms still recognizable today.',
   'Mallet''s Daily Courant was a single sheet, printed on both sides, containing foreign news translated from Continental papers. She explicitly promised not to add editorial comment — a proto-objectivity norm. The paper''s daily publication required new infrastructure: reliable printing, distribution networks, and advertising support. Daily journalism as a concept was radical; previously, news was published as pamphlets, broadsides, or weekly gazettes. The Daily Courant''s model — advertisement-supported, daily, claiming factual neutrality — became the template for three centuries of commercial journalism.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('22222222-0002-0005-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'The Rise of Slow Journalism',
   'Slow journalism is a movement that rejects the speed imperative of the 24-hour news cycle in favor of deeply reported, carefully edited, long-form narratives published weeks or months after events.',
   'Publications like Delayed Gratification, De Correspondent, and Delayed Satisfaction built business models around quarterly publication or member-supported deep reporting. Delayed Gratification deliberately covers news from three months ago, asking: what actually mattered? Research from the Reuters Institute suggests readers increasingly value depth over speed, with long-form digital articles attracting more time-spent and higher completion rates than breaking news items. The movement draws on the tradition of the New Journalism (Tom Wolfe, Joan Didion) and investigative magazines (New Yorker, Harpers), arguing that accuracy, context, and narrative depth are incompatible with hourly deadlines.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('22222222-0002-0006-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'Horse-Race Journalism',
   'Horse-race journalism covers elections as competitions — who is ahead, who is behind, who made a gaffe — rather than as policy contests. Research consistently shows it dominates campaign coverage and shapes voter priorities accordingly.',
   'Studies of US presidential election coverage show that 60–70% of stories focus on strategy, polling, and competitive dynamics rather than policy substance. Thomas Patterson''s research at Harvard''s Shorenstein Center has documented this pattern across decades. The consequence is agenda-setting: voters who consume primarily horse-race coverage prioritize electability over positions when evaluating candidates. Structural incentives drive the pattern — competitive framing generates more reader engagement and can be produced faster than policy analysis. Critics argue it is self-fulfilling: coverage that ignores policy makes voters who ignore policy, confirming journalists'' assumptions about audience preferences.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('22222222-0002-0007-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'Churnalism: When Press Releases Become News',
   'Churnalism describes journalism that reproduces press releases, agency copy, or public relations material with minimal independent reporting — creating the appearance of original news where little exists.',
   'Nick Davies coined the term in "Flat Earth News" (2008) after a Cardiff University study found that 60% of UK broadsheet stories were substantially derived from press releases or wire copy. The economic pressures are direct: as newsroom staff shrank and publication speed increased, individual journalists were required to produce more stories with less time for verification and sourcing. PR agencies now employ more people than newsrooms in most Western countries. The result is a news ecosystem where organizations with PR budgets can reliably place favorable stories, while underfunded public interest beats go uncovered. Churnalism Check (a UK tool) allowed readers to compare articles to original press releases.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('22222222-0002-0008-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'The War of the Worlds Panic Was Largely Invented',
   'The 1938 Orson Welles radio broadcast of "War of the Worlds" is remembered as causing mass panic across America. In reality, the panic was vastly exaggerated — largely invented by newspapers hostile to radio as a competitor.',
   'Contemporary research by Jefferson Pooley and Michael Socolow found that the "nationwide panic" was primarily constructed by newspapers the following morning. Actual listenership for the broadcast was small (most Americans were tuned to a competing comedy program). Police received some concerned calls, and a small number of people briefly fled their homes. But newspapers — threatened by radio''s rise as an advertising competitor — amplified scattered incidents into a national hysteria narrative. The story about the panic became more influential than the original panic, establishing a durable myth about media power and mass suggestibility that shaped communication scholarship for decades.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('22222222-0002-0009-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'How Wire Services Work',
   'Wire services — AP, Reuters, AFP, Bloomberg — are wholesale news agencies that produce and sell content to thousands of subscribing outlets worldwide. A single AP dispatch may appear, slightly edited, in hundreds of papers simultaneously.',
   'The Associated Press was founded in 1846 as a cooperative: New York newspapers pooled resources to receive news via telegraph from the Mexican-American War front. Today AP has journalists in virtually every country; its content reaches half the world''s population daily. Reuters was founded in London in 1851, initially using carrier pigeons to bridge gaps in telegraph lines. Wire service copy is the skeleton of the global news system — providing baseline factual reporting that local outlets dress with regional context. When wire services make errors (as in mistaken early reports of deaths or events), they propagate instantly through thousands of outlets simultaneously, making corrections difficult.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('22222222-0002-0010-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'Manufacturing Consent: Media Filter Theory',
   'Noam Chomsky and Edward Herman''s 1988 "Manufacturing Consent" argues that mass media in capitalist societies serve elite interests through five structural filters — not through conspiracy but through institutional incentives.',
   'The five filters are: (1) concentrated media ownership, (2) advertising as primary revenue source, (3) reliance on official sources, (4) "flak" — organized criticism that disciplines non-conforming journalists, and (5) an ideological framework that defines acceptable debate. Herman and Chomsky contrasted coverage of comparable atrocities by US allies vs. enemies, finding systematic differences that could not be explained by newsworthiness alone. Critics argue the model is too deterministic and ignores adversarial journalism. Defenders point to the model''s predictive power across media systems worldwide. The book remains among the most cited and debated works in media studies.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('22222222-0002-0011-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'Framing Effects in News',
   'The same set of facts, presented with different frames — crime vs. public health, individual vs. structural, us vs. them — produces dramatically different reader responses, policy preferences, and attributions of blame.',
   'Iyengar and Kinder''s foundational research (1987) showed that TV news primed viewers to evaluate presidents based on whatever issues dominated recent coverage. Entman''s framing theory (1993) showed that frames define problems, diagnose causes, make moral judgments, and suggest remedies — all simultaneously, often invisibly. A crime framed as individual failure produces support for punishment; the same crime framed as systemic failure produces support for social programs. News organizations make framing choices constantly, often unconsciously, reflecting the assumptions of their reporters, sources, and audiences. Media literacy education increasingly focuses on identifying frames rather than just checking facts.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('22222222-0002-0012-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'The Watchdog Role and What Happens When Local News Dies',
   'Investigative journalism''s watchdog role — holding governments and corporations accountable — is most effective at the local level, where officials are most directly observable. The collapse of local news has measurable effects on corruption and spending.',
   'A 2018 study in the Journal of Financial Economics found that municipalities covered by fewer local journalists paid significantly higher borrowing costs — a market signal of reduced accountability and increased corruption risk. University of North Carolina''s "News Deserts" project documented 2,500 US newspapers closed between 2005 and 2020, with more than half of US counties having only one local news outlet. Research links news deserts to lower voter turnout, higher government spending, and more government debt. The collapse is structural: digital platforms captured the classified advertising revenue that had cross-subsidized journalism since the 19th century.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('22222222-0002-0013-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'The Inverted Pyramid Structure',
   'The inverted pyramid places the most important information first (who, what, when, where, why), followed by supporting details in decreasing order of importance. Editors can cut from the bottom without losing the story''s essential content.',
   'The structure emerged from telegraph journalism after the US Civil War, when correspondents could not guarantee transmission of long dispatches and prioritized getting the key facts through before the line cut out. It became the dominant structure for hard news globally. Critics argue it produces dull, front-loaded writing that kills narrative tension. Feature writers use the "Wall Street Journal formula" instead (anecdote → nut graph → development → conclusion). Digital analytics have complicated the model: scroll depth data shows many readers never reach the bottom of inverted pyramid stories, but engagement time correlates more with narrative structure than fact-front loading.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('22222222-0002-0014-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'Fact-Checking Organizations and Their Limits',
   'Independent fact-checking organizations like PolitiFact, FactCheck.org, and Snopes play an important role in the information ecosystem — but research on their effectiveness at changing beliefs is sobering.',
   'Studies show fact-checks are most effective with politically unaffiliated readers and when delivered promptly. However, the "backfire effect" — where corrections entrenched misbeliefs — once seemed powerful but has been substantially replicated away; most people do update beliefs when presented with accurate corrections. The bigger challenge is reach: fact-checks typically reach a small, already-skeptical audience rather than those who most need them. Google and Facebook''s integration of fact-check labels showed modest effects on reducing shares of flagged content. The structural problem: misinformation travels faster and more emotionally than corrections by design, and scale advantages heavily favor producers of false content.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('22222222-0002-0015-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'How Paywalls Changed Journalism Economics',
   'Digital paywalls — hard, metered, and freemium — shifted news from an advertising-dependent model toward reader-supported revenue, with profound effects on which outlets survive and what they cover.',
   'The New York Times introduced its metered paywall in 2011 and now earns more from subscriptions than advertising — a historic reversal. The Times has ~10 million digital subscribers; the Washington Post, Atlantic, and Financial Times have also successfully pivoted. But the model works only for news organizations with strong brand identity and broad national/international appeal. Local newspapers, which already struggled to charge for content readers had always received free, have largely failed to convert paywalls into sustainable revenue. The result is a bifurcated ecosystem: well-resourced national outlets serving paying subscribers, and diminished or absent local coverage for everyone else.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('22222222-0002-0016-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'The Objectivity Norm and Its Critics',
   'American journalism''s commitment to "objectivity" — presenting "both sides" without editorial judgment — is a 20th-century professional norm, not an eternal journalistic value. It is increasingly challenged as sometimes producing false balance.',
   'Before the 20th century, partisan journalism was the norm globally — newspapers were explicitly affiliated with political parties or viewpoints. The objectivity norm emerged in the US after World War I as journalists sought professional legitimacy by distancing themselves from propaganda. Critics today argue it creates "false balance" — treating settled scientific consensus (climate change, vaccines) as equivalent to fringe dissent, or giving space to bad-faith actors as a matter of neutrality. Jay Rosen''s "view from nowhere" critique argues objectivity without moral clarity abdicates journalism''s civic function. Defenders counter that disciplined neutrality is the only alternative to pure advocacy journalism.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('22222222-0002-0017-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'Embedded Journalism in War Coverage',
   'Embedded journalism — reporters living and traveling with military units — was systematically formalized by the US military in the 2003 Iraq War. It improved access but raised serious questions about independence and perspective.',
   'The Pentagon embedded roughly 700 journalists with military units during the 2003 invasion, granting unprecedented front-line access in exchange for signing ground rules limiting what could be reported. Studies of embedded coverage found it was more sympathetic to soldiers and military operations than non-embedded reporting — a "bonding effect" from shared risk and proximity. Critics noted that embed conditions excluded coverage of civilian casualties, strategic failures, and dissenting military voices. The military concluded that embedding was strategically effective at shaping coverage; journalism schools now teach the ethical tensions explicitly. The tradeoff between access and independence remains one of the most contested questions in war journalism.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('22222222-0002-0018-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'Tabloid Journalism and the Public Interest Defense',
   'Tabloid journalism — sensationalized, celebrity-focused, often invasive — routinely defends privacy intrusions with a "public interest" argument. The line between genuine accountability journalism and voyeuristic entertainment is genuinely contested.',
   'The UK phone-hacking scandal (revealed fully by 2011) showed News of the World journalists hacking the voicemails of murder victims, celebrities, and politicians — justifying it as investigative journalism. The Leveson Inquiry that followed distinguished between "public interest" (matters citizens need to know to participate in democracy) and "what the public is interested in" (entertainment and gossip). Genuine tabloid public interest journalism has broken major stories: The Sun''s Profumo Affair coverage contributed to a government collapse; tabloids first exposed several major corporate frauds. The genre''s methods and its genuine contributions are inseparable — the challenge is building frameworks that preserve the latter while constraining the former.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('22222222-0002-0019-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'The Pulitzer Prize: History and Significance',
   'The Pulitzer Prize, established in 1917 through Joseph Pulitzer''s bequest to Columbia University, is the most prestigious award in American journalism. It has recognized both genuine accountability journalism and, occasionally, contested work.',
   'Pulitzer, the Hungarian-born publisher of the New York World, left $2 million to Columbia University to establish journalism prizes and a journalism school. The prizes cover 21 categories: 15 in journalism, 6 in arts and letters. Notable Pulitzers include the Pentagon Papers (1972), Watergate reporting (1973), and Abu Ghraib photography (2004). But the prize has also had controversies: Walter Duranty won in 1932 for Moscow reporting that suppressed evidence of the Ukrainian famine; a 1981 prize was revoked when the winning story was revealed as fabricated. The prize''s gatekeeping role — defining what counts as excellent journalism — gives it outsized influence on journalism''s professional norms.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('22222222-0002-0020-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'Press Freedom Index: A Wide Global Variation',
   'Reporters Without Borders'' annual Press Freedom Index reveals dramatic global variation — from Nordic countries where journalism operates freely, to states where journalists face imprisonment and assassination.',
   'The 2024 index ranked Norway, Denmark, and Sweden at the top; North Korea, Eritrea, and Iran at the bottom. The US ranked 55th — below most Western European nations — due to journalist arrests during protests, shield law weaknesses, and ownership consolidation. The Committee to Protect Journalists documented 320 journalists imprisoned globally in 2023, with China, Myanmar, and Belarus as the top jailers. Countries with strong press freedom correlate with lower corruption (Transparency International data shows the relationship is bidirectional: press freedom enables accountability, and accountable governance protects press freedom). The index uses 5 criteria: political context, legal framework, economic context, sociocultural context, and safety.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('22222222-0002-0021-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'Algorithmic News Feeds and Filter Bubbles',
   'Social media algorithms optimize for engagement, systematically surfacing content that generates strong emotional reactions — which tends to be partisan, outrage-inducing, and confirmatory of existing beliefs.',
   'Eli Pariser coined "filter bubble" in 2011 to describe personalization algorithms that shield users from challenging information. Facebook''s own internal research (leaked in 2021) showed the algorithm amplified divisive political content because it generated more engagement. However, subsequent academic research has complicated the picture: most people''s online news diets are less ideologically siloed than feared, and exposure to cross-cutting content does not reliably reduce polarization. The strongest polarization effects appear to be driven by partisan media choices, not algorithmic curation alone. The debate remains active: the question is not whether filter bubbles exist but how large their independent effect is relative to pre-existing political sorting.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('22222222-0002-0022-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'The Death of the Classified Ad',
   'Classified advertising — the revenue backbone of 20th-century local newspapers — was destroyed within a decade by Craigslist, which offered the same service free. The loss eliminated the economic foundation of local journalism.',
   'In 2000, US newspapers earned roughly $19.6 billion in classified ad revenue. By 2012, it had collapsed to $4.6 billion — an unprecedented destruction of revenue in a single industry vertical within a decade. Craig Newmark launched Craigslist in San Francisco in 1995 as a free community notice board; it never needed to monetize because it had no shareholders demanding returns. Newspapers had used classified profits to cross-subsidize expensive investigative journalism, foreign bureaus, and local coverage. When classified vanished, these subsidies ended. The structural lesson is stark: digital platforms that give away for free what newspapers charged for don''t just compete — they eliminate the entire funding model.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('22222222-0002-0023-0002-000000000002', 'news', 'Solutions Journalism vs. Problem Journalism',
   'Solutions journalism reports on evidence-based responses to social problems — not to advocate, but to show what has worked, why, and under what conditions. It complements but does not replace accountability reporting.',
   'The Solutions Journalism Network distinguishes solutions journalism from "good news" or advocacy: it must include critical analysis of limitations and failures, not just success. Research by the SJN and Johns Hopkins found that solutions-framed stories increase readers'' sense of agency and civic engagement without reducing critical awareness of problems. The model addresses a documented weakness of traditional journalism: problem-focused coverage can create fatalism, particularly on complex issues like climate change, poverty, or mental health. Critics argue solutions journalism risks being captured by organizations it covers positively; proponents counter that the same methodological rigor applied to problems can be applied to responses.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- History ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('33333333-0003-0004-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'Alexander Selkirk: The Real Robinson Crusoe',
   'Alexander Selkirk was a Scottish sailor who spent four years and four months alone on an uninhabited island in the South Pacific after demanding to be put ashore in 1704. His story directly inspired Daniel Defoe''s 1719 novel.',
   'Selkirk had a dispute with his captain over the seaworthiness of their privateer vessel and chose to be marooned rather than continue. He was left on Más a Tierra (now Isla Robinson Crusoe) in the Juan Fernández Archipelago, 670 km off Chile. He survived by hunting feral goats, building shelters, and reading a Bible to maintain his literacy. A British privateer, Woodes Rogers, rescued him in February 1709. Rogers''s account of the encounter, published in 1712, fascinated London society and reached Defoe, who transformed the tale into a foundational novel of individualism and self-reliance. Selkirk''s story captures the Enlightenment''s fascination with the "natural man."',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('33333333-0003-0005-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'The Black Death Raised Wages',
   'The Black Death (1347–1353) killed 30–60% of Europe''s population. By eliminating surplus labor, it paradoxically raised wages, strengthened peasant bargaining power, and accelerated the end of serfdom — reshaping European society.',
   'Before the plague, Europe''s landowners held near-total power over an oversupplied peasant labor force. After, surviving peasants could demand wages, mobility, and better conditions — or simply leave for better opportunities. The English Statute of Laborers (1351) tried to freeze wages at pre-plague levels; it largely failed. The resulting tensions contributed to the 1381 Peasants'' Revolt. Historians like Walter Scheidel ("The Great Leveler") identify mass death as one of the only forces historically capable of compressing extreme inequality — a disturbing observation that highlights how entrenched elite advantage tends to be under normal conditions.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('33333333-0003-0006-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'Terra Nullius: The Legal Fiction of Empty Land',
   'Terra nullius — "nobody''s land" in Latin — was the legal doctrine used to justify colonial seizure of inhabited territories by declaring them legally unoccupied. Australia was declared terra nullius until 1992.',
   'The doctrine held that land not "improved" through agriculture or fixed settlement had no legal owner — excluding Indigenous peoples'' claims based on hunting, nomadism, or non-European land use. Australia was declared terra nullius by British settlers in 1788 despite an Aboriginal population of at least 300,000. The High Court of Australia overturned terra nullius in Mabo v Queensland (1992), recognizing native title for the first time. In North America, the Doctrine of Discovery (a related concept rooted in 15th-century papal bulls) continues to influence property law. Legal scholars note that these doctrines were not neutral descriptions but active constructs designed to enable dispossession.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('33333333-0003-0007-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'The Great Stink of 1858',
   'In the summer of 1858, London''s Thames River — which served as both water supply and open sewer for two million people — produced an unbearable stench that forced Parliament to act on sanitation infrastructure.',
   'London in the 1850s had no centralized sewage system. Human waste flowed through open channels into the Thames, which also supplied drinking water. The hot summer of 1858 accelerated bacterial decomposition, producing hydrogen sulfide at concentrations that drove Members of Parliament from their chambers despite soaking the curtains in chloride of lime. Within 18 days of the crisis, Parliament passed the Metropolitan Board of Works Act, funding engineer Joseph Bazalgette''s revolutionary sewer system — 1,100 miles of street sewers connecting to 82 miles of main sewers — completed in 1875. The project reduced cholera deaths by 90% and became a model for urban infrastructure worldwide.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('33333333-0003-0008-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'Why Rome Fell: Systems Failure, Not Barbarians',
   'The traditional narrative blames Germanic barbarian invasions for Rome''s fall. Modern historians increasingly see it as a cascading systems failure: fiscal breakdown, institutional decay, climate disruption, and pandemic preceding military collapse.',
   'Edward Gibbon''s 18th-century account blamed Christianity and barbarians. Bryan Ward-Perkins''s "The Fall of Rome and the End of Civilization" (2005) emphasizes the catastrophic material consequences: archaeological evidence shows dramatic declines in pottery quality, building construction, and literacy after 400 CE — suggesting genuine civilizational regression, not just political transition. Peter Heather''s research shows the Hunnic migrations pushed Germanic peoples west, but Roman institutional and fiscal weakness had already become systemic before the invasions intensified. The Justinianic Plague (541–549 CE) may have been the final blow, killing 25–50 million people across the Mediterranean world.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('33333333-0003-0009-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'Presentism: Judging History by Modern Standards',
   'Presentism is the tendency to interpret historical events and figures through contemporary moral frameworks — praising or condemning people of the past for failing to meet standards that did not yet exist.',
   'Historians distinguish presentism from legitimate moral evaluation: the question is not whether past actors can be judged morally (they can) but whether our judgment is informed by understanding the conceptual and institutional constraints of their era. Jefferson owned enslaved people, but abolition movements existed in his lifetime and he knew the arguments. Aristotle defended natural slavery, in a society where virtually no philosophical tradition questioned it. The presentism debate matters practically: monument removal, curriculum choices, and institutional renaming all involve judgments about when past wrongs are sufficiently contextual vs. sufficiently universal to warrant symbolic condemnation. Historians generally advocate for context without excuse.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('33333333-0003-0010-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'Tulip Mania Was Exaggerated',
   'The Dutch tulip mania of 1636–37 — supposedly the first speculative bubble, with bulbs selling for the price of a house — has been dramatically overstated by later historians. The actual economic disruption was modest.',
   'Anne Goldgar''s "Tulipmania" (2007) painstakingly traced actual contemporary records and found that the number of people involved was small, the peak prices were for extraordinary rare bulbs rather than common ones, and there is no contemporary evidence of the widespread economic ruin described by later morality tales. The mania was real but localized to a small network of traders. The story was systematically exaggerated by 17th-century moralists using it as a cautionary tale against speculation, and then recycled uncritically by subsequent economists as a convenient founding example of irrational market behavior. The myth is more instructive than the reality — revealing how we construct cautionary economic narratives.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('33333333-0003-0011-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'The Printing Press and the First Information Overload',
   'Gutenberg''s press (c.1440) did not simply spread truth — it created the first information overload and a crisis of authority, flooding Europe with conflicting claims, enabling the Reformation, and producing the first pamphlet wars.',
   'Within 50 years of Gutenberg, over 8 million books had been printed — more than European scribes had produced in the previous millennium. The Catholic Church, which had controlled knowledge production through monasteries, lost its monopoly. Martin Luther''s 95 Theses (1517) spread across Germany within weeks — an early viral moment. But the press also spread astrological almanacs, miracle stories, and anti-Semitic libels with equal efficiency. Elizabeth Eisenstein''s "The Printing Press as an Agent of Change" (1980) argues the press didn''t just transmit the Renaissance — it fundamentally restructured how knowledge was organized, standardized, and contested. The parallels with the internet are striking and deliberate in contemporary scholarship.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('33333333-0003-0012-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'Counterfactual History',
   'Counterfactual history — "what if" questions about historical turning points — is a legitimate historical method when used rigorously. It reveals which events were contingent (could have gone otherwise) vs. structurally determined.',
   'Niall Ferguson''s "Virtual History" (1997) defended counterfactual reasoning as a way to test causal claims: if we say X caused Y, we implicitly claim that without X, Y would not have occurred. Counterfactual thinking makes this implicit claim explicit. What if Archduke Franz Ferdinand had not been assassinated? World War I might have been delayed — but would structural tensions (alliance systems, imperial rivalries, arms races) have eventually produced a comparable war? The methodology distinguishes between proximate causes (the assassination) and structural causes (the international system). Counterfactuals poorly constrained by historical evidence produce fiction; well-constrained counterfactuals produce insight into historical causation.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('33333333-0003-0013-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'The Columbian Exchange',
   'The Columbian Exchange — the transfer of plants, animals, diseases, and people between the Americas, Europe, Africa, and Asia after 1492 — was the most consequential biological event in human history since the Ice Age.',
   'The Americas contributed tomatoes, potatoes, maize, chocolate, vanilla, tobacco, and chili peppers to the Old World — transforming global cuisines and agricultural systems. European caloric yields increased substantially after potato adoption; Irish dependence on the potato led to catastrophic famine when blight struck in 1845. The exchange''s dark side was epidemiological: smallpox, measles, and influenza killed an estimated 50–90% of Indigenous American populations within a century of contact — the largest demographic collapse in recorded history. Alfred Crosby''s "The Columbian Exchange" (1972) and "Ecological Imperialism" (1986) established this biological lens as essential for understanding colonial history.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('33333333-0003-0014-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'The Congress of Vienna Invented Modern Diplomacy',
   'The Congress of Vienna (1814–15), which reorganized Europe after Napoleon''s defeat, established the modern framework of great-power diplomacy: multilateral conferences, balance of power, and the distinction between great powers and secondary states.',
   'Metternich, Castlereagh, Talleyrand, and Alexander I negotiated for nine months in Vienna, establishing a concert of great powers (Austria, Prussia, Russia, Britain, and eventually France) that managed European affairs through consultation rather than perpetual war. The resulting order was remarkably stable: no general European war occurred for 99 years (until 1914). Henry Kissinger''s doctoral dissertation, later published as "A World Restored" (1954), argued the Congress established a legitimate international order through its willingness to include even the defeated power (France) rather than punish it into resentment — a lesson he claimed was ignored at Versailles in 1919.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('33333333-0003-0015-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'Assassination as Political Tool',
   'Political assassination has shaped history at critical junctures — Julius Caesar, Abraham Lincoln, Archduke Franz Ferdinand, Mahatma Gandhi — but its strategic effectiveness is limited: it rarely achieves its political objectives.',
   'Franklin Ford''s "Political Murder" (1985) documented how assassination rarely produces the intended political change. Caesar''s assassins sought to restore the Republic; they triggered civil war and permanent autocracy. Franz Ferdinand''s murder led to a world war that destroyed the empires his assassins sought to liberate from. The targeting of leaders assumes political outcomes depend on individuals rather than structures and movements — a poor model for complex political systems. Successful cases exist: Heydrich''s killing disrupted Nazi administration in Prague; Yamamoto''s death weakened Japanese naval coordination. But these are exceptions to a general pattern of assassination producing blowback rather than strategic gain.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('33333333-0003-0016-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'Why the Roman Calendar Was Chaos Before Julius Caesar',
   'The pre-Julian Roman calendar was a political instrument deliberately manipulated by officials to extend their terms or disadvantage opponents — so chaotic that by 46 BCE the calendar was 80 days out of sync with the solar year.',
   'Caesar''s solution required first correcting the accumulated drift: 46 BCE was decreed a "year of confusion" of 445 days — the longest year in Roman history — to realign the calendar with the seasons. He then consulted the Alexandrian astronomer Sosigenes and introduced a 365.25-day solar year, adding a leap day every four years. The Julian calendar was used throughout Europe for 1,600 years. Its only flaw was slight over-correction: the true solar year is 11 minutes shorter than 365.25 days, which accumulated to a 10-day discrepancy by the 16th century. Pope Gregory XIII commissioned the Gregorian reform in 1582 — dropping 10 days and adjusting the leap year rule — and the new calendar was adopted at different times across Europe, with Russia not switching until 1918.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('33333333-0003-0017-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'The History of Public Debt',
   'Government borrowing is as old as organized states. The Mesopotamian city-states issued temple debt; medieval Italian city-states invented the bond market; the British national debt funded an empire. Debt is not a modern aberration.',
   'The Republic of Genoa created the Casa delle Compere in 1407 — one of the first formal government bond markets — to finance wars. The Bank of England was founded in 1694 explicitly to fund King William III''s wars through perpetual bonds called consols. Britain''s enormous national debt, often cited as a potential source of weakness, actually became a strategic strength: reliable debt service created trust, allowing Britain to borrow cheaply and sustain military campaigns that cash-strapped rivals could not. Historians Carmen Reinhart and Kenneth Rogoff''s "This Time Is Different" (2009) documented 800 years of sovereign debt crises, showing recurring patterns of overleveraging, denial, and crisis that contemporary policymakers consistently believe won''t apply to them.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('33333333-0003-0018-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'The Guillotine Was Invented to Be Humane',
   'The guillotine was proposed by Dr. Joseph-Ignace Guillotin in 1789 as an egalitarian, humane alternative to the class-based executions of the ancien régime — where nobles received quick beheading and commoners faced slower deaths.',
   'Guillotin himself opposed the death penalty and promoted the device precisely because he believed it would end suffering and treat all condemned persons equally regardless of class. The device was actually designed by Antoine Louis and built by German harpsichord maker Tobias Schmidt. It became operational in 1792 and was used until the last French execution in 1977. The question of whether consciousness persists after guillotine decapitation was debated seriously in the 19th century — experiments with severed heads suggested possible brief awareness for seconds. The device''s association with the Terror (1793–94) permanently attached Guillotin''s name to mass execution, ironically inverting his humane intention.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('33333333-0003-0019-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'Why the 1918 Flu Was Called Spanish',
   'The 1918 influenza pandemic killed 50–100 million people worldwide — far more than World War I. It was called "Spanish flu" not because it originated in Spain but because Spain, neutral in the war, had no wartime censorship and reported the outbreak freely.',
   'The pandemic likely originated in Kansas or possibly China; the earliest documented cases were at Camp Funston, Kansas, in March 1918. But the warring powers — Britain, France, Germany, the US — suppressed news of mass illness to maintain morale, creating the impression that only Spain was affected. King Alfonso XIII''s highly publicized illness gave the press a named victim. The "Spanish flu" label stuck despite being geographically inaccurate. The pandemic killed more Americans than all US wars of the 20th century combined, yet was largely absent from cultural memory for decades — partly due to wartime censorship shaping what was permissible to discuss.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('33333333-0003-0020-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'Cartography as Political Power',
   'Maps have always been instruments of power — defining territories, asserting sovereignty, erasing alternatives. Who draws the map, and how, encodes political claims as apparent objective facts.',
   'J.B. Harley''s "Deconstructing the Map" (1989) argued that maps are never neutral: they reflect the power relations of their producers. The Mercator projection (designed for European navigators) centers Europe and makes colonial territories appear small relative to imperial ones. British Ordnance Survey maps of Ireland in the 19th century anglicized Gaelic place names, effectively erasing a layer of cultural history. China''s "nine-dash line" map asserts South China Sea claims through cartographic assertion that international courts have rejected. Colonial maps drew borders without consulting local populations; post-colonial states inherited these borders and the conflicts embedded in them.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('33333333-0003-0021-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'How Napoleon Changed European Law',
   'The Napoleonic Code (1804) remains one of the most influential legal documents in history — replacing feudal patchwork with unified, rational civil law. It still forms the basis of legal systems in France, Quebec, Louisiana, and much of Latin America.',
   'Napoleon unified the revolutionary legal principles — equality before the law, property rights, secular authority, abolition of feudal privilege — into a coherent civil code of 2,281 articles. He oversaw the drafting personally, participating in 55 of 84 drafting sessions. The code replaced dozens of incompatible regional law systems in France and was exported through conquest across Europe and colonial territories. Its influence on Germany''s BGB (1900), the Swiss Civil Code (1907), and civil law systems across Africa, Asia, and the Americas makes it arguably the most exported legal document in history. Common law (Britain, US, Australia) is the main alternative tradition that resisted Napoleonic influence.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('33333333-0003-0022-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'The Postal System as Infrastructure of Empire',
   'Every major empire built a postal system — the Persian Chapar, the Roman cursus publicus, the Mongol Yam — not for civilian convenience but to transmit military orders, tax records, and intelligence across vast distances.',
   'The Persian royal road system (c. 550 BCE) used relay stations every 14 miles, allowing messages to travel 1,700 miles in 7 days — faster than any army could march. The Roman cursus publicus was restricted to government use; civilians had to organize their own letter-carrying. The Mongol Yam network at its peak had 50,000 horses at 1,400 stations across Eurasia — enabling Kublai Khan to govern from Beijing to Persia. The Pony Express (1860) lasted only 19 months before the transcontinental telegraph made it obsolete. These systems reveal a consistent pattern: communication infrastructure and political control are inseparable, and losing communication speed is often the first symptom of imperial decline.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('33333333-0003-0023-0003-000000000003', 'history', 'Vikings Did Not Wear Horned Helmets',
   'The horned Viking helmet is almost entirely a 19th-century Romantic invention. Actual Viking helmets were simple iron or leather caps, sometimes with a nose guard. The iconic image comes from opera costumes and nationalist iconography.',
   'Only one complete Viking Age helmet survives from Norway: the Gjermundbu helmet, found in 1943. It is a rounded iron cap with a spectacle-like eye and nose guard — no horns. Horned ceremonial helmets did exist in Scandinavia but date to the Bronze Age (1000–500 BCE), over a thousand years before the Viking Age. The horned-helmet image was popularized by Swedish artist Gustav Malmström''s illustrations (1820s), then cemented by costume designer Carl Emil Doepler''s designs for Wagner''s "Ring Cycle" (1876). The image spread through advertising and popular culture as a shorthand for "Viking." Archaeological Norse military equipment was practical, not theatrical.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Sports ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('44444444-0004-0004-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Peak Athletic Performance Varies by Sport',
   'Sprinters peak in their mid-20s; marathon runners peak in their late 20s to early 30s; chess grandmasters peak in their late 30s; golfers can compete at the highest level into their 40s. Peak performance is not a single thing.',
   'The difference reflects what each sport demands. Explosive sports requiring fast-twitch muscle fiber recruitment and reaction speed peak early — sprinters'' muscle fiber composition and nervous system speed begin declining in the late 20s. Endurance sports reward cardiovascular efficiency, which builds slowly and declines later. Sports requiring technical skill accumulation (golf, snooker, tennis) allow longer competitive windows because experience partially compensates for physical decline. Research by Jeff Stibel documented peak performance ages across 100 activities, finding strategic and intellectual performance peaks significantly later than physical ones — with chess peaking around 35–40.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('44444444-0004-0005-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'The Winner''s Curse in Sports Contracts',
   'In competitive bidding for athletes, the team that wins the bidding war often overpays — because it was the most optimistic about the player''s value. This "winner''s curse" is well-documented in sports economics.',
   'The winner''s curse is a general auction theory result: in a competitive bid for an uncertain asset, the winner tends to be the most optimistic bidder, meaning they typically overestimate the asset''s value. In sports, teams bidding for free agents with uncertain injury risk, age-related decline, and fit compatibility face maximum uncertainty. Studies of NFL, NBA, and MLB contracts consistently show that large, multi-year free agent contracts signed by the bidding-war winner underperform their cost. Teams that win bidding wars for marquee free agents are often disappointed; teams that lose narrow bidding wars often get the better outcome. Moneyball''s insight was partly about avoiding the winner''s curse by targeting undervalued players.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('44444444-0004-0006-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Regression to the Mean in Sports',
   'A player who dramatically outperforms their career average will likely perform closer to average next year — not because they "regressed" in ability but because extreme performance always contains a luck component that doesn''t persist.',
   'Regression to the mean is one of the most misunderstood concepts in sports. When a quarterback throws for 45 touchdowns in a season after averaging 30, the increase contains genuine skill improvement and random variance. The random component will likely not repeat at the same extreme level the following season. Sports journalists attribute this to "a sophomore slump," "the injury," or "the pressure" — when it is largely statistical inevitability. The phenomenon matters practically: teams that trade significant assets for a player coming off a career year often overpay for variance that will regress. Daniel Kahneman documented his own discovery of this principle while teaching Israeli Air Force instructors why praising good performance "seemed to cause" subsequent worse performance.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('44444444-0004-0007-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Home Advantage: Real but Smaller Than We Think',
   'Home advantage is a documented phenomenon across virtually every team sport studied — home teams win roughly 55–60% of games. But the effect is smaller than most fans believe and has been declining over decades.',
   'The home advantage effect was first systematically documented by Courneya and Carron in 1992, who analyzed thousands of games across multiple sports. Proposed mechanisms include: crowd noise disrupting officials'' decisions, familiarity with the venue and travel fatigue for visitors, and home crowd energy affecting home player performance. The COVID-19 pandemic provided a natural experiment: when games were played in empty stadiums, home advantage dropped significantly in football, basketball, and soccer, suggesting referee bias (driven by crowd pressure) is a major component. The advantage has declined as venues standardize, travel improves, and officiating becomes more scrutinized.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('44444444-0004-0008-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Why Athletes Choke Under Pressure',
   'Choking under pressure — performing worse than expected in high-stakes moments — is explained by the explicit monitoring theory: conscious attention disrupts automated motor skills that perform best without interference.',
   'The theory, developed by Sian Beilock, proposes that expert motor skills become proceduralized — stored as automatic sequences that the conscious mind should not interfere with. Under pressure, athletes direct conscious attention to movements that normally run on autopilot, disrupting their fluency. A basketball player who normally shoots free throws without thinking consciously monitors their elbow angle and release point under pressure — and performs worse. The paradox: the more expert you are, the more damaging conscious attention to technique becomes. Countermeasures include pre-performance routines (which occupy conscious attention with a ritual rather than the skill), and training under simulated pressure to build automaticity.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('44444444-0004-0009-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Expected Goals (xG) in Football',
   'Expected Goals (xG) measures the probability that a shot results in a goal based on historical data about shot location, type, and context. It reveals which teams create high-quality chances — independent of whether they actually scored.',
   'A shot from the penalty spot has an xG of roughly 0.79 — it becomes a goal 79% of the time. A shot from 30 yards out has an xG of perhaps 0.03. Teams can over- or underperform their xG in the short term through goalkeeper quality and finishing luck, but xG is a stronger predictor of future performance than actual goals scored. The metric was developed independently by several analysts around 2012 and is now standard in professional football analytics. Teams like Liverpool under Jürgen Klopp were early adopters; their performance over multiple seasons tracked xG closely. xG has moved from cult analytics tool to a standard metric shown on broadcast graphics within a decade.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('44444444-0004-0010-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'The Modern Olympics Were Invented, Not Revived',
   'Pierre de Coubertin invented the modern Olympics in 1896 inspired by ancient Greek ideals he largely romanticized. The ancient Olympics were very different: professional, brutal, openly commercial, and exclusively Greek.',
   'The ancient Olympic games (776 BCE – 393 CE) were a religious festival for Zeus featuring only Greek citizens, including nudity, life-threatening events, and no concept of amateurism — victors received substantial material rewards. De Coubertin, a French aristocrat obsessed with physical education as a national regeneration tool after France''s defeat in the Franco-Prussian War, proposed "reviving" an idealized version. His concept of amateurism — participation for love of sport, not pay — was itself a class artifact, excluding workers who couldn''t afford to train without compensation. The International Olympic Committee he founded in 1894 reflected European aristocratic values and excluded women from most events until the mid-20th century.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('44444444-0004-0011-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Female Athletes Are Under-Researched',
   'Sports science research has historically been conducted overwhelmingly on male athletes and generalized to women — despite significant physiological differences. This research gap affects training, injury prevention, and performance optimization for half the athletic population.',
   'A 2021 British Journal of Sports Medicine study found that in sports medicine research, fewer than 7% of participants in exercise science studies are female. Injury research is particularly skewed: ACL tears occur 2–8 times more frequently in female than male athletes, yet most ACL prevention research was conducted on men. The menstrual cycle significantly affects injury risk, training adaptation, and performance — but most coaches and training systems don''t account for it. Organizations like the IOC and FIFA have begun mandating sex-disaggregated data in research. The growing field of female-specific sports science is revealing that many "universal" training principles were simply male principles.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('44444444-0004-0012-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Periodization: Structured Elite Training',
   'Periodization is the systematic organization of training into phases — building base fitness, developing sport-specific qualities, peaking for competition, then recovering — to optimize performance at the right moment.',
   'The concept was developed by Soviet sports scientists in the 1950s, particularly Tudor Bompa, and was a major factor in Soviet Olympic dominance during the Cold War. A classic periodization model has macrocycles (full training years), mesocycles (4–6 week blocks), and microcycles (weekly training structure). Training variables — volume, intensity, specificity — are manipulated systematically rather than randomly. Modern "block periodization" concentrates one training quality at a time rather than developing several simultaneously. Elite endurance athletes use polarized training: roughly 80% of training at low intensity, 20% at very high intensity, avoiding the "moderate" zone that creates fatigue without optimal adaptation.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('44444444-0004-0013-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'The Relative Age Effect in Elite Sports',
   'In age-grouped youth sports, children born just after the eligibility cutoff are significantly more likely to reach elite levels — simply because they''re older, more physically mature, and selected more frequently than younger peers.',
   'Malcolm Gladwell popularized this in "Outliers" using Canadian hockey data. Children born in January (just after the January cutoff) are nearly a year older than December-born peers at age 8, creating large physical differences that coaches misinterpret as talent. Selected players receive better coaching, more practice, and higher competition quality — creating genuine performance advantages that compound over years. Research shows 40–50% of elite youth athletes in many sports are born in the first quarter of the eligibility year. Solutions include rotating cutoff dates, age-ordered grouping (matching children by biological maturity), and quota systems for each birth quarter.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('44444444-0004-0014-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Doping and the Tour de France',
   'The Tour de France has been so thoroughly intertwined with performance-enhancing drugs since its founding in 1903 that historians debate whether there has ever been a "clean" era — only different eras of different drugs.',
   'Early riders used strychnine (a stimulant at low doses), cocaine, and amphetamines openly — the Tour was considered a test of human endurance at the edge of death. EPO (erythropoietin, which increases red blood cell production) transformed the sport in the 1990s: estimates suggest virtually every top-10 finisher in the 1995–2005 era was doping. Lance Armstrong''s stripping of 7 Tour titles in 2012 was the most public reckoning, but investigations showed systematic team-level doping across multiple organizations. The biological passport, introduced in 2008, tracks athlete blood values longitudinally to detect manipulation without catching a specific substance — marking a methodological shift in anti-doping strategy.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('44444444-0004-0015-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'The Science of Slipstreaming',
   'Slipstreaming (drafting) allows a following athlete or vehicle to travel faster using significantly less energy by exploiting the low-pressure wake behind a leader. In cycling, following in a slipstream reduces energy cost by up to 40%.',
   'When a cyclist or vehicle moves through air, it creates a zone of disturbed, lower-pressure air behind it. A following rider in this zone faces less aerodynamic drag. In professional cycling, this creates the tactical logic of the peloton: riders take turns at the front (high energy cost) while others draft behind (low energy cost). The effect is largest in cycling but significant in running (6–8% energy savings), swimming, and NASCAR (where entire race strategies are built around drafting). Eliud Kipchoge''s sub-2-hour marathon in 2019 used a rotating formation of pacers specifically calculated to maximize his drafting advantage.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('44444444-0004-0016-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Why Marathon Runners Hit the Wall',
   'Hitting "the wall" around mile 20 of a marathon occurs when glycogen stores deplete and the body is forced to rely primarily on fat oxidation — a significantly slower energy system unable to maintain race pace.',
   'The human body can store approximately 1,800–2,000 calories of glycogen in muscles and liver — enough for roughly 20 miles at marathon pace. Fat stores are effectively unlimited but are metabolized more slowly. The transition from glycogen to fat-dominated metabolism produces a dramatic subjective sense of fatigue, pace drop, and cognitive impairment. Elite marathoners train to run at paces where fat metabolism contributes more energy ("fat adaptation" training) and consume carbohydrates aggressively during races to delay depletion. Carbohydrate gels consumed every 20–30 minutes can push the wall back by 5–8 miles. Slow marathon training (80% at low intensity) increases the density of fat-oxidizing mitochondria in muscle cells.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('44444444-0004-0017-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Free Throw Shooting and Pressure',
   'Free throw accuracy in NBA games is approximately 75% — surprisingly low for an uncontested, standardized shot from a fixed spot. NBA players typically shoot 85–90% in practice, meaning the 10–15 percentage point drop in games is not explained by physical difficulty alone.',
   'The explanation is explicit monitoring theory: the conscious attention triggered by high stakes disrupts the automated motor program that normally runs the shot. Pre-shot routines — the bouncing, spinning, and deep breathing that look like superstition — are cognitively functional: they give the conscious mind something to do so it stops interfering with the motor sequence. Players with more consistent routines show smaller accuracy drops under late-game pressure. The strategic consequence has been significant: the "hack-a-Shaq" foul-on-purpose tactic (targeting poor free throw shooters like Shaquille O''Neal, who shot 52% from the line) transformed NBA late-game strategy around a single psychological vulnerability, forcing rule changes to limit its use.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('44444444-0004-0018-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'How Altitude Training Works',
   'Training at high altitude (2,000–3,000 meters) where oxygen is scarce triggers physiological adaptations that improve sea-level endurance performance — primarily by increasing red blood cell mass and hemoglobin concentration.',
   'At altitude, the body responds to hypoxia (low oxygen) by secreting erythropoietin (EPO), which stimulates bone marrow to produce more red blood cells. More red blood cells mean more oxygen delivery to working muscles at sea level — the same adaptation that EPO doping provides artificially. The "Live High, Train Low" protocol (developed by Ben Levine and Jim Stray-Gundersen in the 1990s) maximizes the adaptation: athletes sleep at altitude to stimulate EPO production but train at sea level to maintain high training intensity. Altitude tents replicate hypobaric conditions during sleep without full-time relocation. World Athletics permits altitude training and EPO tents — the line between "natural" and "artificial" advantage is blurry.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('44444444-0004-0019-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'The Forward Pass Changed American Football',
   'The forward pass, now central to American football, was illegal until 1906 and introduced as a safety reform. President Theodore Roosevelt threatened to ban the sport entirely unless injuries were reduced — the forward pass was the solution.',
   'In the early 1900s, American football was a mass-formation running game that caused dozens of deaths and hundreds of serious injuries per season. The flying wedge formation was particularly lethal. In 1905, 18 college players died. Roosevelt summoned college presidents and coaches to the White House and demanded reform. The forward pass, opening the field horizontally, dispersed the violence of mass formation play. It was initially used rarely — coaches didn''t trust it. Quarterback Bradbury Robinson and end Jack Schneider threw the first completed legal forward pass on September 5, 1906. Passing became central to strategy only gradually, transforming completely in the "air raid" offensive era of the 1980s–90s.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('44444444-0004-0020-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Why Tennis Scores 15, 30, 40',
   'Tennis''s unusual scoring (15, 30, 40 instead of 1, 2, 3) likely derives from medieval French gambling traditions or clock-face scoring systems — though the exact origin is debated and may be lost to history.',
   'The most cited explanation involves a clock face divided into four quarters: 15, 30, 45 minutes, where the fourth point won the game. "45" is thought to have contracted to "40" either for ease of calling or to create space for deuce (requiring two consecutive points to win when tied). Alternative theories trace the scoring to French jeu de paume (real tennis) gambling conventions where 15 sous was the standard bet. What is certain is that the system predates modern tennis (invented 1873) by centuries, as it appears in medieval court tennis records. The word "love" for zero may derive from the French "l''oeuf" (the egg, meaning a zero-shaped goose egg) or from "love" as playing for nothing (no stakes).',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('44444444-0004-0021-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'How Analytics Changed Basketball',
   'The "analytics revolution" in basketball — driven by Daryl Morey in Houston and later spreading league-wide — fundamentally changed shot selection, lineup construction, and player valuation by applying data science to decisions previously made by intuition.',
   'The key analytical insight was shot value: a three-pointer is worth 1.5x a two-pointer if made at the same rate, so teams should maximize shots at the rim (high percentage two-pointers) and three-pointers, while eliminating long two-pointers (the worst shot in basketball by expected value). The Rockets under Morey took this to an extreme, nearly eliminating mid-range shots. Spatial tracking data (installed in all arenas by 2014) enabled entirely new metrics: defensive coverage, off-ball movement, pick-and-roll efficiency. Player valuation shifted from traditional stats (points, rebounds, assists) to metrics like RAPTOR, LEBRON, and EPM that attempt to capture total contribution. The league''s three-point attempts have roughly doubled since 2012.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('44444444-0004-0022-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Overtraining Syndrome',
   'Overtraining syndrome occurs when training load exceeds recovery capacity over weeks or months — producing not improved fitness but declining performance, persistent fatigue, mood disturbance, and immune suppression.',
   'Overtraining is distinct from normal fatigue: a few days of rest does not resolve it. Full recovery typically requires weeks to months of dramatically reduced training. Diagnosis is challenging because there is no single biomarker — it is characterized by a constellation of symptoms including performance decline despite adequate rest, elevated resting heart rate, sleep disturbance, increased injury susceptibility, and psychological indicators like loss of motivation and depression. Elite athletes are paradoxically at highest risk because they have the fitness to tolerate high training loads — until the cumulative deficit catches them. The Matveyev model of periodization includes mandatory recovery phases specifically to prevent overtraining; "more is better" is the most dangerous belief in endurance sport.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('44444444-0004-0023-0004-000000000004', 'sports', 'Left-Hand Advantage in Racket Sports',
   'Left-handed athletes are significantly overrepresented at the elite levels of tennis, squash, fencing, and boxing relative to their 10% share of the general population — a phenomenon explained by the frequency-dependent advantage of unusual opponents.',
   'In interactive sports where opponents directly face each other, left-handers benefit from two asymmetric advantages: they have extensive experience playing right-handers, while right-handers have little experience playing left-handers. The "fighting hypothesis" in evolutionary biology proposes that left-handedness is maintained in the population by this frequency-dependent advantage — common enough to encounter, rare enough to give an edge. In tennis, left-handers'' serves curve in the opposite direction from right-handers'' serves, creating unexpected trajectories. Rafael Nadal''s heavy topspin to the backhand (most players'' weaker side) became his forehand because he plays left-handed despite being naturally right-handed for other activities.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Finance ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('55555555-0005-0004-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'Tulip Mania Was Mostly Exaggerated',
   'The Dutch tulip mania of 1636–37 — supposedly the first speculative bubble — has been significantly overstated by economic historians. Actual trading was limited to a small network; widespread economic ruin is largely a myth.',
   'Anne Goldgar''s meticulous archival research in "Tulipmania" (2007) found that the market involved far fewer people than legend suggests, that few contracts were actually honored at peak prices, and that contemporary evidence of economic devastation is thin. The cautionary tale was constructed and amplified by Dutch Calvinist moralists who saw the speculation as a sin against frugality. It was subsequently recycled uncritically by economists from Charles Mackay (1841) onward as a founding example of irrational market behavior. The myth''s persistence reveals something important: we need cautionary financial tales, and we will preserve and embellish them regardless of accuracy.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('55555555-0005-0005-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'Loss Aversion Costs Investors More Than Fees',
   'Loss aversion — the tendency to feel losses about twice as intensely as equivalent gains — causes investors to sell during downturns, lock in losses, and miss recoveries. Behavioral costs from bad timing typically exceed even high fee costs.',
   'Kahneman and Tversky''s Prospect Theory (1979) documented that losses feel roughly twice as painful as equivalent gains feel pleasurable. For investors, this manifests as panic selling during market downturns — crystallizing paper losses into real ones — and reluctance to reinvest during recoveries. Dalbar''s annual Quantitative Analysis of Investor Behavior consistently shows that average equity fund investors earn significantly less than the funds themselves return over 20-year periods (roughly 4% vs. 7% annually) because they buy high (after good performance) and sell low (after bad performance). The single most valuable financial advice is often simply: stay invested through volatility.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('55555555-0005-0006-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'Dollar-Cost Averaging',
   'Dollar-cost averaging (DCA) means investing a fixed amount at regular intervals regardless of market conditions — buying more shares when prices are low and fewer when high. It removes the need to time the market.',
   'DCA works through a simple arithmetic property: buying a fixed dollar amount at varying prices results in a lower average cost per share than buying a fixed number of shares at varying prices. A $100 monthly investment buying shares at $10, $5, and $20 acquires 10, 20, and 5 shares respectively — 35 shares at an average cost of $8.57, vs. the average price of $11.67. Psychologically, DCA also removes the paralysis of "waiting for the right moment" — a waiting game that typically leads to buying at higher prices after extended recoveries. Studies comparing lump-sum investing (when money is available) vs. DCA show lump-sum wins about 2/3 of the time in markets that trend upward, but DCA wins on risk-adjusted terms for investors with genuine loss aversion.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('55555555-0005-0007-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'The January Effect Has Been Arbitraged Away',
   'The January Effect — the historical tendency for stock prices, especially small-cap stocks, to rise in January — was once a reliable anomaly. Once documented, it was traded away and largely disappeared.',
   'The January Effect was first documented by Sidney Wachtel in 1942. The proposed mechanism: tax-loss harvesting in December (selling losers to realize tax deductions) depresses prices, followed by reinvestment in January lifting them. Once the effect was widely known and published, institutional investors began buying in December to capture the January rise — eliminating the return. This illustrates the efficient market hypothesis''s self-defeating mechanism: published anomalies get arbitraged away by the very attention they attract. Several studies since the 1990s have confirmed the January Effect has weakened substantially in US markets, though some researchers find residual effects in less-traded markets with less institutional attention.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('55555555-0005-0008-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'The Minsky Moment: Boom-Bust Cycles',
   'Economist Hyman Minsky argued that financial stability itself breeds instability: prolonged stability encourages risk-taking, leverage, and speculation until the system becomes fragile — and then collapses at a "Minsky moment."',
   'Minsky''s Financial Instability Hypothesis (1992) describes three stages of debt: hedge finance (borrowers can service debt from income), speculative finance (borrowers need to roll over debt), and Ponzi finance (borrowers need asset price appreciation to service debt). As stability persists, lenders relax standards and borrowers take more risk, moving from hedge to Ponzi finance. The "Minsky moment" is when the pyramid collapses — typically triggered by some modest tightening that causes overleveraged positions to unwind violently. The 2007–08 financial crisis followed this pattern almost textbook precisely: two decades of "the Great Moderation" bred the complacency and leverage that made the crisis so severe. Minsky died in 1996, largely ignored; he became required reading after 2008.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('55555555-0005-0009-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'The Sunk Cost Fallacy in Investing',
   'The sunk cost fallacy leads investors to hold losing positions because of money already spent — rather than because of future expected returns. Rational investing requires evaluating positions purely on their future prospects.',
   'The sunk cost fallacy occurs because losses are psychologically registered as ongoing until realized. An investor holding a stock that has fallen 50% often thinks: "I can''t sell now, I''d be locking in my loss." But the loss already happened — it is sunk. The rational question is: given current prices, is this the best use of this capital going forward? Holding a losing position because of what was paid is letting past decisions distort present ones. Professional fund managers are often insulated from this (they evaluate positions on forward-looking metrics) while retail investors are most vulnerable. Forced sell disciplines — like stop-loss rules or systematic rebalancing — help by removing emotional decision-making from exit decisions.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('55555555-0005-0010-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'Gold''s Real Return Over 100 Years Is Near Zero',
   'Gold is widely considered a store of value and inflation hedge. Over long time horizons, it approximately keeps pace with inflation — meaning its real (inflation-adjusted) return is close to zero. It produces no income.',
   'From 1900 to 2020, gold''s inflation-adjusted return was approximately 0.5–1% annually — far below equities (roughly 6–7% real). Gold does not produce dividends, rent, or earnings; it is purely a bet on nominal price appreciation. Its value as a "safe haven" is real but situational: it performs well during acute crises and periods of very high inflation but underperforms dramatically over multi-decade horizons. Claude Erb and Campbell Harvey''s research on gold (2013) showed that gold''s "fair value" is roughly twice the per-ounce cost of a Roman soldier''s annual kit — suggesting gold tracks purchasing power over centuries but creates no real wealth. Holding gold as insurance against catastrophic scenarios is defensible; holding it as a primary investment is not.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('55555555-0005-0011-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'Why 80% of Day Traders Lose Money',
   'Studies of day traders consistently show that roughly 70–80% lose money after accounting for transaction costs, taxes, and the bid-ask spread. Active trading is a negative-sum game for retail participants.',
   'A landmark Brazilian study (Chague, De-Losso, and Giovannetti, 2020) tracked every day trader in Brazil over a decade, finding that only 1.1% of active day traders earned more than the minimum wage after costs — and that success was not persistent (winners in year one were not winners in year two). The structural disadvantage is fundamental: retail traders pay bid-ask spreads, commissions, and capital gains taxes on every trade. High-frequency trading firms operate with nanosecond-level execution advantages and information asymmetries that retail participants cannot overcome. The survivors who trade actively tend to have genuine information advantages (unusual industry expertise) or systematic algorithmic advantages — not better chart-reading.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('55555555-0005-0012-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'Compound Interest: The Eighth Wonder',
   'Compound interest — earning returns on both principal and accumulated returns — produces exponential rather than linear growth over long time horizons. A small rate difference compounded over decades produces enormous outcome differences.',
   'Einstein may or may not have called compound interest the eighth wonder (the attribution is disputed), but the mathematics are genuinely remarkable. $10,000 invested at 7% annually becomes $76,000 in 30 years; at 10% it becomes $174,000 — a 129% difference from a 3 percentage point rate difference. Warren Buffett has earned 99% of his wealth after age 50 — despite being a skilled investor since childhood — because compounding is a time function. The practical implication: starting early matters far more than investing large amounts later. A 25-year-old investing $200/month until 65 at 7% outperforms a 35-year-old investing $400/month to the same age — simply by starting 10 years earlier.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('55555555-0005-0013-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'The Fed Put: Central Banks as Market Backstops',
   'The "Fed Put" describes investors'' expectation that the Federal Reserve will cut interest rates or intervene to prevent market crashes — essentially providing a floor on asset prices. This expectation shapes risk-taking throughout the financial system.',
   'The term originated after Alan Greenspan cut rates following the 1987 Black Monday crash and became explicit policy during the 1998 LTCM bailout and 2001 recession. Like a put option (which provides downside protection), the Fed Put encourages risk-taking because investors believe losses will be backstopped. This creates a moral hazard: the expectation of a bailout encourages the excessive risk-taking that makes bailouts necessary. The Fed has reinforced the expectation through the 2008 bailouts, 2020 bond purchases, and 2023 emergency bank lending. Critics argue this contributes to asset bubbles and inequality by systematically supporting financial asset prices — benefiting primarily the wealthy who hold financial assets.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('55555555-0005-0014-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'The History of Insurance',
   'Insurance is one of humanity''s oldest financial innovations — the Code of Hammurabi (1754 BCE) included provisions for marine loans that were forgiven if cargo was lost at sea, a primitive form of risk pooling. The first true insurance contracts appeared in 14th-century Genoa and Venice: merchants pooled risk against ship losses for a fee, separating the transfer of risk from the underlying loan.',
   'From this Mediterranean foundation the industry grew outward. Lloyd''s of London began as a coffee house in 1688 where merchants and ship captains gathered to share risk on individual voyages — each underwriter signing for a share rather than the whole. The Great Fire of London in 1666, which destroyed 13,000 homes, created the political will for fire insurance infrastructure and organized fire brigades. Life insurance developed through the 18th century as actuarial mathematics advanced — Edmond Halley (of comet fame) produced the first mortality tables in 1693. The modern insurance industry works because risk is predictable at scale even when individual outcomes are not: you cannot know if your house will burn, but an actuary can estimate the fraction of 100,000 houses that will burn within a year, allowing premiums to be priced with precision.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('55555555-0005-0015-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'The 4% Retirement Withdrawal Rule',
   'The 4% rule holds that retirees can withdraw 4% of their portfolio in year one, then adjust for inflation each year, with high probability of not outliving a 30-year retirement — based on historical US market data.',
   'Financial planner William Bengen published the original research in 1994, analyzing all 30-year retirement periods from 1926 onward and finding that a 4% initial withdrawal rate survived all historical scenarios, including the worst market stretches. The Trinity Study (1998) by three professors extended and popularized the finding. Critics note: the rule is based on US historical market returns that may not repeat; it uses a 30-year horizon that may be too short for early retirees; and current low interest rates and high valuations may reduce future returns. Updated research suggests 3–3.5% may be more conservative given current conditions. The rule is a useful starting framework, not a guarantee.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('55555555-0005-0016-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'How Black-Scholes Changed Finance',
   'The Black-Scholes options pricing model (1973) was the first mathematically rigorous formula for pricing options contracts. It triggered an explosion in derivatives markets and, eventually, contributed to the conditions for the 2008 financial crisis.',
   'Fischer Black, Myron Scholes, and Robert Merton derived a partial differential equation using physical Brownian motion mathematics to price a European call option. The formula made options pricing tractable — before it, pricing was essentially guesswork. The Chicago Board Options Exchange opened the same year; within a decade, options trading volumes multiplied 100-fold. Scholes and Merton won the 1997 Nobel Prize (Black had died). Their Long-Term Capital Management hedge fund used the same mathematical framework and collapsed spectacularly in 1998, requiring a Fed-organized bailout — demonstrating that the model''s assumption of continuous, normally distributed price changes fails catastrophically in actual tail-risk events.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('55555555-0005-0017-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'The Carry Trade',
   'The carry trade involves borrowing in a low-interest-rate currency and investing in a high-interest-rate one, pocketing the rate differential — a strategy that works steadily until it doesn''t, unraveling violently when exchange rates shift.',
   'Classic carry trades borrow in Japanese yen (low rates) and invest in Australian dollars or emerging market bonds (high rates). The trade earns the interest rate differential as long as exchange rates remain stable or move favorably. The risk is sudden "carry trade unwind": when the funding currency strengthens or risk aversion rises, carry traders simultaneously close positions, amplifying currency moves. The 2008 financial crisis saw dramatic yen carry unwinds; the August 2024 market turbulence was similarly triggered by yen strengthening forcing global carry unwinds. The carry trade embodies a fundamental risk-return principle: consistent small gains come at the cost of occasional large losses — a pattern that looks attractive until the catastrophic reversal.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('55555555-0005-0018-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'Why Countries Devalue Their Currencies',
   'Currency devaluation — reducing the exchange rate of a national currency — makes exports cheaper and imports more expensive, potentially boosting manufacturing and reducing trade deficits. But it also raises inflation and erodes savings.',
   'Countries with fixed exchange rates (pegged to another currency or gold) can formally devalue by resetting the peg; countries with floating currencies allow market forces to weaken their currency or actively sell reserves. The logic: a cheaper currency makes domestically produced goods cheaper for foreign buyers, boosting export competitiveness. The UK''s 1967 sterling devaluation by Harold Wilson (his "pound in your pocket" speech) was a politically damaging admission of economic failure. The 1994 Mexican peso crisis showed the risks of sudden uncontrolled devaluation: foreign-currency-denominated debt becomes immediately unserviceable, triggering sovereign default and banking crises. Competitive devaluations (multiple countries simultaneously weakening currencies) produce inflation with no competitive benefit — the "beggar-thy-neighbor" trap.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('55555555-0005-0019-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'The History of the Credit Card',
   'The modern credit card was born in 1950 when Frank McNamara, unable to pay a restaurant bill, founded Diners Club — the first general-purpose charge card. Within a decade, it had spawned an industry that now processes trillions annually.',
   'McNamara''s founding insight was that creditworthiness could be separated from any specific merchant relationship, creating a general medium of deferred payment. Bank of America launched the BankAmericard (later Visa) in 1958 by mailing unsolicited cards to 60,000 Fresno residents. American Express followed. The interchange fee model — merchants pay a percentage to card networks — created a profitable structure that funded rewards programs and drove adoption. Credit cards democratized access to credit but created new debt traps: the minimum payment design deliberately extends payoff periods and maximizes interest. The consumer credit industry now earns most of its revenue from the 40% of cardholders who carry balances rather than the 60% who pay in full.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('55555555-0005-0020-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'How Warren Buffett''s Value Investing Works',
   'Warren Buffett''s approach — learned from Benjamin Graham and modified through Charlie Munger''s influence — involves buying businesses trading below their intrinsic value, with durable competitive advantages, held indefinitely.',
   'Graham''s "margin of safety" principle: buy only when price is significantly below intrinsic value, providing cushion against estimation errors. Buffett refined this with Munger''s insight that paying a fair price for a wonderful business beats paying a wonderful price for a fair business — shifting from cheap, mediocre companies to quality companies at reasonable prices. The key concept is "economic moat" (durable competitive advantage): Coca-Cola''s brand, See''s Candies'' local dominance, GEICO''s cost structure. Buffett holds indefinitely because selling triggers capital gains taxes and loses the compound growth of reinvested retained earnings. His 20% rule — never more than 20 portfolio additions in a lifetime — forces extreme selectivity. The strategy is well-understood but psychologically difficult: buying when others panic and holding through downturns.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('55555555-0005-0021-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'The Paradox of Thrift',
   'Keynes''s paradox of thrift: what is rational for an individual during a recession (save more, spend less) becomes destructive when everyone does it simultaneously, reducing aggregate demand and deepening the very downturn that motivated the saving.',
   'In microeconomics, saving is virtuous: it builds wealth and funds investment. But in macroeconomics, spending by one person is income for another. When households simultaneously increase saving rates during a downturn, aggregate demand falls, businesses cut production and workers, incomes fall, and households save even more — a deflationary spiral. Keynes used this fallacy of composition to argue for government deficit spending as the only entity that can break the cycle: when private demand collapses, public demand must fill the gap. The 1930s Great Depression and 2008–09 recession both involved paradox-of-thrift dynamics. The political challenge is that government stimulus is counterintuitive when households are tightening — the macroeconomic logic runs against everyday financial wisdom.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('55555555-0005-0022-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'Why Inflation Hits the Poor Hardest',
   'Inflation is not neutral across income levels. Lower-income households spend higher proportions of income on necessities (food, energy, rent) whose prices tend to rise faster than luxury goods during inflationary periods.',
   'The Consumer Price Index (CPI) measures average price changes for a notional "basket of goods" — but the composition of that basket varies dramatically by income. Research by Olivier Coibion and colleagues showed that during the 2021–23 inflation episode, lower-income households experienced inflation 2–3 percentage points higher than upper-income households because energy and food (which rose most) represent larger budget shares for the poor. Additionally, lower-income households have fewer financial assets: stock portfolios and real estate provide partial inflation hedges for the wealthy that renters and wage earners don''t have. Fixed-income savers (retirees, people with savings accounts) also suffer because nominal interest rates often lag inflation.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('55555555-0005-0023-0005-000000000005', 'finance', 'The Principal-Agent Problem in Executive Pay',
   'The principal-agent problem arises when one party (agent) is paid to act in another party (principal)''s interest but has different incentives. Executive compensation is a textbook case: CEOs paid in stock options may maximize short-term share price at the expense of long-term value.',
   'Shareholders (principals) want executives (agents) to maximize long-term firm value. Stock options were introduced in the 1980s and 90s as an alignment mechanism — tying pay to share price. But options create asymmetric incentives: executives profit from price increases but don''t bear proportionate losses from declines. This encourages excessive risk-taking and short-termism: cutting R&D and capital investment to boost near-term earnings per share, or buying back shares to inflate price before options vest. Research by Lucian Bebchuk documents that average CEO pay in the US grew from 20x average worker pay in 1965 to 350x by 2020, without proportionate productivity gains — evidence that boards have limited ability to constrain the agents they are supposed to monitor.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Science ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('66666666-0006-0004-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'Why the Sky Is Blue: Rayleigh Scattering',
   'The sky is blue because air molecules scatter short-wavelength blue light much more efficiently than long-wavelength red light. At sunset, light travels a longer path through the atmosphere, scattering away blue and leaving red and orange.',
   'Lord Rayleigh worked out the scattering mathematics in 1871: scattering intensity is proportional to the inverse fourth power of wavelength. Blue light (wavelength ~450 nm) scatters roughly 5.5 times more than red light (~700 nm). Sunlight contains all wavelengths; by the time it reaches your eye from any direction other than directly at the sun, the blue has been scattered toward you preferentially. At sunset, the sun is near the horizon and light traverses 10x more atmosphere; nearly all blue is scattered away, leaving the rich reds and oranges. Mars''s sky appears reddish-pink because iron-oxide dust (red) scatters light differently than nitrogen and oxygen molecules.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('66666666-0006-0005-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'The Placebo Effect Is Getting Stronger',
   'Clinical trials in the US show that the placebo effect in pain studies has been growing stronger over decades. Patients on placebo report greater pain relief today than they did 30 years ago — making it harder for real drugs to demonstrate superiority.',
   'A 2015 analysis of 84 clinical trials for neuropathic pain drugs found the placebo response nearly doubled between 1990 and 2013 in US trials. The effect was not seen in European or Asian trials, suggesting something specific about US clinical trial culture — possibly increased direct-to-consumer drug advertising making patients more expectant, or more elaborate trial protocols generating stronger expectation effects. The placebo effect is not imaginary: neuroimaging shows genuine opioid release, cortisol reduction, and dopamine activation in response to placebo. Open-label placebo (patients knowingly take a sugar pill) still works — even when patients know it''s inert — because the ritualized context of medical care may drive the effect.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('66666666-0006-0006-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'Falsifiability: The Criterion for Real Science',
   'Karl Popper argued that a theory is scientific only if it makes predictions that could be proven wrong. Theories that can explain any possible outcome — and thus cannot be falsified — are not science but metaphysics.',
   'Popper developed falsifiability as a "demarcation criterion" to distinguish science from pseudoscience. His examples: Freudian psychoanalysis and Marxist economics could explain any observable outcome — they were unfalsifiable and thus unscientific. Einstein''s general relativity, by contrast, made specific predictions (light bending around the sun) that could be — and were — tested. Modern philosophy of science has complicated Popper''s picture: theories are tested as systems (the Duhem-Quine thesis), not in isolation, and auxiliary assumptions can always be adjusted to save a core theory. But falsifiability remains the best single heuristic for evaluating scientific claims — "what observation would change your mind?" is a powerful question.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('66666666-0006-0007-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'The Double-Slit Experiment',
   'When particles — even single electrons fired one at a time — pass through two slits, they produce an interference pattern as if they were waves. But when you measure which slit they go through, the interference disappears. Observation changes the outcome.',
   'The double-slit experiment was first performed with light by Thomas Young in 1801, establishing light''s wave nature. The quantum twist emerged when physicists tried it with single particles: each electron arrives at a point, like a particle, but the accumulated hits form an interference pattern, like a wave. When detectors are added to determine which slit each electron passes through, the interference pattern vanishes — even if the detection happens after the particle has passed the slits. This is not a measurement-disturbs-particle story: the wave function that determines probabilities seems to respond to whether information about the path exists anywhere in the universe. No fully satisfying explanation exists — it is the central mystery of quantum mechanics.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('66666666-0006-0008-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'The Replication Crisis',
   'Roughly 50–70% of published psychology and biomedical findings fail to replicate when other researchers attempt to reproduce them. This is not fraud — it reflects systematic structural problems in how science is practiced and published.',
   'The Open Science Collaboration''s 2015 effort to replicate 100 psychology studies found only 36–39% reproduced the original results. Contributing factors include: publication bias (journals prefer positive findings, so negative results go unpublished), small sample sizes (underpowered studies have high false-positive rates), p-hacking (running multiple analyses until p<0.05 is achieved), and HARKing (Hypothesizing After Results are Known). Fields vary: physics and chemistry replicate well; social psychology and nutritional epidemiology replicate poorly. Solutions include pre-registration (publishing hypotheses before collecting data), larger samples, and journals that accept replication studies. The crisis has produced a methodological reform movement that is genuinely improving scientific practice.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('66666666-0006-0009-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'Emergence: Complexity from Simple Rules',
   'Emergence describes how complex, unpredictable phenomena arise from simple underlying rules. Consciousness from neurons, traffic jams from individual driving decisions, market prices from individual trades — all are emergent phenomena.',
   'Philip Anderson''s essay "More Is Different" (1972) articulated emergence''s challenge to reductionism: knowing the laws of physics does not allow you to predict the laws of chemistry, knowing chemistry does not allow you to predict biology, knowing biology does not allow you to predict psychology. At each level, genuinely new properties appear that are not deducible from lower-level rules alone. Ant colonies exhibit sophisticated collective problem-solving with no central coordination — the colony "knows" things no individual ant knows. Murmurations of starlings produce fluid, coherent shapes from three simple local rules each bird follows. The hard problem is that emergence can be observed but its mechanism — how macro-properties arise from micro-rules — is often deeply unclear.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('66666666-0006-0010-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'Bees Can Fly: The Physics Myth Explained',
   'The famous claim that "bees shouldn''t be able to fly according to physics" was based on an error from the 1930s — aerodynamic calculations applied to fixed wings rather than the complex, rotating, figure-eight motion of insect wings.',
   'The myth originated with French entomologist Antoine Magnan''s 1934 book, where he acknowledged that a colleague''s rough calculation showed bees couldn''t fly under simplified aerodynamic assumptions. He immediately noted this was clearly wrong. But the "bees can''t fly" meme escaped its context. Modern high-speed photography revealed that bees use a fundamentally different mechanism: short, rapid wing strokes in a complex figure-eight pattern that generates lift through unsteady aerodynamics — particularly a leading-edge vortex that fixed-wing calculations don''t capture. A 2005 Caltech study precisely modeled insect flight aerodynamics, definitively explaining how bees fly. The myth persists because it''s a satisfying story about science''s limitations.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('66666666-0006-0011-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'Why We Age: Competing Theories',
   'Aging lacks a single accepted explanation. Leading theories include accumulated DNA damage, shortening telomeres, mitochondrial dysfunction, cellular senescence, and evolutionary disposability — all contribute, but their relative importance is debated.',
   'The disposable soma theory (Kirkwood, 1977) frames aging evolutionarily: organisms invest limited energy in either reproduction or bodily maintenance; natural selection favors reproduction, leaving maintenance underfunded. Telomere attrition: chromosomal end-caps shorten with each cell division until cells enter senescence or apoptosis — telomerase can rebuild them but is suppressed in most adult cells (overexpression is associated with cancer). Senescent cells accumulate: they stop dividing but secrete inflammatory signals (the "senescence-associated secretory phenotype") that damage neighboring tissue. Senolytics — drugs that selectively eliminate senescent cells — are in clinical trials and show promising results in animal models. Aging research has accelerated dramatically since the discovery that single gene changes can dramatically extend lifespan in model organisms.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('66666666-0006-0012-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'Homeostasis',
   'Homeostasis is the body''s tendency to maintain stable internal conditions — temperature, blood sugar, pH, oxygen levels — despite external changes. It is the defining property of living systems and operates through feedback loops.',
   'Negative feedback is the core mechanism: a sensor detects deviation from a set point, and effectors act to correct it. Body temperature illustrates this precisely — when core temperature rises, sweat glands activate and surface blood vessels dilate to dissipate heat; when it falls, shivering generates heat and vasoconstriction preserves it. Blood glucose is regulated by opposing hormones: insulin (secreted by the pancreas when glucose is high) drives glucose into cells; glucagon (secreted when glucose is low) triggers glycogen breakdown in the liver. Type 1 diabetes is a failure of insulin production; Type 2 is a failure of insulin response — both expose how dependent survival is on this one feedback loop. The concept has been extended beyond physiology: allostasis describes set points that shift based on anticipated demands rather than simply correcting deviations after the fact.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('66666666-0006-0013-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'The Discovery of Oxygen: Who Really Did It?',
   'Oxygen was discovered independently by Carl Wilhelm Scheele in 1772 and Joseph Priestley in 1774. Antoine Lavoisier — who correctly understood what it was and named it — is often credited with the discovery he did not make first.',
   'Scheele, a Swedish chemist, produced oxygen in 1772 through several methods and recognized it as a distinct gas supporting combustion. Due to publishing delays, his work appeared in 1777. Priestley isolated oxygen from mercuric oxide in 1774 and shared his findings with Lavoisier. Lavoisier ran definitive experiments showing oxygen''s role in combustion and named it (from Greek for "acid-forming," a property he incorrectly believed it possessed universally). Lavoisier''s conceptual framework — dismantling phlogiston theory and establishing modern chemistry''s framework — earned him the mantle of "father of modern chemistry." Priority disputes in science are common because discovery is rarely a single moment; Scheele''s case is one of history''s most egregious instances of credit misattribution.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('66666666-0006-0014-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'How Vaccines Work at the Cellular Level',
   'Vaccines train the immune system by presenting antigens — harmless fragments or weakened versions of a pathogen — prompting an immune response that creates lasting memory cells without causing disease.',
   'The adaptive immune system has two key memory cell types: B cells (which produce antibodies that neutralize pathogens) and T cells (which kill infected cells and coordinate immune response). Vaccines introduce antigen (the threat signal) without the pathogen''s ability to replicate and cause disease. The immune system responds, and a subset of responding B and T cells become long-lived memory cells. On re-exposure to the actual pathogen, these memory cells respond within hours rather than the days required for a naive immune response — preventing disease or greatly reducing its severity. mRNA vaccines (COVID-19) deliver instructions for cells to produce the antigen themselves, a method developed over 30 years of prior research. The body degrades the mRNA within days; it does not integrate into DNA.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('66666666-0006-0015-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'The Gut Microbiome and the Brain',
   'The gut contains roughly 100 million neurons and 37 trillion bacteria, communicating with the brain via the vagus nerve, immune signals, and hormone production. This gut-brain axis influences mood, cognition, and mental health.',
   'The enteric nervous system — the gut''s own neural network — operates largely independently of the brain. Gut bacteria produce neurotransmitters including 90% of the body''s serotonin and large quantities of GABA, dopamine precursors, and short-chain fatty acids that cross the blood-brain barrier. Germ-free mice (raised without any gut bacteria) show dramatically different stress responses and anxiety-like behavior compared to normal mice; transplanting gut bacteria from anxious to calm mice transfers aspects of the behavioral profile. Human studies link gut microbiome composition to depression, anxiety, and cognitive function, though causality is difficult to establish. Probiotics, diet, and fecal transplants are being investigated as psychiatric interventions — a radical rethinking of mental health treatment.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('66666666-0006-0016-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'Why We Dream: Memory Consolidation Theory',
   'The dominant scientific theory holds that dreams serve memory consolidation — the brain replaying and integrating experiences during sleep to transfer them from short-term hippocampal storage to long-term cortical networks.',
   'REM (rapid eye movement) sleep, when most dreaming occurs, shows hippocampal-cortical dialogue: sharp-wave ripples in the hippocampus replay daytime experiences, and these replays appear in cortical regions simultaneously. Studies by Matthew Walker and colleagues show that sleeping after learning improves retention by 20–40% vs. staying awake. Targeted memory reactivation — playing odors or sounds associated with learned material during sleep — enhances consolidation of those specific memories. The theory doesn''t fully explain why dreams are bizarre and emotionally charged rather than simple replays: Matthew Walker proposes that REM sleep also functions as "emotional first aid," replaying distressing memories in a neurochemically calm state (low norepinephrine) to process emotional content without re-traumatizing.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('66666666-0006-0017-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'The Speed of Light as a Cosmic Speed Limit',
   'Nothing with mass can reach the speed of light because the energy required to accelerate it increases with velocity, approaching infinity as speed approaches c. This is not a technological limitation but a fundamental property of spacetime.',
   'Einstein''s special relativity (1905) showed that space and time are not separate, absolute entities but are woven into spacetime that warps around mass and energy. As an object with mass accelerates, its relativistic mass increases, requiring exponentially more energy to accelerate further. At 99% of c, it would require ~7x its rest energy; at 99.9%, ~22x. The energy required to reach c is literally infinite. This is not a "speed limit" imposed from outside — it is a structural feature of how spacetime works. Light has no mass, so the limit doesn''t apply. Quantum entanglement can correlate particles faster than light but cannot transmit information at superluminal speeds — the no-communication theorem establishes this rigorously.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('66666666-0006-0018-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'How Nuclear Fission Releases Energy',
   'When a heavy nucleus like uranium-235 splits, the total mass of fragments is slightly less than the original. That missing mass is converted to energy according to E=mc² — a tiny mass difference yields enormous energy because c² is enormous.',
   'E=mc² means mass and energy are equivalent, with c² (9×10¹⁶ m²/s²) as the conversion factor. When uranium-235 absorbs a neutron and splits, the fragments'' mass is 0.1% less than the original — but 0.1% × c² per kilogram is roughly 9×10¹³ joules — the energy equivalent of 21,000 tonnes of TNT per kilogram of uranium. Nuclear power plants use this energy to boil water and drive steam turbines: the same process as a coal plant, but with fuel 3 million times more energy-dense. A pellet of uranium fuel roughly the size of a fingertip contains as much energy as 17,000 cubic feet of natural gas. The spent fuel''s radioactivity is the price of this energy density.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('66666666-0006-0019-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'Rosalind Franklin and the DNA Double Helix',
   'The discovery of DNA''s double helix structure by Watson and Crick in 1953 was crucially enabled by Rosalind Franklin''s X-ray crystallography work — shared with Watson without her knowledge or consent.',
   'Franklin''s Photo 51 — an X-ray diffraction image of DNA — showed the helical structure, the dimensions of the helix, and the position of the phosphate backbone with extraordinary clarity. Her lab director, Maurice Wilkins, showed the image to James Watson without telling Franklin. Watson immediately recognized its significance and used it to confirm and refine the model he and Crick were building. Watson and Crick''s 1953 Nature paper acknowledged Franklin''s data only briefly. Franklin died of cancer in 1958, four years before Watson, Crick, and Wilkins received the 1962 Nobel Prize, which cannot be awarded posthumously. The episode is now a canonical case study in scientific credit, gender bias, and research ethics.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('66666666-0006-0020-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'Why Antibiotics Are Losing Effectiveness',
   'Antibiotic resistance occurs when bacteria evolve to survive antibiotic attack through mutation and natural selection. Overuse and misuse of antibiotics accelerates this process, threatening to return common infections to the pre-antibiotic era of mortality.',
   'Every antibiotic use creates selection pressure: most bacteria die, but resistant mutants survive and reproduce. Bacteria also share resistance genes horizontally — a resistant pathogen can transfer its resistance mechanism to entirely different species within hours through plasmid exchange. Agricultural overuse (70% of global antibiotics go to livestock, primarily to promote growth) is a major driver: resistant bacteria in animals transfer to humans through food chains and environmental contamination. WHO estimates antimicrobial resistance could kill 10 million people annually by 2050 — exceeding current cancer mortality. The pipeline of new antibiotics is nearly empty because developing antibiotics is commercially unattractive: they cure patients quickly, unlike chronic disease drugs that generate long-term revenue.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('66666666-0006-0021-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'What the Higgs Boson Actually Is',
   'The Higgs boson is the particle associated with the Higgs field — a field that permeates all of space and gives other particles their mass through their interaction with it. Without the Higgs field, fundamental particles would have no mass and atoms couldn''t form.',
   'In the Standard Model of particle physics, particles acquire mass by interacting with the Higgs field (analogous to moving through a resistive medium). Particles that interact strongly with the field have high mass (top quarks); those that interact weakly have low mass (electrons); photons don''t interact and are massless. The Higgs boson is the excitation of this field — the "particle" form it takes when struck by enough energy. CERN''s Large Hadron Collider detected it on July 4, 2012, with a mass of ~125 GeV — the last missing piece of the Standard Model. Peter Higgs and François Englert won the 2013 Nobel Prize. The discovery doesn''t explain why particles have the specific masses they have — that remains one of physics'' open questions.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('66666666-0006-0022-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'How the Immune System Distinguishes Self from Non-Self',
   'The immune system must attack pathogens while sparing the body''s own cells — a distinction it establishes during development through a selection process in the thymus that deletes T cells that react too strongly to self-proteins.',
   'T cells develop in the thymus through two selection stages. Positive selection: T cells that can recognize self-MHC proteins (the body''s "identity tag" system) survive; those that can''t are deleted. Negative selection: T cells that react too strongly to self-proteins are deleted — eliminating auto-reactive cells that would cause autoimmunity. The survivors: T cells that can recognize self-MHC but don''t attack self-proteins. B cells undergo similar tolerance induction in bone marrow. The system isn''t perfect: autoimmune diseases (lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, type 1 diabetes) occur when tolerance breaks down and the immune system attacks self-tissue. Pregnancy presents a specific challenge — the fetus is "non-self" but is tolerated through specialized immunosuppressive mechanisms.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('66666666-0006-0023-0006-000000000006', 'science', 'Dark Matter: What We Know We Don''t Know',
   'Dark matter is the invisible substance that makes up roughly 27% of the universe''s mass-energy content. We know it exists from its gravitational effects but have never directly detected it — one of the deepest mysteries in physics.',
   'The evidence for dark matter is indirect but overwhelming: galaxies rotate too fast at their edges for the visible matter to hold them together (Fritz Zwicky noticed this in 1933); gravitational lensing bends light more than visible matter can explain; and the cosmic microwave background''s structure requires dark matter to form the seeds of galaxy formation. What it is, however, is unknown. Leading candidates include WIMPs (weakly interacting massive particles), axions, and sterile neutrinos — but decades of increasingly sensitive direct detection experiments have found nothing. An alternative called MOND (modified Newtonian dynamics) attempts to explain the observations without dark matter by modifying gravity''s behavior at low accelerations, but struggles with cosmological data. The mystery is one of physics'' most pressing open problems.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Technology ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('77777777-0007-0004-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'ARPANET Was Not Built to Survive Nuclear War',
   'The myth that the internet was designed to survive nuclear attack is widespread but false. ARPANET was built for research resource-sharing. The nuclear-resilience idea came from a separate RAND study that influenced its design peripherally.',
   'ARPANET, funded by DARPA beginning in 1969, was built to allow researchers to share computing resources across universities — a mundane but expensive problem in an era when mainframes cost millions. Paul Baran''s 1964 RAND paper on distributed communications did propose packet-switched networks as more resilient than centralized ones for nuclear scenarios, and influenced ARPANET''s architects. But the two projects were distinct, and ARPANET''s primary motivation was resource efficiency. The nuclear-survival myth was popularized by a 1988 book and has proven remarkably persistent despite being well-documented as false. The actual internet is far more centralized — and fragile — than the myth implies, with critical infrastructure vulnerable to both physical attack and cyberattack.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('77777777-0007-0005-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'Wirth''s Law: Software Gets Slower Over Time',
   'Niklaus Wirth observed in 1995 that software is getting slower more rapidly than hardware is getting faster. This counterintuitive trend — codified as Wirth''s Law — has intensified in the era of cloud software and Electron apps.',
   'Wirth, creator of the Pascal programming language, noted that programmers rely on faster hardware to compensate for inefficient code — creating a treadmill where hardware advances are immediately consumed by bloated software. The phenomenon accelerated with web technologies: Chrome (or any modern browser) running a web application uses hardware that would have run entire operating systems 20 years ago. "Web bloat" researchers have documented that the average webpage now requires 100x more resources than in 2000 while loading faster only because bandwidth has increased faster. Native apps built with Electron (a framework that ships an entire browser engine with each application) have an enormous baseline resource cost. The incentive structure is clear: programmer time is more expensive than user hardware.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('77777777-0007-0006-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'Open Source: How Sharing Code Built the Internet',
   'The internet''s infrastructure runs overwhelmingly on open source software — Linux, Apache, PostgreSQL, Python, Git. A collaborative model where source code is freely shared and improved has outcompeted proprietary alternatives across most critical domains.',
   'Linux powers 96.3% of the world''s top 1 million web servers. Git (Linus Torvalds''s 2005 creation) manages version control for virtually all software development. The Apache Software Foundation''s server software has powered more than half the web since the late 1990s. The economic logic: open source aligns incentives across companies who share infrastructure costs while competing on applications built on top. IBM, Google, Microsoft, and Amazon collectively employ thousands of open source contributors and have contributed billions in code, not from altruism but because the shared infrastructure benefits their core businesses. The Linux Foundation''s Linux kernel has over 15,000 contributors from 1,400 companies — the largest collaborative software project in history.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('77777777-0007-0007-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'QWERTY Was Not Designed to Slow Typists',
   'The widely repeated claim that QWERTY keyboards were designed to slow typists to prevent typewriter jamming is false. QWERTY was designed to speed typists — specifically by separating commonly paired letters to reduce mechanical arm collisions.',
   'Researchers Koichi Yasuoka and Motoko Yasuoka traced QWERTY''s design history to Sholes & Glidden typewriter prototypes (1873–1878). The layout evolved through iterative feedback from telegraph operators and typewriter salesmen who needed to type Morse code transcriptions quickly. Letters frequently paired in Morse (like "OE" and "IS") were separated so mechanical typebars wouldn''t jam. The "Dvorak is faster" alternative-layout claim is also overstated: modern keyboards have no jamming problem, but decades of studies show typists retrained on Dvorak gain at most 5–10% speed — far below the 70% improvement its inventor claimed. QWERTY''s persistence is a classic example of path dependence: suboptimal standards become locked in through installed base effects.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('77777777-0007-0008-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'Why AI Language Models Hallucinate',
   'AI language models generate text by predicting likely next tokens — they have no ground-truth knowledge base they check against. When asked about something outside their training or asking for precision, they produce plausible-sounding but false information.',
   'Large language models learn statistical patterns from vast text corpora, developing internal representations that encode facts implicitly. But the generation process is next-token prediction: given the context, what token is most likely to follow? This process is not retrieval from a verified database — it is pattern completion. When a model is asked about a rare fact it saw rarely (or never) in training, it generates what a confident, knowledgeable-sounding text about that topic would likely say. The result can be fluent, confidently stated, and completely false. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) addresses this by retrieving actual documents before generating; chain-of-thought reasoning improves factual accuracy by making the model''s reasoning process explicit and checkable.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('77777777-0007-0009-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'Algorithmic Bias: When Training Data Encodes Discrimination',
   'Machine learning systems trained on historical data learn the patterns in that data — including discriminatory patterns. An algorithm that learned from biased human decisions will reproduce and often amplify those biases at scale.',
   'ProPublica''s 2016 investigation of COMPAS (a recidivism prediction algorithm used in US courts) found it falsely flagged Black defendants as future criminals at twice the rate of white defendants. Amazon''s résumé-screening AI, trained on a decade of hiring decisions (predominantly male), penalized CVs containing the word "women''s" and graduates of all-women''s colleges. Facial recognition systems trained primarily on lighter-skinned faces show dramatically higher error rates for darker-skinned individuals (up to 35% error vs. under 1% for light-skinned males in one MIT study). The core problem: if training data reflects historical discrimination, the algorithm learns historical discrimination as the correct pattern. Technical fixes (rebalancing training data, fairness constraints) can reduce but rarely eliminate the problem.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('77777777-0007-0010-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'The First Computer Bug Was a Real Moth',
   'The word "bug" for an engineering defect predates computers — Thomas Edison used it in 1878 and it appears throughout 19th-century mechanical engineering. But the term got its most memorable computer-specific illustration on September 9, 1947, when Grace Hopper''s team at Harvard found a moth trapped in a relay of the Mark II computer, and taped it into the logbook with the note "First actual case of bug being found."',
   'The logbook, now preserved at the Smithsonian Institution''s National Museum of American History, is the most famous artifact in computing history. Hopper herself went far beyond it: she developed the first compiler (A-0 system, 1952), which translated human-readable code into machine instructions — a conceptual leap that transformed programming from machine-specific to language-agnostic work. She later led the design of COBOL, the business programming language that still runs significant portions of banking and government infrastructure today. She championed the idea that programming should be accessible without deep hardware knowledge — a philosophy that shaped every high-level language that followed. Hopper was awarded the National Medal of Technology in 1991 and the Presidential Medal of Freedom posthumously in 2016.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('77777777-0007-0011-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'Why Encryption Works: One-Way Functions',
   'Modern encryption relies on mathematical operations that are easy to perform in one direction but computationally infeasible to reverse. Multiplying two large primes takes microseconds; factoring their product takes longer than the age of the universe.',
   'RSA encryption (Rivest, Shamir, Adleman, 1977) uses the difficulty of integer factorization: two large primes (each 600+ digits) are multiplied to create a public key; anyone can encrypt with it, but only the holder of the original primes can decrypt. The same asymmetry underpins elliptic curve cryptography (used in modern HTTPS) and Diffie-Hellman key exchange (which allows two parties to create a shared secret over a public channel). Quantum computers threaten RSA specifically: Shor''s algorithm can factor large numbers efficiently on a quantum computer. The cryptographic community has spent a decade developing "post-quantum cryptography" — algorithms based on mathematical problems quantum computers cannot efficiently solve — and NIST standardized the first post-quantum algorithms in 2024.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('77777777-0007-0012-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'Dark Patterns: UX Designed to Manipulate',
   'Dark patterns are user interface design choices that deliberately mislead, confuse, or pressure users into actions they didn''t intend — subscribing to services, sharing data, or making purchases. They are pervasive and largely legal.',
   'UX researcher Harry Brignull coined the term in 2010 and catalogued dozens of types: "roach motels" (easy to enter, hard to leave — like subscription cancellation flows), "confirmshaming" (opt-out buttons labeled "No thanks, I don''t want to save money"), "misdirection" (visual design drawing attention away from important information), and "hidden costs" (charges appearing only at checkout). A Princeton study of 11,000 shopping websites found dark patterns on 11% — with major retailers the worst offenders. The EU''s Digital Services Act (2022) and US FTC enforcement actions have begun treating dark patterns as deceptive trade practices. The underlying dynamic: designers who work for metrics (conversion rates, opt-in percentages) are rewarded for dark patterns regardless of user harm.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('77777777-0007-0013-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'Fitts''s Law: The Physics of Clicking',
   'Fitts''s Law predicts how long it takes to point at a target: time increases with distance and decreases with target size. It is one of the most empirically validated principles in human-computer interaction.',
   'Paul Fitts formulated the law in 1954 for human motor movement; it was adapted to computer interfaces in the 1970s. The mathematical formulation: MT = a + b × log₂(2D/W), where MT is movement time, D is distance, and W is target width. Practical implications: the corners and edges of screens are infinitely large targets (the cursor stops there automatically), which is why macOS puts the menu bar at the screen edge rather than inside application windows. Buttons should be as large as practical, especially for touch interfaces. The close button for a modal dialog should be positioned predictably and sized generously. Fitts''s Law is routinely used to evaluate interface designs and explains why smartphone touchscreens struggled with keyboard accuracy before designers understood finger-sized targets.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('77777777-0007-0014-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'Why New Programming Languages Keep Being Invented',
   'There are over 700 programming languages in active use. New ones keep appearing not because old ones are broken but because languages embody tradeoffs, and different problems have different optimal tradeoffs.',
   'A programming language is a set of abstractions over hardware: it chooses what to make easy (and thus implicit) and what to make explicit. C makes memory management explicit and gives hardware-level control — optimal for operating systems. Python makes type declarations implicit and prioritizes readability — optimal for data science and prototyping. Rust makes memory safety explicit at compile time, eliminating entire categories of bugs — optimal for systems programming where C''s safety issues are costly. New problem domains create new tradeoff requirements: the web needed JavaScript (interactive browsers), data science needed Python''s scientific ecosystem, distributed systems needed Go''s concurrency primitives. Language invention reflects genuine technical evolution, not just novelty-seeking.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('77777777-0007-0015-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'The Internet''s Physical Infrastructure',
   'The internet is physically real: 400+ submarine fiber-optic cables carry 99% of international data traffic. These cables — laid on the ocean floor — are the literal backbone of global communication and are surprisingly vulnerable.',
   'A single submarine cable can carry over 200 terabits per second — enough for millions of simultaneous HD video streams. The cables are typically the diameter of a garden hose, armored near shore (where anchors and fishing trawls are a risk) and unarmored in deep water. Around 100–150 cable faults occur annually; most are from ship anchors and fishing equipment. In 2022, Tonga lost nearly all internet connectivity when a submarine volcanic eruption severed its cable — a reminder that entire nations depend on single fiber strands. Cable laying ships — among the world''s most specialized vessels — are operated by a small number of companies, creating strategic dependencies that governments are increasingly treating as critical infrastructure.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('77777777-0007-0016-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'Moore''s Law Is Over But Continues in New Forms',
   'Moore''s original prediction — transistor density doubling every two years — has effectively ended at the physical limit of silicon. But the broader phenomenon of computing doubling continues through 3D stacking, specialization, and new architectures.',
   'Intel''s 2nm process (2024) places transistors roughly 10 silicon atoms wide — within a few generations of atomic limits. The industry has responded with multiple strategies: 3D chip stacking (TSMC and Samsung now stack multiple die layers, effectively multiplying density vertically), domain-specific chips (GPUs for graphics, TPUs for machine learning, NPUs for neural networks — each dramatically outperforming general CPUs at their specific task), and chiplets (disaggregating a chip into smaller, specialized units connected at high speed). The practical result is that computing continues improving at rates comparable to the original Moore''s Law era, but the mechanism has diversified from a single scaling trajectory into a portfolio of simultaneous approaches.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('77777777-0007-0017-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'How GPS Works',
   'GPS uses a constellation of 31 satellites, each broadcasting a precise atomic-clock timestamp. A GPS receiver calculates its position by measuring the time delay from four satellites — three for position, one to calibrate the receiver''s imprecise clock.',
   'Each GPS satellite broadcasts its precise location and the exact time of transmission. The receiver measures how long each signal took to arrive — since the signal travels at the speed of light, delay × c = distance. Four satellites overdetermine the position (three would suffice if your clock were perfect), using the fourth to solve for the receiver clock error. The system requires extraordinary atomic clock precision: a 1-microsecond error in timing produces 300 meters of position error. GPS satellites carry cesium and rubidium atomic clocks accurate to ~20 nanoseconds. Civilian GPS accuracy (4–10 meters) is limited by intentional signal degradation (removed in 2000), atmospheric delays, and multipath reflections. Differential GPS and RTK systems achieve centimeter accuracy for surveying and autonomous vehicles.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('77777777-0007-0018-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'The Password Is a Bad Security Model',
   'Passwords are the primary authentication mechanism for most systems despite being fundamentally flawed: humans choose predictable ones, reuse them, and can be tricked or stolen. The security industry has known this for 60 years and has largely failed to replace them.',
   'Fernando Corbató implemented the first computer passwords at MIT in 1961 for time-sharing systems, and immediately recognized the problem: users would write them down. The issues are structural: a strong password is unmemorable; a memorable password is guessable; using the same password everywhere means one breach compromises everything; security questions are often publicly findable information. Have I Been Pwned documents over 12 billion compromised credentials. Despite decades of alternatives (biometrics, tokens, certificates), passwords persist due to ubiquity and zero-cost deployment. Passkeys (FIDO2 standard), which authenticate using cryptographic key pairs stored on your device rather than shared secrets, represent the most credible replacement — but require coordinated ecosystem adoption that is slowly happening.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('77777777-0007-0019-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'How Recommendation Algorithms Create Filter Bubbles',
   'Recommendation algorithms optimize for engagement — maximizing clicks, watch time, and returns. Content that makes users angry, scared, or outraged generates more engagement than calm content, systematically surfacing extreme viewpoints.',
   'YouTube''s recommendation system (powering 70% of watch time) was discovered by researchers to systematically recommend progressively more extreme content — not due to political agenda but because extreme content generates longer watch sessions and more return visits. The algorithm was "learning" to keep users engaged, and outrage proved more engaging than moderation. Internal Facebook research (2019) found its algorithm amplified divisive political content because it generated more reactions and comments. However, subsequent academic work by Guess, Nyhan, and colleagues found that removing algorithmic feeds reduced political news consumption more broadly, not just extreme content — complicating simple narratives. The core problem is that engagement maximization is not equivalent to user wellbeing maximization, and the two can diverge severely.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('77777777-0007-0020-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'The Cold Start Problem in Tech Platforms',
   'Two-sided platforms — marketplaces, social networks, app stores — have no value for either side until both sides are present. Getting the first users is the "cold start problem," and solving it often determines which platforms succeed.',
   'A marketplace with no sellers has no buyers; no buyers means no sellers. OpenTable (restaurant reservations) spent years manually entering menus and managing restaurant schedules to build supply before users existed. Uber launched city by city, subsidizing both drivers and riders with venture capital to build density. WhatsApp piggybacked on phone contact lists to provide immediate utility without requiring network building. Reddit''s founders created fake accounts to populate threads until real users arrived. Andrew Chen''s "The Cold Start Problem" (2021) documents that successful platforms typically solve this through: atomic networks (starting with small, tight communities that have standalone value), killer features for early adopters, and geographic or demographic concentration before expansion.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('77777777-0007-0021-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'Planned Obsolescence in Consumer Electronics',
   'Planned obsolescence — designing products to fail or become obsolete on a schedule — is both an engineering reality and a contested business strategy. In consumer electronics, software obsolescence has largely replaced hardware failure as the primary mechanism.',
   'Hardware planned obsolescence (Phoebus cartel''s 1924 agreement to limit lightbulb lifespan to 1,000 hours) has been documented and outlawed. Modern electronics obsolescence is more subtle: software updates that degrade older hardware (Apple''s 2017 iOS controversy), parts unavailability, battery non-replaceability, and software end-of-life that leaves hardware functional but insecure. Right-to-repair movements (and legislation in EU, UK, and several US states) push back against manufacturer practices that restrict repair. The sustainability implications are severe: the global e-waste stream is the fastest-growing waste category, with 53 million tonnes annually and low recovery rates for the valuable and toxic materials inside.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('77777777-0007-0022-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'How Blockchains Work Without Trusting Anyone',
   'A blockchain is a distributed ledger maintained by thousands of independent computers that collectively enforce rules through cryptography — eliminating the need for a trusted central authority to validate transactions.',
   'Each block in a blockchain contains a hash of the previous block, transaction data, and a nonce. The hash links blocks together; altering any historical transaction would change its block''s hash, breaking the chain from that point forward — and the alteration would be immediately detectable by all other nodes. In Bitcoin''s proof-of-work system, adding a new block requires solving a computationally expensive puzzle, making history rewriting extremely costly. The "consensus mechanism" (proof-of-work, proof-of-stake, etc.) determines how the network agrees on the canonical chain when different nodes see different transactions. The system''s key property: no individual node needs to be trusted because the rules are mathematically enforced and all nodes can verify all transactions. The tradeoff is efficiency — Bitcoin uses more electricity than many countries to process fewer transactions per second than a single credit card company.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('77777777-0007-0023-0007-000000000007', 'technology', 'The History of the Computer Mouse',
   'Douglas Engelbart invented the computer mouse in 1963 at the Stanford Research Institute as part of his vision for "augmenting human intellect" through computers. His 1968 "Mother of All Demos" showed the mouse, hypertext, video conferencing, and collaborative editing — simultaneously.',
   'Engelbart''s original mouse was a wooden block with two perpendicular wheels on the bottom, tracking movement by wheel rotation. He called it a mouse because the cord resembled a tail. Xerox PARC refined the design in the 1970s, replacing wheels with a rolling ball. Apple licensed and commercialized the design for the Lisa (1983) and Macintosh (1984), bringing it to mass market. Microsoft introduced the optical mouse in 1999, eliminating the mechanical ball. Engelbart received the National Medal of Technology in 2000 and the Turing Award in 1997, but earned almost nothing from his invention — his SRI patent expired before the commercial mouse market emerged. His 1968 demo remains the most consequential technology demonstration in history.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Art ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('88888888-0008-0004-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'The Oldest Cave Art Is 45,500 Years Old',
   'The world''s oldest known figurative cave art — a painting of a Sulawesi warty pig — was found in a cave in Sulawesi, Indonesia, and dated to at least 45,500 years ago. It predates European cave art by over 10,000 years.',
   'The discovery, published in Science Advances in 2021 by Maxime Aubert and colleagues, used uranium-series dating of calcium carbonate crusts overlying the pigment. The Sulawesi paintings challenge the long-held assumption that cave art originated in Europe. Similarly aged handprint stencils in Sulawesi caves suggest symbolic and representational art may have been brought out of Africa by anatomically modern humans 60,000–70,000 years ago as a pre-existing cognitive capacity. The oldest European cave art (Chauvet Cave, France) dates to approximately 36,000 years ago. The Sulawesi finds have profoundly shifted the archaeology of art and cognition.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('88888888-0008-0005-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Why Most Art Throughout History Is Lost',
   'The vast majority of art ever created — paintings, sculptures, performances, textiles, music — has been destroyed by time, war, fire, deliberate iconoclasm, and simple neglect. What survives is a tiny, unrepresentative sample.',
   'Bronze statues were systematically melted for reuse; wooden panels rotted; papyrus manuscripts crumbled; frescos were plastered over; libraries burned. The Library of Alexandria''s destruction was less a singular catastrophe than a gradual decline over centuries, but it symbolizes the structural fragility of cultural preservation. We have roughly 10% of ancient Greek literature (the works deemed worthy of repeated copying); Shakespeare''s works survived partly by accident — half his plays weren''t published in his lifetime and survive only because two actors compiled them posthumously. Digital preservation faces new fragility: obsolete file formats, failed hard drives, and platform shutdowns mean contemporary digital art is also precarious. Every surviving artwork is a survivor of a vast, ongoing extinction.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('88888888-0008-0006-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Negative Space',
   'Negative space is the space around and between subjects in an artwork — the area that is not the "subject." Skillful use of negative space can be as powerful as the subject itself, defining shape, creating balance, and directing the eye.',
   'The concept appears across art forms: in FedEx''s logo, the negative space between E and x forms an arrow visible once noticed and impossible to unsee. Japanese ink painting traditions (sumi-e) deliberately leave vast areas empty, using the blank paper as active compositional element. M.C. Escher built entire works from the interplay of positive and negative space, where the "background" continuously transforms into foreground figures. In sculpture, Henry Moore''s pierced forms make holes compositionally central rather than incidental. Design disciplines have largely adopted the term "white space" — the recognition that emptiness is not wasted space but an active element of visual composition.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('88888888-0008-0007-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'The Mona Lisa Was Stolen and Missing for Two Years',
   'The Mona Lisa was stolen from the Louvre on August 21, 1911, by Vincenzo Peruggia, an Italian handyman who had worked in the museum. It was recovered in Florence in December 1913 — and the theft made it the world''s most famous painting.',
   'Peruggia, who believed the painting had been taken from Italy by Napoleon (incorrectly — it was acquired legally by Francis I in 1518), hid in a closet overnight and walked out with the painting under his coat. The theft was not discovered until the next day. During the two-year absence, thousands of people came to the Louvre to stare at the blank wall. The international sensation of the theft — reported worldwide, with suspects including Picasso — made the Mona Lisa a global icon. Before 1911, it was considered a minor Leonardo; it was the theft that transformed it into the world''s most recognized artwork. The painting was recovered when Peruggia tried to sell it to an art dealer in Florence.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('88888888-0008-0008-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'How Beauty Standards Are Historically Constructed',
   'Beauty standards are not universal or timeless — they change dramatically across cultures and centuries, reflecting shifting ideals of health, fertility, status, and social meaning rather than objective aesthetic qualities.',
   'Rubens''s 17th-century nudes celebrated full-figured bodies as a sign of wealth and health in an era when food security was uncertain for most. The thin ideal in Western culture emerged in the early 20th century with fashion industrialization and media imagery. Foot binding in imperial China (10th–20th century) was considered highly attractive; its eradication required sustained public health campaigns. Teeth blackening was fashionable across East and Southeast Asia for centuries. Skin-lightening products generate billions of dollars in revenue across Africa, Asia, and Latin America — a direct product of colonial beauty hierarchies. Naomi Wolf''s "The Beauty Myth" (1990) argued that beauty standards function as social control mechanisms, shifting the terms of compliance as women gain other forms of power.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('88888888-0008-0009-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Sfumato: Leonardo''s Smoke Technique',
   'Sfumato — Italian for "evaporated" or "smoky" — is a painting technique developed by Leonardo da Vinci that blends edges and transitions so subtly that no defined boundary between areas can be detected by the eye.',
   'Leonardo achieved sfumato by applying dozens of translucent paint layers (glazes), each so thin that individual layers are invisible — the technique is called "velaturas." X-ray and infrared analysis of the Mona Lisa revealed up to 30 distinct glaze layers in some areas, each only a few micrometers thick. The effect creates soft, atmospheric transitions that give Leonardo''s subjects their characteristic sense of being enveloped in light rather than lit from a specific source. The technique was so labor-intensive and subtle that no contemporary successfully replicated it. Modern technical analysis confirmed in 2010 that some Mona Lisa layers are only 2 micrometers thick — thinner than a human hair — requiring application with the fingertip rather than any brush.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('88888888-0008-0010-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Blue Was Rare and Expensive in Ancient Art',
   'Before the 19th century, blue pigment was among the rarest and most expensive colors in art — more valuable by weight than gold in medieval Europe. Artists rationed it carefully, reserving it for the most important figures.',
   'The only stable blue pigment available in Europe for centuries was lapis lazuli, a semiprecious stone mined exclusively in Afghanistan and traded across the ancient world. Grinding lapis lazuli into ultramarine blue was labor-intensive; the pigment cost more than gold. Artists reserved it for the Virgin Mary''s robes — its expense was itself a devotional statement. The ancient Egyptians developed "Egyptian blue" (copper calcium silicate) — one of the first synthetic pigments — around 3000 BCE, but the formula was lost in the early medieval period. Prussian blue (1704) was the first modern synthetic blue; synthetic ultramarine followed in 1826. The sudden affordability of blue transformed painting — look at the explosion of blue in landscapes and interiors throughout the 19th century.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('88888888-0008-0011-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Art Forgery Detection: Science vs. Fakes',
   'Art forgery detection is a cat-and-mouse game between forgers and scientists. Technical analysis — radiocarbon dating, X-ray fluorescence, infrared reflectography, pigment analysis — can expose fakes that fool the human eye.',
   'Han van Meegeren, who sold fake Vermeers to Hermann Göring during World War II, was caught when post-war investigators noticed the "newly discovered" Vermeers'' stylistic clusters. Elmyr de Hory forged hundreds of Matisses, Picassos, and Modiglianis that fooled museums for decades — his story inspired Clifford Irving''s "Fake!" (1969). Technical analysis has since become routine: X-ray fluorescence identifies pigments by their elemental composition (zinc white, available after 1850, in a "Rembrandt" is a giveaway); radiocarbon dating of canvas and wood; infrared reflectography revealing underdrawings inconsistent with the claimed period. The most sophisticated forgers now study and replicate historical techniques meticulously, and some have been convicted only on documentary evidence when technical analysis was inconclusive.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('88888888-0008-0012-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Memento Mori',
   'Memento mori (Latin: "remember you must die") is an artistic tradition that incorporates reminders of mortality — skulls, hourglasses, wilting flowers — into works of art to prompt reflection on life''s brevity and what matters.',
   'The tradition extends from ancient Roman triumphs (a slave whispering "memento mori" to the victorious general) through medieval dance macabre paintings, Dutch Golden Age vanitas still life (skulls beside luxuries), Victorian mourning jewelry, and contemporary artists like Damien Hirst. Philippe Ariès''s "The Hour of Our Death" traced how Western attitudes toward death shifted from familiarity and public display (medieval period) to denial and medicalization (20th century). Contemporary memento mori art — from Andy Warhol''s Death and Disaster series to Hirst''s diamond-encrusted skull "For the Love of God" — interrogates commodity culture''s relationship to mortality. The philosophy behind it: awareness of death focuses attention on the quality and meaning of life.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('88888888-0008-0013-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'How Photography Changed Painting',
   'The invention of photography (1839) threatened to make realistic painting obsolete — and instead liberated painting from its documentary function, directly enabling Impressionism, Post-Impressionism, and the entire trajectory of modern art.',
   'Freed from documentation, painters began pursuing what the camera could not capture: movement, subjective light, emotion, and the painter''s own perceptual act. Monet''s serial paintings of haystacks and Rouen Cathedral at different times of day were explicitly exploring the camera''s blind spot — treating the same scene at dawn and dusk as different subjects. Cézanne abandoned the single fixed viewpoint entirely, painting multiple simultaneous perspectives into one canvas. Muybridge''s motion photography (1878) had an ironic corrective effect: it revealed that painters had been depicting galloping horses incorrectly for centuries, and painters quickly adopted accurate motion. Photography also changed the painter''s relationship to source material — working from photographs became possible, enabling Degas''s intimate, seemingly candid compositions that would have been impossible to hold as live poses. The camera did not kill painting; it changed its job description.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('88888888-0008-0014-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'The Medici as the First Arts Patrons',
   'The Medici banking dynasty''s investment in Renaissance art was not purely aesthetic — it was strategic, converting financial wealth (socially suspect under Church usury prohibitions) into cultural capital and political legitimacy through visible patronage of churches and public works.',
   'The Church condemned usury (charging interest), which created a specific problem for bankers: great wealth and social stigma simultaneously. Cosimo de'' Medici''s solution was systematic: fund the dome of Florence''s Cathedral, commission Ghiberti''s Baptistery doors, support Donatello — embedding Medici imagery in civic religion and buying theological legitimacy through visible piety. Lorenzo de'' Medici extended this to Botticelli, Leonardo, and the teenage Michelangelo, transforming Florence into Europe''s cultural capital and the Medici name into a byword for civilization rather than lending. Richard Goldthwaite''s economic history of Renaissance patronage shows the art market was a sophisticated system of competitive contracts and reputation management, not spontaneous cultural flowering. The model is recognizable today: tech billionaire philanthropy follows the same logic of converting financial capital into cultural and civic legitimacy.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('88888888-0008-0015-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Iconoclasm: Destroying Images as Political Act',
   'Iconoclasm — the deliberate destruction of images, statues, or religious icons — has been a recurring political act throughout history, from Byzantine religious disputes to the Taliban''s destruction of the Bamiyan Buddhas in 2001.',
   'Byzantine iconoclasm (726–843 CE) was a theological dispute: were icons legitimate aids to devotion or sinful idol worship? Emperor Leo III ordered icon destruction; the controversy split the Church and contributed to the eventual East-West Schism. Protestant Reformers destroyed Catholic statues and stained glass throughout Northern Europe in the 16th century as attacks on idolatry. Colonial missionaries destroyed Indigenous sacred objects to break cultural identity. Totalitarian regimes systematically remove images of deposed leaders (Stalin-era "airbrushing" of purged officials from photographs). Contemporary statue-toppling (Confederate monuments, colonial figures) participates in this ancient tradition. Art historian David Freedberg argues iconoclasm''s violence proves that images hold genuine power — we don''t destroy things we believe are merely inert.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('88888888-0008-0016-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Color Psychology in Advertising',
   'Colors carry consistent emotional and associative meanings that advertisers exploit — blue signals trust and professionalism, red urgency and appetite, green health and sustainability, yellow optimism. These associations are partly universal, partly cultural.',
   'Blue is the most commonly used color for corporate logos globally — IBM, Facebook, Samsung, Ford, HP — reflecting research showing blue signals reliability and competence. Red creates urgency (sale tags, stop signs) and increases appetite (McDonald''s, Coca-Cola, KFC) — possibly linked to the color of ripe fruit. A 2006 study by researchers Andrew Elliot and Markus Maier found that seeing red before an exam impaired performance, while blue improved it on creative tasks. Green dominates environmental and health branding. However, cultural variation is significant: white is funerary in China and India; green is associated with death in some Latin American contexts; purple signals royalty in Europe but mourning in Thailand.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('88888888-0008-0017-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'The Sublime vs. the Beautiful',
   'Edmund Burke (1757) and Immanuel Kant (1790) both distinguished the beautiful (orderly, pleasing, finite) from the sublime (vast, overwhelming, potentially terrifying). The distinction transformed how Western culture thought about nature, landscape, and art''s highest purpose.',
   'Burke''s "Philosophical Enquiry" identified the sublime with darkness, vastness, power, and obscurity — things that produce astonishment rather than pleasure. A calm sea is beautiful; a raging sea is sublime. Kant refined this: the mathematical sublime (vast quantities overwhelming imagination) and the dynamical sublime (power exceeding physical capacity) both initially produce a sense of inadequacy — but this inadequacy triggers an awareness of our rational capacity that exceeds nature, producing a morally elevating experience. Romantic landscape painters (Turner, Friedrich) explicitly pursued the sublime: mountains, shipwrecks, storms. The category influenced everything from 19th-century landscape photography to blockbuster films, which regularly deploy the sublime through scale and power.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('88888888-0008-0018-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Street Art and the Legitimacy Debate',
   'Street art occupies a contested position between vandalism and fine art. Works created illegally in public spaces have been acquired by major museums, sold at auction for millions, and celebrated by the same institutions that prosecute their creators.',
   'Banksy''s work commands millions at Sotheby''s while the artist remains anonymous specifically to maintain the subversive charge of illegality. When his "Devolved Parliament" sold for £9.9 million in 2019, it was both celebrated as great art and remained legally the product of criminal acts in many of the buildings where he''d painted it. Jean-Michel Basquiat moved from illegal subway graffiti to gallery representation within four years, dying at 27 as one of the most sought-after painters of his generation. The legitimacy debate reveals the art world''s gatekeeping function: the same act (painting a public wall) is vandalism or art depending entirely on whether institutional validation is granted. Ai Weiwei, Shepard Fairey, and Os Gemeos navigate the same tension as careers rather than one-off provocations.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('88888888-0008-0019-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'How the Art Auction System Works',
   'Major auction houses (Christie''s, Sotheby''s, Phillips) earn revenue from buyer''s premium and seller''s commission — but the real mechanics involve guarantee deals, third-party bids, and chandelier bidding that obscure where market prices actually come from.',
   'A "guaranteed" consignment means the auction house promises the seller a minimum price regardless of the auction result — the house takes the risk of the work not selling. Guarantees are sometimes backed by third parties (typically dealers or collectors) who receive a fee for providing the guarantee and get to buy the work at the guarantee price if no one bids higher. "Chandelier bids" are fictitious bids from the auctioneer to build momentum — legal in most jurisdictions under auction law. The buyer''s premium (typically 15–26% of hammer price) is the primary revenue source. The system creates apparent price discovery through competitive bidding while the actual dynamics involve sophisticated pre-auction negotiations invisible to most bidders. The total art market is estimated at $65 billion annually, with auction representing roughly a third.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('88888888-0008-0020-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Duchamp''s Urinal and the Role of Context',
   'Marcel Duchamp''s 1917 "Fountain" — a factory-produced urinal submitted to an art exhibition under a pseudonym — argued that art is constituted by context and institutional framing, not by craft, skill, or the object''s physical properties.',
   'Duchamp''s "readymades" (manufactured objects exhibited as art) were a philosophical provocation: what makes something art? His answer: an artist''s declaration, combined with institutional acceptance, suffices. The Fountain was rejected by the Society of Independent Artists (who had promised to show any submitted work); Duchamp, who was on the board, resigned in protest. A reproduction is displayed at the Centre Pompidou today; the original was lost. Arthur Danto''s "artworld" theory (1964) formalized Duchamp''s insight: art is constituted by its relationship to art history and institutional context. George Dickie''s Institutional Theory of Art builds on this: something is art if the artworld treats it as art. The implications are radical — they dissolve any essential difference between art and non-art based on physical properties.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('88888888-0008-0021-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'How Linear Perspective Was Invented',
   'Linear perspective — the technique of depicting three-dimensional space on a flat surface using converging lines — was mathematically formalized by Filippo Brunelleschi in Florence around 1413, transforming Western painting''s relationship to depicted space.',
   'Brunelleschi demonstrated perspective using a mirror and a painted panel of the Florence Baptistery, showing that a mathematical system could generate the illusion of depth on a flat surface. Leon Battista Alberti codified the system in "Della Pittura" (1435), making it teachable. The technique spread rapidly through Italian painting and into Northern Europe by the mid-15th century. Perspective is not "natural" seeing — it is a convention: the eye does not have a fixed point of view, and binocular vision creates depth differently. David Hockney and Charles Falco controversially argued (2001) that many Old Masters used optical devices (camera lucida, mirrors) to achieve perspective — triggering a still-active debate in art history.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('88888888-0008-0022-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Portraiture as Status Symbol',
   'For most of Western art history, having a painted portrait was an exclusive privilege of royalty, nobility, and wealthy merchants — a declaration of social significance that justified the enormous cost of commissioning a skilled artist.',
   'The earliest Western portraits (5th century BCE Greek coins, Egyptian Faiyum mummy portraits) established the convention of individualizing the human face for posterity. Hans Holbein''s portraits of Henry VIII and his court are simultaneously political documents: the king''s pose, costume, and physical presentation encode power claims. The Dutch Golden Age democratized portraiture to prosperous merchants — Rembrandt''s Amsterdam sitters were wealthy burghers, not aristocrats. Photography (1839) made portraiture accessible to the middle class; the price of recorded likeness collapsed from hundreds of guilders to shillings. Contemporary Instagram selfie culture represents a further democratization: the desire to project a curated self-image, once expressed through expensive commissions, now operates at planetary scale and no cost.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('88888888-0008-0023-0008-000000000008', 'art', 'Abstract Expressionism and the CIA',
   'During the Cold War, the CIA covertly funded international tours and exhibitions of Abstract Expressionist art (Pollock, de Kooning, Rothko) as cultural propaganda — positioning American freedom against Soviet socialist realism''s conformity.',
   'The Congress for Cultural Freedom, secretly funded by the CIA from 1950, organized concerts, publications, and art exhibitions promoting Western modernism internationally. The reasoning was explicit in internal documents: Abstract Expressionism''s individualism, emotional freedom, and formal experimentation embodied American values in contrast to the state-mandated figurative art of the Soviet bloc. The irony is multilayered: the US government was simultaneously using McCarthyite investigations to harass some of the same left-leaning artists whose work it was promoting abroad. Frances Stonor Saunders documented the program in "The Cultural Cold War" (1999). The artists themselves were largely unaware of CIA involvement. The episode raises genuine questions about whether the art''s meaning is contaminated by its deployment, or whether the CIA simply recognized genuine quality.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Psychology ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('99999999-0009-0004-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'The Bystander Effect Has Been Partly Debunked',
   'The classic bystander effect — people are less likely to help in emergencies when others are present — has been substantially revised. A 2019 analysis of real CCTV footage found bystanders intervene in 90% of real-world conflicts.',
   'Latané and Darley''s 1968 research (inspired by the Kitty Genovese case) established the bystander effect in laboratory settings: participants were less likely to report a smoke-filled room or assist a falling person when they believed others were present. But Philpot et al.''s 2019 study of 219 real violent incidents across Amsterdam, Cape Town, and Lancaster found intervention in 90.8% of cases — increasing with the number of bystanders. The laboratory effect appears real but weaker in naturalistic settings, particularly when the emergency is clear, bystanders can communicate, and there is shared group identity. The Kitty Genovese case itself was substantially misreported — there were far fewer witnesses, most saw less than claimed, and several did call the police.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('99999999-0009-0005-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'False Confessions: Why Innocent People Confess',
   'An estimated 25–30% of DNA exoneration cases involve false confessions — people who confessed to crimes they did not commit. The mechanisms are well-understood and involve psychological manipulation, not just torture.',
   'The Reid Technique, used by US police for decades, involves confrontational interrogation designed to produce behavioral signs of guilt (whether real or not), followed by minimization (suggesting the crime was understandable) and maximization (exaggerating the consequences of denial). Sleep deprivation, prolonged isolation, and false evidence claims ("we have your DNA") are documented in many false confession cases. Saul Kassin''s research identifies three types: voluntary (false belief in guilt), coerced-compliant (knows they''re innocent but believes confessing is optimal), and coerced-internalized (actually comes to believe in their guilt after interrogation). Juveniles, people with intellectual disabilities, and those with high suggestibility are especially vulnerable. The Innocence Project now trains law enforcement in less coercive approaches.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('99999999-0009-0006-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Anchoring Bias',
   'The anchoring bias occurs when people rely too heavily on the first piece of information encountered (the "anchor") when making estimates or decisions. Arbitrary numbers significantly shift estimates even when people know the anchor is irrelevant.',
   'Tversky and Kahneman''s foundational experiment (1974): participants watched a wheel spin to 65 or 10, then estimated what percentage of African countries were in the UN. Those who saw 65 guessed 45% on average; those who saw 10 guessed 25% — despite the spin being random and irrelevant. Anchoring is pervasive in negotiation (first offer anchors the range), legal sentencing (higher numeric sentencing recommendations produce higher sentences), and consumer pricing ($999 anchors against $1,000 as a bargain). Experts are less susceptible but not immune — real estate agents, judges, and physicians all show anchoring effects. The most effective de-anchoring strategy is generating your own estimate before encountering any external anchor.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('99999999-0009-0007-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Milgram''s Experiments Had Serious Flaws',
   'Stanley Milgram''s famous obedience experiments (1961) — showing ordinary Americans would administer apparently lethal shocks on orders from an authority figure — are more methodologically flawed than their popular retelling suggests.',
   'Milgram found that 65% of participants administered the maximum 450-volt shock. But Gina Perry''s archival research and Nestar Russell''s analysis found significant problems: many participants had serious doubts about the shocks'' reality (the actor screaming wasn''t convincing to everyone), Milgram''s prods were more varied and coercive than his published accounts suggested (including direct threats), and participants were debriefed differently in different conditions affecting reported outcomes. The finding that obedience was high is probably real, but the clean 65% figure and the implication that compliance was universal under standard conditions is significantly overstated. The experiments also cannot be replicated today under modern ethics protocols, which themselves partly reflect their legacy.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('99999999-0009-0008-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'The Habit Loop',
   'Habits form through a neurological loop: cue → routine → reward. Once established, the cue automatically triggers the routine without conscious deliberation. Breaking habits requires interrupting this loop, not just willpower.',
   'Charles Duhigg''s "The Power of Habit" (2012) popularized Ann Graybiel''s neuroscience research showing that habitual behaviors are managed by the basal ganglia rather than the prefrontal cortex. Once a habit is "chunked" in the basal ganglia, it requires minimal cognitive resources and resists conscious override. The implication: you cannot simply "stop" a habit through willpower — the routine continues to fire whenever the cue appears. Effective habit change requires keeping the cue and reward while substituting a new routine, or radically changing the environment to eliminate cues. James Clear''s "Atomic Habits" extends this with implementation intentions: if-then plans that specify exactly when, where, and how a new routine will be performed — dramatically increasing follow-through rates.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('99999999-0009-0009-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Learned Helplessness',
   'Learned helplessness occurs when repeated exposure to uncontrollable events causes organisms to stop trying to escape or avoid aversive situations — even when escape later becomes possible. It is a model for clinical depression.',
   'Martin Seligman and Steven Maier''s 1967 experiments exposed dogs to inescapable shocks in a shuttle box. When later placed in a situation where they could escape by jumping a barrier, the previously helpless dogs simply lay down and whimpered — even though escape was easy. Dogs without prior helplessness training immediately jumped the barrier. Seligman applied this model to human depression: repeated experiences of uncontrollability produce passivity, negative cognition, and motivational deficits that persist even when circumstances change. The cognitive reformulation (Abramson et al., 1978) added attributional style: helplessness is most severe when failure is attributed to internal, stable, global causes ("I''m always bad at everything"). Cognitive behavioral therapy targets these attributional patterns directly.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('99999999-0009-0010-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'The Stanford Prison Experiment Was Largely Fabricated',
   'Philip Zimbardo''s 1971 Stanford Prison Experiment — where college students playing guards became abusive within days — is among the most cited studies in psychology. It was also substantially fabricated and methodologically disastrous.',
   'Journalist Ben Blum''s 2018 investigation and recordings of Zimbardo coaching "guards" to act aggressively reveal that the experiment''s results were partly manufactured. Zimbardo himself played the role of prison superintendent, actively encouraging abuse rather than observing it neutrally. Participants who broke the experiment''s rules by resisting powerfully were excluded from analysis. The guard who most became the experiment''s symbol of brutality later said he was consciously roleplaying a character, not losing himself to the situation. Despite these methodological catastrophes, the study remains in virtually every introductory psychology textbook as evidence of situational determinism. A 2018 French documentary presented recordings that fundamentally undermined Zimbardo''s account.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('99999999-0009-0011-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Flow State: Optimal Experience',
   'Flow — Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi''s term for the state of complete absorption in a challenging activity — is characterized by effortless concentration, loss of self-consciousness, distorted time sense, and intrinsic reward.',
   'Csikszentmihalyi identified flow through interviews with chess grandmasters, surgeons, rock climbers, and factory workers who described identical phenomenology across radically different activities. The conditions for flow require a challenge-skill balance: too easy produces boredom; too difficult produces anxiety; the narrow band where challenge slightly exceeds skill but the person has tools to meet it produces flow. Neuroimaging during flow shows reduced activity in the default mode network (the "inner critic") and prefrontal cortex (effortful deliberation), with heightened connectivity between task-relevant regions. Flow is intrinsically motivating — the activity becomes its own reward. Csikszentmihalyi proposed that designing lives for regular flow experiences is a more reliable path to wellbeing than pursuing happiness directly.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('99999999-0009-0012-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Priming: Context Shapes Behavior',
   'Priming is the phenomenon where exposure to one stimulus influences responses to later stimuli — often outside conscious awareness. Being primed with elderly-related words causes people to walk more slowly; being primed with money makes people more selfish.',
   'John Bargh''s "Florida effect" study (1996) showed that participants primed with words associated with the elderly (Florida, bingo, retirement) walked more slowly to the elevator afterward. This and many related priming studies faced replication problems in the early 2010s — the replication crisis hit social priming especially hard. Some effects (conceptual priming, semantic priming) replicate robustly; behaviorally dramatic effects (walking slower, etc.) replicate poorly. The revised picture: priming on conceptual and perceptual tasks is real and reliable; the more dramatic behavioral priming effects are small, context-dependent, and require specific conditions to appear. The concept remains important but the dramatic claims require substantial qualification.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('99999999-0009-0013-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Social Proof: Using Others to Decide',
   'Social proof is the tendency to use others'' choices as information about the correct behavior in ambiguous situations. It explains why restaurants post "most popular" items, why Amazon displays review counts, and why laugh tracks work.',
   'The evolutionary logic is simple: in a world where gathering information is costly, others'' behavior provides free signals about what is safe, desirable, or correct. The effect scales with similarity — we weight behavior from people like us most heavily, which is why "most popular among people in your city" outperforms generic popularity counts. Asch''s conformity experiments (1951) showed participants giving obviously wrong answers to simple perceptual questions when surrounded by confederates giving wrong answers — not from stupidity but from treating social consensus as evidence. Cialdini''s field research showed that hotel towel reuse increased when signs said "most guests in this room reuse their towels" versus generic environmental appeals. Critically, negative social proof backfires: "Most people don''t file taxes on time" increases non-compliance. The correct framing is always the majority doing the right thing, never the majority doing the wrong one.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('99999999-0009-0014-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'The Mere Exposure Effect',
   'We tend to prefer things we have encountered before — merely because we have encountered them. Familiarity breeds preference, not contempt: repeated exposure to stimuli increases our liking for them, even without awareness of the prior exposure.',
   'Robert Zajonc documented the mere exposure effect in 1968, showing that exposure to nonsense words, Chinese characters, or photographs increased preference ratings — even when exposures were so brief (sub-millisecond) that participants had no conscious recognition of them. The effect is robust across cultures, species, and stimulus types. Advertisers use it deliberately: repetition creates preference through familiarity alone, independent of message content. Political campaign research shows that the volume of name recognition (regardless of valence) predicts electoral success — voters prefer the name they''ve heard more, other things equal. The effect has limits: at very high exposure levels, satiation can reverse it. The evolutionary hypothesis: in a world where novel stimuli are often dangerous, familiarity is a proxy for safety.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('99999999-0009-0015-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Emotional Contagion',
   'Emotions spread from person to person through unconscious mimicry and physiological synchrony — we automatically imitate others'' facial expressions, postures, and vocalizations, and this mimicry causes us to feel their emotions.',
   'Elaine Hatfield''s emotional contagion theory (1993) proposes a two-step mechanism: automatic unconscious mimicry of others'' emotional cues, followed by the resulting proprioceptive feedback producing the actual emotion. Experiments show that people asked to suppress facial expressions during an emotional interaction feel less empathy and are less able to accurately read others'' emotions. A 2014 Facebook experiment exposed ~700,000 users to news feeds with positive or negative emotional content manipulated without their knowledge: users showed measurable emotional contagion in their own subsequent posts. The experiment was deeply controversial — participants hadn''t consented to emotional manipulation — but the finding itself supported contagion effects operating through digital text without any nonverbal cues.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('99999999-0009-0016-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'The Curse of Knowledge',
   'The curse of knowledge is a cognitive bias where knowing something makes it hard to imagine not knowing it. Experts consistently underestimate how difficult their knowledge is to communicate because they cannot recover their pre-knowledge state.',
   'The effect was named by economists Colin Camerer, George Loewenstein, and Martin Weber in 1989. A famous illustration: "tappers and listeners" — one person taps a well-known song, listeners try to identify it. Tappers estimate listeners will identify it 50% of the time; actual success rate is 2.5%. Tappers "hear" the music in their heads; listeners hear only random taps. Teachers systematically underestimate how hard material is for students; engineers misjudge how difficult their products are to use; doctors misjudge how confusing medical explanations are to patients. The cure requires active perspective-taking — not just "thinking like a beginner" (impossible once you know something) but getting actual beginner feedback. User testing, beta readers, and pilot audiences exist specifically to counter the curse.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('99999999-0009-0017-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Implementation Intentions Beat Willpower',
   'If-then plans — "When X happens, I will do Y" — dramatically outperform vague intentions in producing behavioral follow-through. Specifying exactly when, where, and how you will act closes the gap between intention and action.',
   'Peter Gollwitzer developed implementation intention theory in the 1990s. Meta-analyses of 94 studies show implementation intentions increase goal achievement rates by 200–300% vs. simple goal-setting. The mechanism: if-then plans delegate the decision to the situation — the cue automatically triggers the behavior without conscious deliberation. "I will exercise three times a week" requires repeated willpower expenditure to decide when and how. "Every Monday, Wednesday, and Friday at 7am, I will put on my running shoes and run for 30 minutes from my front door" automates the decision. The behavior fires when the cue appears. BJ Fogg''s "Tiny Habits" extends this: anchor new behaviors to existing ones ("After I pour my morning coffee, I will do 10 push-ups"), creating a stable if-then structure within daily routine.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('99999999-0009-0018-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Choice Architecture',
   'Choice architecture is the design of how options are presented — which is default, how many options are offered, how they are ordered and framed. These design choices predictably and powerfully shape decisions without restricting any options.',
   'Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein''s "Nudge" (2008) documented how default options dramatically affect outcomes: switching organ donation from opt-in to opt-out raised donor rates in European countries from ~10% to ~90%. Cafeteria food placement studies (Brian Wansink, though many of his studies face replication issues) showed that simply moving healthy foods to more visible positions increased their selection. 401(k) auto-enrollment (defaulting employees into retirement savings plans) dramatically increased participation rates among low-income workers who would never actively sign up. The key insight: there is no neutral presentation of choices — every choice environment has a default that favors some options, and the designer of that environment holds real power regardless of whether they intend to.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('99999999-0009-0019-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Self-Serving Attribution Bias',
   'People systematically attribute successes to internal factors (ability, effort) and failures to external factors (luck, other people, circumstances). This self-serving bias protects self-esteem but distorts learning from experience.',
   'The self-serving attribution bias was documented in psychology by Dale Miller and Michael Ross in 1975. Students who passed an exam attributed their success to intelligence and hard work; those who failed attributed it to unfair questions or bad luck. Professional sports provide a near-perfect laboratory: athletes and coaches consistently take credit for wins and blame circumstances, opponents, or officiating for losses. The bias has an interpersonal correlate: the fundamental attribution error, where we attribute others'' behavior to their character while explaining our own through circumstances. The bias is adaptive — protecting self-esteem — but interferes with accurate feedback loops. High-performing organizations cultivate cultures where failure is attributed internally (what did we do wrong?) to enable genuine learning.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('99999999-0009-0020-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Terror Management Theory',
   'Terror Management Theory proposes that awareness of inevitable death is a fundamental source of human anxiety — and that much cultural behavior, from self-esteem maintenance to nationalism to religious belief, functions to manage this existential terror.',
   'Jeff Greenberg, Sheldon Solomon, and Tom Pyszczynski developed TMT based on Ernest Becker''s "The Denial of Death" (1973). The theory''s core claim: awareness of our mortality is so anxiety-provoking that we construct symbolic systems (culture, religion, nationalism, self-esteem) that promise either literal immortality (heaven) or symbolic immortality (living on through legacy, children, national causes). When death is made salient ("mortality salience"), people show increased defense of their cultural worldview and increased hostility to those who challenge it — a robust finding across hundreds of experiments in dozens of countries. TMT provides a psychological framework for understanding authoritarianism, xenophobia, and religious extremism as, in part, death-anxiety management responses.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('99999999-0009-0021-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'The Spotlight Effect',
   'People systematically overestimate how much others notice and evaluate them — a phenomenon called the spotlight effect. We feel as if a spotlight is following us, when most people are too preoccupied with their own spotlight to notice ours.',
   'Thomas Gilovich and colleagues demonstrated this with a simple experiment: participants wearing embarrassing T-shirts (Barry Manilow, to Cornell undergraduates) estimated that 46% of people in a room noticed their shirt; actual observers: 23%. The overestimation reflects our egocentric perspective — our shirt is central to our experience, so it feels central to everyone''s. The spotlight effect has practical implications for social anxiety: the feared judgment we imagine from stumbling over a presentation, wearing the wrong outfit, or making an awkward comment is dramatically overstated. Others are experiencing their own version of the spotlight and have limited attention for ours. Knowing this empirically doesn''t eliminate the feeling, but it can calibrate our behavioral responses to social mistakes.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('99999999-0009-0022-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'Grit vs. Talent: What Predicts Success',
   'Angela Duckworth''s research on grit — perseverance and passion for long-term goals — found that in certain high-achievement contexts, grit predicts success beyond IQ and talent. But the concept has been substantially contested since.',
   'Duckworth''s 2007 paper reported grit predicted retention at West Point, National Spelling Bee success, and teacher effectiveness better than IQ or talent measures. The finding attracted enormous popular attention because it seemed to democratize success — anyone could develop grit. Subsequent replication attempts produced mixed results: effect sizes for grit''s predictive power are modest once correlated traits (conscientiousness, self-control) are controlled for. Critics note the grit scale correlates heavily with conscientiousness — one of the Big Five personality traits with 50 years of research behind it. The most balanced conclusion: deliberate practice and persistence matter significantly for achievement; but framing this as a single trait called "grit" independent of talent, opportunity, and domain-specific learning is oversimplified.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('99999999-0009-0023-0009-000000000009', 'psychology', 'The Backfire Effect',
   'The backfire effect claimed that correcting false beliefs can entrench them — confronting someone with facts that contradict their worldview makes them believe their original claim more strongly. The effect has largely failed to replicate.',
   'Brendan Nyhan and Jason Reifler''s 2010 study reported the backfire effect across political topics: corrections increased belief in the original false information among people ideologically motivated to believe it. The paper was widely cited as explaining why correcting misinformation is futile. But Nyhan himself led subsequent replication attempts (2019) across 52 experiments and found no systematic backfire effects. Most people do update their factual beliefs when presented with accurate corrections — the updating is just less complete and less durable than we''d like. The related phenomenon that does robustly replicate is motivated reasoning: people apply different standards of evidence to claims that confirm vs. disconfirm their priors. Correction is not futile; it just faces headwinds from motivation-dependent information processing.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

