INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0004-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Voyager 1 Is Still Sending Data from 24 Billion km Away',
   'Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 is now over 24 billion kilometers from Earth — farther than any human-made object. Its signals, traveling at the speed of light, take over 22 hours to reach us. It still functions.',
   'Voyager 1 entered interstellar space in 2012, confirmed when scientists detected a sudden change in the plasma environment around the spacecraft. Its nuclear power source (a radioisotope thermoelectric generator) produces about 4 watts of electricity — less than a dim nightlight. At this power level, NASA can still receive data from its plasma wave, magnetic field, and cosmic ray instruments. Engineers communicate with it using the Deep Space Network''s 70-meter dish antennas, transmitting commands at 160 bits per second — slower than a 1970s modem. A 2023 anomaly caused garbled telemetry; NASA engineers took months to diagnose and fix the issue at a distance where each round-trip communication takes 45 hours.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0005-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Space Breaks the Human Body in Measurable Ways',
   'Microgravity causes bone density loss, muscle atrophy, fluid shifts toward the head, cardiovascular deconditioning, and vision impairment — all within weeks. Extended spaceflight poses serious medical challenges not yet fully solved.',
   'Astronauts lose 1–2% of bone density per month in microgravity — faster than severe osteoporosis on Earth. Without gravitational loading, bones remodel toward lower density; exercise mitigates but doesn''t prevent this. Fluid shifts cephalad (toward the head) increase intracranial pressure, causing the syndrome SANS (spaceflight-associated neuro-ocular syndrome) — flattening of the eyeball and vision changes seen in over 60% of long-duration crew members. The cardiovascular system, no longer working against gravity, deconditions rapidly; returning astronauts frequently faint when standing. Scott Kelly''s year on the ISS changed 7% of his gene expression compared to his twin Mark, with most changes reverting within months but some persisting. Mars missions (2.5 years) will require solving these problems.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0006-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Event Horizon: The Point of No Return',
   'A black hole''s event horizon is the boundary beyond which nothing — not even light — can escape. It is not a physical surface but a mathematical boundary in spacetime, defined by the escape velocity exceeding the speed of light.',
   'The Schwarzschild radius — the radius at which a mass becomes a black hole — equals 2GM/c² (G = gravitational constant, M = mass, c = speed of light). For Earth, this is about 9mm; for the Sun, about 3km. Inside the event horizon, all paths through spacetime — including the path of light — lead toward the central singularity. An observer falling through the event horizon would notice nothing special at that moment (no wall, no surface) but would be unable to transmit any signal to the outside universe. From an outside observer''s perspective, an infalling object appears to freeze and redden at the event horizon due to gravitational time dilation — never quite seeming to cross it. The first photograph of a black hole''s shadow (M87*, 2019) showed the event horizon''s silhouette through the Event Horizon Telescope.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0007-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Moon Dust Smells Like Gunpowder',
   'Apollo astronauts reported that lunar dust tracked inside the habitat smelled strongly of gunpowder — a surprising sensory detail from the Moon''s surface. The smell likely comes from highly reactive freshly-fractured mineral surfaces.',
   'The Moon has no atmosphere and no weather — its surface has been bombarded by cosmic rays and micrometeorites for billions of years, creating a fine, sharp-edged regolith. Each dust grain has a chemically reactive surface that would normally be oxidized in an atmosphere. When astronauts brought dust inside and it encountered oxygen and moisture, the reactive surfaces oxidized rapidly — producing chemical compounds similar to those in gun smoke (primarily from gunpowder''s nitrocellulose). The abrasive, razor-sharp nature of lunar dust was a serious engineering problem: it scratched helmet visors, infiltrated suit joints, clogged air filters, and was inhaled by astronauts. Lunar dust''s toxicity (silica nanoparticles) and abrasiveness are among the main challenges for future lunar habitation.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0008-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Why We Haven''t Returned to the Moon',
   'The US last landed humans on the Moon in 1972. The hiatus is not a capability problem — it is primarily economic and political. Maintaining Apollo-era infrastructure and political will proved more expensive than abandoning it.',
   'The Saturn V rocket was never restarted after 1973: the production lines were closed, the supply chain dissolved, and institutional knowledge dispersed. Rebuilding costs more than the original development. More fundamentally, Apollo was driven by Cold War competition with a defined political goal and massive sustained funding (4.4% of the federal budget at peak). Once the Moon landings achieved their geopolitical purpose, political will to continue collapsed. The Nixon administration cancelled the final three planned Apollo missions. Every subsequent program (Apollo Applications, Space Exploration Initiative, Constellation) was defunded before completion. NASA''s Artemis program, backed by sustained bipartisan funding since 2017 and using commercial rockets (SpaceX Falcon Heavy, SLS), represents the most credible return attempt — targeting 2026–2027 as of 2025.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0009-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Cosmic Inflation: Faster Than Light in the First Second',
   'In the first fraction of a second after the Big Bang, the universe underwent exponential expansion called inflation — expanding by a factor of at least 10²⁶ in roughly 10⁻³² seconds. This did not violate relativity because space itself was expanding.',
   'Alan Guth proposed cosmic inflation in 1980 to solve several cosmological puzzles: the horizon problem (regions too far apart to have ever been in contact have identical temperatures — possible only if they were once adjacent), the flatness problem (the universe''s geometry is precisely flat rather than curved), and the monopole problem (grand unified theories predict magnetic monopoles that have never been found). Inflation solves all three: everything was originally in contact, inflation stretched any initial curvature to flatness, and monopoles were diluted to undetectable density. The theory predicts a specific pattern of temperature fluctuations in the cosmic microwave background — which has been confirmed by WMAP and Planck satellite data. What drove inflation (the "inflaton field") remains unknown.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0010-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'The Late Heavy Bombardment',
   'Approximately 4.1–3.8 billion years ago, the inner solar system experienced a dramatic spike in asteroid and comet impacts — the Late Heavy Bombardment. It reshaped the Moon''s surface and may have delivered key ingredients for life to early Earth.',
   'The leading explanation, the Nice model (named after Nice, France, where it was developed), proposes that gravitational interactions between the outer planets caused Jupiter and Saturn to migrate, destabilizing the asteroid and Kuiper belt and sending a barrage of material inward. The evidence: the Moon''s heavily cratered highlands date primarily to this period, and lunar samples from Apollo missions cluster around 4.1–3.8 billion years ago. Paradoxically, the bombardment may have been necessary for life: it likely delivered water, organic molecules, and other volatiles to early Earth. Whether it sterilized any earlier life or allowed life to persist in deep ocean vents remains debated. Zircon crystals from Jack Hills, Australia, suggest Earth had liquid water before 4.1 billion years ago — possibly before the bombardment.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0011-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Dark Energy and the Accelerating Universe',
   'Dark energy — a mysterious repulsive force or property of space itself — makes up 68% of the universe''s total energy content and is causing the expansion of the universe to accelerate. We have no satisfactory physical explanation for it.',
   'In 1998, two independent teams studying Type Ia supernovae (which are "standard candles" of known brightness) found that distant supernovae were dimmer than expected — meaning they were farther away than a decelerating universe would predict. The universe was not just expanding; it was accelerating. Saul Perlmutter, Brian Schmidt, and Adam Riess won the 2011 Nobel Prize for the discovery. The cosmological constant (Λ) that Einstein added then removed from his equations ("my greatest blunder") describes the effect mathematically, but explaining it physically is unresolved. Proposed explanations include vacuum energy (quantum field theory predicts a vacuum energy density, but 120 orders of magnitude too large), quintessence (a dynamic scalar field), and modifications to general relativity. The discrepancy between observation and theory is one of physics'' deepest puzzles.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0012-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Red Dwarf Stars Will Outlive Our Sun by Trillions of Years',
   'Red dwarf stars — the smallest and most common stellar type — burn so slowly that they will remain on the main sequence for 1–10 trillion years. Our Sun, by comparison, will exhaust its fuel in about 5 billion years.',
   'Red dwarfs (spectral class M) have masses between 7.5% and 50% of the Sun''s. Their lower mass means lower core pressure, lower fusion rates, and dramatically longer lifespans. Proxima Centauri, the nearest star to our Sun, is a red dwarf with a projected lifespan of 4 trillion years — roughly 300 times the current age of the universe. As massive, short-lived stars die, the fraction of red dwarfs in the galaxy grows: in the far future, red dwarfs will dominate the stellar population. Because red dwarfs are so common (70% of all stars), most of the galaxy''s potentially habitable planets orbit red dwarfs — raising profound questions about whether life can persist on planets subject to red dwarf flares and tidal locking.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0013-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'The Hercules-Corona Borealis Great Wall',
   'The Hercules-Corona Borealis Great Wall is the largest known structure in the observable universe — a supercluster filament of galaxies spanning roughly 10 billion light-years, or about 10% of the observable universe''s diameter.',
   'Discovered in 2013 by Balazs et al. through the distribution of gamma-ray bursts (which trace the locations of massive star-forming galaxies), the structure challenges cosmological models that predict large-scale homogeneity. The cosmological principle holds that, at sufficiently large scales, the universe is homogeneous and isotropic — the same in all directions. The Hercules-Corona Borealis Great Wall is large enough that it takes light longer to cross than it has existed, which some cosmologists argue makes it a statistical fluctuation rather than a coherent structure. Whether it represents a genuine challenge to the cosmological principle or an artifact of sampling and detection methods is actively debated.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0014-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Neutron Stars: City-Sized and Denser Than the Sun',
   'A neutron star packs more mass than the Sun into a sphere about 20 km across — smaller than most cities. Its density is so extreme that a teaspoon would weigh roughly a billion tonnes on Earth.',
   'Neutron stars are the collapsed cores of massive stars that underwent supernova. When the core collapses, electron pressure is overwhelmed and electrons merge with protons to form neutrons — producing a densely packed sphere of almost pure neutronium. The surface gravity is 200 billion times Earth''s. The fastest-spinning neutron stars (pulsars) rotate up to 716 times per second — held together only by their own gravity against the centrifugal force that would tear any other object apart. The 2017 detection of gravitational waves from two merging neutron stars (GW170817), followed by electromagnetic observations across the spectrum, was the first "multi-messenger" astronomical event — and confirmed that heavy elements (gold, platinum, uranium) are forged in neutron star mergers.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0015-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Why Mars Lost Its Atmosphere',
   'Mars has a thin atmosphere (about 1% of Earth''s pressure) because it lacks a global magnetic field. Without this shield, the solar wind stripped away the atmosphere over billions of years — making Mars the cold, dry desert it is today.',
   'Mars''s liquid outer core solidified roughly 4 billion years ago, ending the global magnetic field that had partially protected its atmosphere. Without the field, solar wind particles (charged particles from the Sun) interacted directly with atmospheric molecules, gradually accelerating them to escape velocity. NASA''s MAVEN orbiter measured this stripping process directly, finding Mars currently loses about 100 grams of atmosphere per second. Ancient Mars had a much thicker atmosphere: river valleys, lake beds, and mineral deposits indicate liquid water flowing on the surface for potentially billions of years. Terraforming Mars through artificial magnetosphere proposals (large orbiting magnets at L1) is theoretically plausible but would require millennia for atmosphere to rebuild even if stripping were stopped.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0016-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'The Oort Cloud: Where Long-Period Comets Come From',
   'The Oort Cloud is a vast, spherical shell of icy objects surrounding the solar system at distances between 2,000 and 200,000 AU. It is the reservoir that supplies long-period comets — those with orbital periods exceeding 200 years.',
   'Jan Oort proposed the cloud''s existence in 1950 to explain why long-period comets arrive from all directions and on seemingly random orbits — indicating an isotropic source far outside the plane of the solar system. The cloud is thought to contain trillions of icy bodies, remnants of the early solar system that were gravitationally scattered outward by the giant planets. Perturbations from passing stars and the galactic tide occasionally deflect Oort Cloud objects inward, sending them on long elliptical paths into the inner solar system. The cloud has never been directly observed — no telescope can detect individual objects at such distances — but is inferred from the orbits of incoming long-period comets. Its outer edge may extend nearly a quarter of the way to Proxima Centauri.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0017-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'How Stars Make Elements Heavier Than Iron',
   'Stellar nucleosynthesis can fuse elements up to iron, where fusion stops releasing energy. Elements heavier than iron are forged in supernovae and neutron star mergers — violent events that inject massive neutron fluxes into existing nuclei.',
   'In stellar cores, hydrogen fuses to helium, helium to carbon, carbon to neon, and so on up through the periodic table to iron (Fe-56). Iron''s binding energy per nucleon is the highest of any element — fusing iron absorbs energy rather than releasing it. When a massive star''s iron core exceeds 1.4 solar masses, it collapses in milliseconds, triggering a supernova that releases more energy in seconds than the Sun will emit over its entire lifetime. The resulting neutron flood drives rapid neutron capture (r-process): iron nuclei absorb neutrons faster than they can decay, climbing to bismuth, uranium, and beyond. The 2017 kilonova observation confirmed neutron star mergers as the primary site of gold, platinum, and uranium production — meaning the gold in your jewelry was likely forged in a neutron star collision billions of years ago.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0018-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Why Saturn''s Rings Will Disappear',
   'Saturn''s spectacular ring system is geologically young — perhaps only 100–400 million years old — and will be gone within 100 million years as particles rain into Saturn''s atmosphere in a process called "ring rain."',
   'Cassini spacecraft data revealed that Saturn is losing its rings at a rate of 10,000–100,000 tonnes per hour. The mechanism: ultraviolet radiation from the Sun ionizes water molecules in the rings; Saturn''s magnetic field then pulls the charged particles along field lines into the upper atmosphere, where they burn up. The rings are also being gravitationally shepherded inward by Saturn''s inner moons. If the ring loss rate is consistent, the rings will be gone in 100 million years — a cosmic eyeblink. This means Earth-based astronomers during the age of dinosaurs saw no Saturn rings, and astronomers in the distant future will see none. We happen to exist during the brief window when they are present — a cosmic coincidence if we happened to look, and something to appreciate.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0019-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'The Anthropic Principle',
   'The anthropic principle observes that the universe''s physical constants must be compatible with the existence of observers like us — because if they weren''t, we wouldn''t be here to observe them. It is a selection effect, not a teleological claim.',
   'Brandon Carter formulated the weak anthropic principle in 1973: what we observe is constrained by our existence as observers. The strong anthropic principle (more controversial) claims the universe must have properties permitting life. The weak version is a tautology that is nonetheless methodologically important: it explains why we observe a universe compatible with complex structures. Fine-tuning puzzles (why is the cosmological constant almost exactly zero? Why are the fundamental constants in narrow ranges compatible with stars and atoms?) can be answered anthropically in a multiverse: if many universes exist with varying constants, observers only exist in those compatible with complexity. Critics note this can explain anything and therefore tests nothing.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0020-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'Tidal Locking: Why We See Only One Face of the Moon',
   'The Moon always shows the same face to Earth because it rotates exactly once per orbit — a state called tidal locking, produced by billions of years of gravitational friction between Earth''s tidal forces and the Moon''s interior.',
   'Early in the Moon''s history, it rotated faster. Earth''s gravity creates a tidal bulge on the Moon; because the Moon was rotating faster than its orbit, this bulge was pulled slightly ahead of the Earth-Moon line. Earth''s gravity pulled back on this bulge, creating a torque that slowed the Moon''s rotation until it matched its orbital period — tidal locking. The process takes hundreds of millions to billions of years. Many of the solar system''s moons are tidally locked to their planets, as are Pluto and Charon to each other. Mercury is not locked to the Sun but is in a 3:2 spin-orbit resonance — rotating exactly 1.5 times per orbit, which is also a stable configuration. Earth itself is slowly being tidally braked — Earth''s day is lengthening by about 1.4 milliseconds per century.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0021-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'How Radio Telescopes Work',
   'Radio telescopes detect radio waves from space — not by amplifying light but by focusing radio frequency electromagnetic radiation onto sensitive receivers. They can peer through dust and gas opaque to visible light, revealing the universe''s cold, energetic structures.',
   'Radio waves from space are focused by large dish antennas onto a receiver at the focal point. The signal is amplified, filtered to specific frequency bands, and digitized for analysis. Because radio wavelengths are orders of magnitude longer than visible light, a single radio telescope has far lower resolution than an optical telescope — requiring extremely large dishes (the FAST telescope in China has a 500-meter aperture) or interferometry. Very Long Baseline Interferometry (VLBI) links radio telescopes across continents — creating an effective aperture the size of Earth, enabling the resolution that produced the first black hole image in 2019. Radio astronomy has revealed pulsars, cosmic microwave background radiation, quasars, water masers in star-forming regions, and the 21-cm hydrogen line used to map the structure of galaxies.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0022-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'The Cosmic Microwave Background',
   'The Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) is light from 380,000 years after the Big Bang — when the universe cooled enough for atoms to form and photons to travel freely for the first time. It is the oldest light we can observe.',
   'Before recombination (380,000 years post-Big Bang), the universe was an opaque plasma; photons couldn''t travel without scattering off free electrons. When electrons combined with protons to form neutral hydrogen, the universe became transparent. The photons released at that moment have been traveling ever since, stretched by cosmic expansion to microwave wavelengths. Discovered accidentally by Penzias and Wilson in 1964 (earning the 1978 Nobel Prize), the CMB has a temperature of 2.725 Kelvin with tiny fluctuations (1 part in 100,000) that are the seeds of all subsequent cosmic structure — galaxies, clusters, and the cosmic web. The WMAP (2003) and Planck (2009–2013) satellites mapped these fluctuations with exquisite precision, providing our most detailed picture of the early universe.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('aaaaaaaa-000a-0023-000a-00000000000a', 'space', 'How Exoplanet Atmospheres Are Detected',
   'Astronomers detect exoplanet atmospheres by analyzing starlight filtered through the atmosphere during transit — different molecules absorb specific wavelengths, leaving spectroscopic fingerprints that reveal atmospheric composition.',
   'During transit (when a planet passes in front of its star), a tiny fraction of starlight passes through the planet''s atmosphere. Molecules absorb characteristic wavelengths — water absorbs infrared at 1.4 and 1.9 microns; carbon dioxide at 4.3 microns; methane at 3.3 microns. By comparing the star''s spectrum during vs. outside transit, astronomers isolate atmospheric absorption features. The James Webb Space Telescope, with its 6.5-meter mirror and infrared sensitivity, has transformed this capability: JWST detected carbon dioxide in the atmosphere of WASP-39b in 2022 — the first clear detection of CO₂ in an exoplanet atmosphere. Detecting biosignatures (oxygen, methane, water in combination) on Earth-sized planets in habitable zones is JWST''s most ambitious scientific goal, requiring dozens of transit observations for each target.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Language ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0004-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'A Language Dies Every Two Weeks',
   'Approximately 7,000 languages exist today; linguists estimate one dies roughly every two weeks. With each death, a unique cognitive framework, oral history, and relationship to the natural world disappears permanently.',
   'Language death typically follows a predictable path: a community shifts to a dominant language for economic opportunity; parents stop transmitting the heritage language to children; the last fluent speakers age and die. UNESCO''s Atlas of the World''s Languages in Danger catalogues over 2,500 languages at risk. Revitalization is possible: Hebrew was revived from a liturgical language to a living mother tongue in a century; Welsh, Maori, and Hawaiian have stabilized through deliberate policy. But most endangered languages lack the political support and economic resources for successful revitalization. Linguists work urgently to document languages before the last speakers die — creating archives that may be the only record of conceptual structures evolved over millennia.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0005-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'How Languages Borrow Words and Keep Grammar',
   'Languages readily borrow vocabulary from contact languages but are highly resistant to borrowing grammatical structures. English has absorbed French, Latin, Norse, and hundreds of other vocabularies while keeping its Germanic grammar almost intact.',
   'After the Norman Conquest (1066), English adopted approximately 10,000 French words in a century — words for government, law, cuisine, and nobility. Yet English retained its Germanic subject-verb-object word order, its Germanic core pronouns, and its Germanic morphological patterns. Linguist Sarah Thomason identified a continuum: lexical borrowing is easy, phonological borrowing intermediate, grammatical borrowing extremely difficult and typically requiring centuries of intense bilingual contact. The rare cases of structural borrowing occur in geographic areas of intense multilingualism (the Balkans, the Pacific Northwest). This asymmetry explains why you can learn Italian vocabulary in a month but Italian grammar takes years — vocabulary is a lookup table, grammar is an operating system.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0006-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'Creole Languages',
   'A creole is a stable, fully-developed language that emerges when speakers of different languages need to communicate and create a simplified contact language (pidgin) — which is then acquired by children as a first language, triggering rapid grammatical elaboration.',
   'Pidgins are simplified languages used for trade or labor coordination; they have limited vocabulary, unstable grammar, and no native speakers. When children grow up hearing a pidgin, they do something remarkable: they add grammatical structure, tonal distinctions, and morphological complexity that the pidgin lacked — producing a creole that is as grammatically rich as any natural language. Derek Bickerton''s Language Bioprogram Hypothesis (1981) proposed that creole languages reveal universal grammatical defaults — the structure the language faculty produces when given minimal input. Haitian Creole, Tok Pisin, Jamaican Patois, and Louisiana Creole are fully functional languages with millions of speakers, not "broken" versions of their lexifier languages.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0007-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'Universal Grammar: Chomsky''s Theory and Its Critics',
   'Noam Chomsky proposed that all humans share an innate "universal grammar" — a biological faculty with core structural properties common to all languages. The theory has been enormously influential and enormously contested.',
   'The poverty of the stimulus argument is Chomsky''s core claim: children acquire complex grammatical structures faster and more uniformly than the impoverished, error-filled input they receive could explain — suggesting that much of the structure is built in. Specific cross-linguistic patterns seemed to confirm this: all languages have nouns and verbs; all appeared to have recursion. The challenges, however, are substantial. Daniel Everett''s analysis of Pirahã (an Amazonian language) claims it lacks recursion entirely — fiercely disputed but unresolved. The World Atlas of Language Structures documents enormous structural variation that any proposed universal must explain away. More fundamentally, usage-based theories (Tomasello, Goldberg) argue that grammar emerges from statistical learning of patterns in language input rather than innate structure — and neural network models that learn grammar from input alone have complicated the poverty-of-stimulus argument empirically.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0008-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'Why Some Languages Have Grammatical Gender',
   'Grammatical gender — the classification of nouns into categories (masculine, feminine, neuter) that govern agreement — appears in about a quarter of the world''s languages. It is cognitively costly and has no obvious communicative advantage. Its origins are partly historical accident.',
   'Indo-European languages inherited a three-way gender system (masculine, feminine, neuter) from Proto-Indo-European. Gender systems tend to persist because they create interdependencies throughout a grammar: adjectives, articles, pronouns, and verb agreement all reference gender, making the system hard to change partially. Contact with languages without gender (Turkish, Finnish, Hungarian) can cause gender system simplification — Persian lost its gender through Semitic contact; English lost its Old English gender through Scandinavian contact. Languages without grammatical gender (Mandarin, Turkish, Swahili) are neither cognitively impoverished nor superior — gender encodes certain information efficiently but can also cause confusion (la problème vs. le problème in French reflects historical arbitrary assignment, not problem''s maleness).',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0009-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'Code-Switching',
   'Code-switching is the practice of alternating between two or more languages within a conversation — often within a single sentence. It is not a sign of linguistic incompetence but of sophisticated bilingual fluency and social signaling.',
   'Monolinguals often view code-switching as confused or impure language use. Research consistently shows the opposite: code-switching requires high proficiency in both languages and operates according to systematic grammatical rules (you cannot switch mid-way through a word; switching typically occurs at constituent boundaries). Socially, code-switching signals identity, intimacy, domain membership, and power dynamics. Spanglish speakers may use English for technical terms and Spanish for emotional content, or switch to signal in-group membership with a bilingual interlocutor. Penelope Gardner-Chloros''s research documents that code-switching patterns reflect the social meaning of each language in the speaker''s community — not random mixing.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0010-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'The Most Translated Book Isn''t the Bible',
   'The most widely translated publication in history is not the Bible but "The Watchtower" magazine, published by Jehovah''s Witnesses. It is published in over 240 languages and distributed in quantities exceeding 50 million copies per issue.',
   'The Watchtower''s organization maintains a systematic, volunteer-driven translation infrastructure covering nearly every language with a significant population. The Bible, while translated into over 700 languages (with portions translated into 3,500+), is rarely re-translated into a single language by a single organization as systematically. In terms of individual book translations, Pinocchio has been translated into over 260 languages; Don Quixote into approximately 140. Literary translation statistics are contested because "translation" ranges from full text to excerpts, adaptations, and simplified versions. The Watchtower example challenges assumptions about what cultural production motivates the most extensive global translation efforts — it is organizational mission, not literary merit.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0011-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'Subvocalization and Reading Speed',
   'Subvocalization — the internal "voice" you hear when reading — is used by nearly all readers. Speed reading courses claim to eliminate it and achieve 1,000+ words per minute. Cognitive science shows this is impossible without catastrophic comprehension loss.',
   'Subvocalization is a remnant of reading acquisition: we learn to read aloud before learning to read silently, and the internal voice persists as a phonological loop that aids comprehension, working memory, and the processing of ambiguous text. Studies with EMG (measuring muscle activity in the larynx) show subvocalization persists even in proficient silent readers. The maximum speed of subvocalization limits reading speed to 300–500 WPM for most skilled readers. Claims of 1,000+ WPM comprehension from speed reading programs do not hold up to reading comprehension testing: skimming at high speeds produces surface understanding, not deep comprehension. Reading faster with comprehension requires building a larger vocabulary and knowledge base — not eliminating phonological processing.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0012-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'Linguistic Relativity: Language Shapes Thought',
   'The Sapir-Whorf hypothesis — that language shapes cognition — has been substantially confirmed in its weak form: language influences thought and perception, but does not determine it. Speakers of different languages show measurable cognitive differences on tasks influenced by their language''s structure.',
   'Strong Whorfianism (language determines what you can think) is discredited: speakers of all languages can think about any concept whether or not their language has a word for it. Weak Whorfianism is supported: Russian speakers (who must grammatically distinguish light blue from dark blue) are faster on color discrimination tasks at the goluboy/siniy boundary. Languages that use absolute directions (north/south) rather than relative ones (left/right) — like Guugu Yimithirr in Australia — have speakers who maintain perfect orientation in novel environments but show different spatial memory patterns. Lera Boroditsky''s research on temporal metaphors (English speakers orient time left-right; Mandarin speakers often top-bottom; Hopi has no grammatical tense) shows language structures habitual ways of thinking about abstract concepts.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0013-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'The Rosetta Stone',
   'The Rosetta Stone — discovered in 1799 by French soldiers in Egypt — bears the same priestly decree in three scripts: Ancient Egyptian hieroglyphics, Demotic script, and Ancient Greek. It provided the key to deciphering hieroglyphics after 1,400 years of lost meaning.',
   'Thomas Young identified the phonetic nature of some hieroglyphics in 1814, recognizing that royal names in cartouches were written phonetically. Jean-François Champollion made the decisive breakthrough in 1822, recognizing that hieroglyphics were a hybrid system — partly logographic (symbols representing concepts) and partly phonographic (symbols representing sounds). Crucially, the Coptic language (which Champollion knew) was the descendant of Ancient Egyptian, giving him a phonetic key to many sounds. The full decipherment took until 1832. The stone itself is now at the British Museum, where it is the most-visited object in the collection. Egypt has periodically requested its return; the British Museum has declined.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0014-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'Proto-Indo-European: The Ancestor Language',
   'Proto-Indo-European (PIE) is the reconstructed ancestor of a language family spanning Irish to Bengali, spoken by people living around the Black Sea or Central Asian steppes approximately 5,000–6,000 years ago. We have no written records of it.',
   'PIE is reconstructed through the comparative method: systematically comparing corresponding words and structures across descendant languages to infer the ancestral forms. English "father," Latin "pater," Greek "patēr," Sanskrit "pitṛ," and Persian "pedar" all derive from PIE *ph₂tḗr. The asterisk marks reconstructed (unattested) forms. Over 400 languages are descended from PIE, including all Germanic, Romance, Slavic, Indo-Iranian, Celtic, and Hellenic languages — spoken by roughly 3 billion people. The Kurgan hypothesis (David Anthony, Marija Gimbutas) locates PIE speakers on the Pontic-Caspian steppe, spreading through migration and cultural adoption as they developed horse domestication and wheeled vehicles — a case where archaeology and linguistics converge.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0015-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'Signed Languages Prove Language Is Modality-Independent',
   'Signed languages — ASL, BSL, LSF — are fully natural human languages with complete grammar, phonology (in the visual-gestural modality), syntax, and pragmatics. They develop spontaneously wherever deaf communities form and are not derived from spoken languages.',
   'William Stokoe''s 1960 description of American Sign Language''s grammatical structure was initially rejected — linguists and the deaf education establishment insisted signing was not a real language but a degraded form of English. Subsequent research established that ASL has all linguistic properties: a compositional phonological system using handshape, location, and movement; morphological processes (aspectual inflection, spatial grammar); complex syntax with relativization, questions, and subordination. When deaf children are not exposed to any language (spoken or signed) in childhood, they invent their own sign systems — first "home signs," and if multiple children are involved, these develop into full languages within a generation, as documented in Nicaraguan Sign Language (NSL).',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0016-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'The Critical Period for Language Acquisition',
   'Children acquire language virtually effortlessly in early childhood; adults learning a second language rarely achieve native-like proficiency. Eric Lenneberg proposed the critical period hypothesis in 1967: between ages 2 and puberty, the brain''s plasticity is optimal for language acquisition, and after puberty, native-level proficiency becomes essentially unattainable regardless of effort.',
   'The evidence converges from multiple directions. Children of immigrants achieve native-like accents in the new country regardless of how long they have been exposed; their parents, arriving as adults, typically retain foreign accents after decades of immersion — the critical asymmetry Lenneberg predicted. Brain damage to language areas in children is recovered from far more completely than equivalent adult damage, reflecting preserved plasticity. The case of Genie — a girl discovered at age 13 having been completely isolated with no language exposure — confirmed the hypothesis tragically: despite years of intensive therapy, she never acquired normal grammar, though vocabulary and pragmatic competence developed. Neuroimaging shows that late language learners process their second language in partly different brain regions from native speakers. The cutoff is not sharp: outcomes are best for learners who begin before age 8, good for beginners before puberty, and diminish progressively after that.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0017-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'The Euphemism Treadmill',
   'The euphemism treadmill describes the cycle where polite replacement words for sensitive topics gradually acquire the negative connotations of the words they replaced — requiring new replacements in an endless cycle.',
   'Steven Pinker coined the term in "The Blank Slate" (2002). Historical examples: "idiot," "imbecile," and "moron" were clinical terms introduced to replace stigmatizing predecessors; they became insults within decades. "Mental retardation" replaced these; it became stigmatizing and was replaced by "intellectual disability." "Toilet" (from French "toile," a cloth) replaced "privy" and "water closet"; "bathroom" and "restroom" followed. The cycle operates because the stigma is not in the word itself but in the concept — any word that refers to a stigmatized condition acquires that stigma. The treadmill has practical and philosophical implications: it suggests that euphemistic replacement alone cannot destigmatize; genuine attitude change is required for words to remain neutral.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0018-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'How Metaphor Structures Abstract Thought',
   'Most abstract thought is metaphorical — we understand time as a resource, arguments as battles, ideas as objects to grasp. These are not literary ornaments but the cognitive structures through which we understand the abstract world.',
   'Lakoff and Johnson''s "Metaphors We Live By" (1980) documented the pervasive "conceptual metaphors" structuring English thought. ARGUMENT IS WAR: "He attacked every weak point in my argument," "She demolished his position." TIME IS MONEY: "I''ve invested a lot of time in this," "Don''t waste my time." These aren''t consciously chosen figures of speech — they are the default conceptual structure. Cross-linguistic research shows conceptual metaphors vary: English speakers represent time horizontally (earlier events are "behind" us); Mandarin speakers often represent time vertically (earlier events are "up"). The neuroscience of metaphor processing shows that conventional metaphors (like "grasp a concept") activate motor cortex — the brain still partially simulates physical grasping even for abstract understanding.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0019-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'The History of Punctuation',
   'Modern punctuation — periods, commas, question marks, quotation marks — is only about 500 years old, a product of the printing press standardizing what had been highly variable manuscript conventions. Ancient Greek and Latin texts had no word spaces, let alone punctuation.',
   'Aristophanes of Byzantium (3rd century BCE) introduced dots at different heights to indicate short, medium, and long pauses for oral performance — ancestors of the comma, colon, and period. Medieval manuscripts had inconsistent punctuation varying by scribe. Aldus Manutius, the Venetian printer (late 15th century), standardized the semicolon and slash-style comma for his printed editions. The question mark evolved from a "quaestio" notation; the exclamation mark from "io" (Latin for joy). Apostrophes indicating possession appeared in English only in the 17th century and have been contested ever since (it''s vs. its; Joneses'' vs. Joneses). The Oxford comma (the comma before "and" in a list) remains genuinely controversial among editors, with legal cases turning on its presence or absence.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0020-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'Descriptivism vs. Prescriptivism in Dictionaries',
   'Dictionaries can describe how language is actually used (descriptivism) or prescribe how it should be used (prescriptivism). Modern professional lexicographers are descriptivists — but most dictionary users think they''re getting prescriptivist authority.',
   'Samuel Johnson''s 1755 dictionary was explicitly prescriptive, attempting to "fix" and purify English. The Oxford English Dictionary (first complete edition 1928) shifted to historical description, tracking actual usage with earliest citations. When Merriam-Webster added "irregardless" or recognized new senses of words like "literally," public outrage followed — because users misunderstood dictionaries as authority rather than record. David Foster Wallace argued for a middle ground: "snoot" usage (caring about language) is valuable, but the descriptivist insight (language is how people actually use it) is linguistically correct. The American Heritage Dictionary uses a usage panel of writers and editors to provide prescriptive guidance while acknowledging actual usage — a pragmatic compromise between the two positions.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0021-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'Why English Has So Many Words',
   'English has approximately 170,000 words in active use (the OED) and perhaps 750,000+ including technical, scientific, and regional terms. This exceptional vocabulary size results from English''s unique history of absorbing vocabularies from multiple language families.',
   'English began as an Anglo-Saxon (Germanic) language, absorbed Norse vocabulary through Viking settlement (giving us "sky," "egg," "knife"), then an enormous French vocabulary after the Norman Conquest (law, government, cuisine), then systematic Latin and Greek borrowings during the Renaissance for scientific and philosophical terminology. Unlike French (which actively resists foreign borrowing through the Académie française) or German (which prefers compound native words like "Fernseher" for television), English happily borrows from any contact language. The result is often near-synonym triplets: Germanic ("begin"), French ("commence"), Latin ("initiate") — each with slightly different register and connotation. This richness is a historical accident of conquest and contact, not inherent superiority.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0022-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'Why Swearing Is Processed Differently in the Brain',
   'Swear words activate emotional brain regions (amygdala, limbic system) more strongly than neutral words, are better remembered, and appear later in aphasia (language loss from brain damage) — suggesting they are stored and retrieved differently from ordinary vocabulary.',
   'Patients with aphasia who cannot produce fluent speech often retain the ability to swear — suggesting swear words are processed partly through subcortical emotional systems rather than the cortical language areas. Timothy Jay''s research documents that taboo words produce stronger skin conductance responses (physiological arousal) than semantically matched non-taboo words. Swearing enhances pain tolerance: participants holding hands in ice water endure it longer while repeating a swear word than a neutral word (Stephens et al., 2009). The effect may work through adrenal arousal. Bilinguals typically swear in their first language even after decades of primary second-language use — emotional words learned early retain their visceral charge. The power of swear words is maintained precisely by their prohibition: euphemistic replacements gradually lose their emotional punch.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('bbbbbbbb-000b-0023-000b-00000000000b', 'language', 'How Writing Was Invented Independently',
   'Writing was invented independently at least three times in human history — in Mesopotamia (cuneiform, c. 3400 BCE), China (oracle bone script, c. 1200 BCE), and Mesoamerica (Olmec/Zapotec, c. 900 BCE). Egyptian and Indus Valley writing may also be independent inventions.',
   'Each independent writing system began as a practical tool for record-keeping — trade tallies, tax records, divination records — not as a medium for literature or communication. Sumerian cuneiform began as clay tokens impressed into clay tablets to track grain and livestock; it was not until 300 years after its invention that scribes adapted it for language sounds. Chinese oracle bones recorded divination questions to royal ancestors. All writing systems can be classified on a continuum from logographic (signs = meanings, e.g., Chinese characters) to syllabic (signs = syllables, e.g., Japanese kana) to alphabetic (signs = phonemes, e.g., Latin alphabet). The alphabet — representing individual sounds — was invented only once, in the ancient Levant around 1800 BCE, and all alphabets in use today are descended from it.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Nature ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('cccccccc-000c-0004-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'The Waggle Dance: Honeybee Navigation',
   'Honeybee scouts communicate the exact location of food sources to hive-mates through the waggle dance — a figure-eight movement whose angle indicates direction relative to the sun and whose duration encodes distance. It is one of the most sophisticated non-human communication systems known.',
   'Karl von Frisch decoded the waggle dance in the 1940s, earning a Nobel Prize in 1973. The duration of the waggle run correlates with distance: each second of waggling represents roughly 1 km. The angle of the dance on the vertical comb corresponds to the angle between the food source, the hive, and the sun — a sophisticated trigonometric encoding. Bees also use the dance to recruit nest-site scouts during swarming, with competing scouts advertising different sites until a democratic quorum is reached. The dance degrades in information quality over time, which continuously updates the colony''s foraging strategy as conditions change.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0005-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Why Forests Need Fire',
   'Many forest ecosystems evolved with regular fire and depend on it for ecological health. Fire clears accumulated deadwood, releases locked-up nutrients, and creates the open conditions some species require for germination and habitat — making periodic burning a feature of these ecosystems, not a threat.',
   'Lodgepole pine and giant sequoia have serotinous cones: sealed with resin, they only open to release seeds when heated by fire, ensuring regeneration immediately after a burn when competing vegetation has been cleared and nutrients released. Without fire, organic matter accumulates into fuel loads that transform manageable surface fires into catastrophic crown fires that burn hot enough to sterilize soil and kill even the seeds adapted to survive. A century of aggressive suppression created exactly this backlog across the American West. Indigenous communities in Australia had used cultural burning for 50,000+ years to maintain productive, fire-resilient landscapes — a practice now being integrated into formal fire management alongside prescribed burns. The challenge is not whether to burn but how to work through decades of accumulated fuel safely.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0006-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Keystone Species',
   'A keystone species is one whose effect on an ecosystem is disproportionately large relative to its abundance. Remove it, and the ecosystem transforms dramatically or collapses. The classic example is the sea otter, whose predation on sea urchins maintains kelp forest ecosystems.',
   'Robert Paine coined the term "keystone species" in 1969 after observing that removing sea stars (Pisaster ochraceus) from an intertidal zone allowed mussels to take over, eliminating dozens of other species and radically simplifying the ecosystem. Sea otters eat urchins; without otters, urchin populations explode and graze kelp down to bare rock — destroying habitat for hundreds of species. Wolves in Yellowstone are another example: their reintroduction triggered a trophic cascade that changed vegetation and even altered river morphology. The keystone concept remains a powerful conservation tool for prioritizing which species to protect.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0007-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'The Octopus: Distributed Intelligence',
   'Octopuses have three hearts, blue blood (from copper-based hemocyanin instead of iron-based hemoglobin), and a distributed nervous system: two-thirds of their neurons are in their eight arms, which can act semi-autonomously from the central brain.',
   'Octopus intelligence challenges assumptions about the neural basis of cognition: their 500 million neurons (comparable to a dog) are organized completely differently from vertebrate brains, having evolved independently. Each arm contains a ganglion that processes sensory information and can execute complex movements without consulting the central brain. Octopuses demonstrate tool use (carrying coconut shells for later shelter), play behavior, individual personalities, and can solve problems through insight rather than trial-and-error. Being almost entirely asocial and short-lived (1–2 years), their remarkable intelligence cannot be explained by social complexity theories — they are a profound counterexample to the social brain hypothesis.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0008-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'How Plants Defend Themselves',
   'Plants are not passive victims of herbivores. They produce chemical defenses — toxins, digestibility reducers, and aromatic signals — and can even communicate with neighboring plants and recruit predators of their attackers.',
   'When a caterpillar begins feeding on a tomato plant, the plant releases volatile organic compounds (VOCs) that neighboring plants detect and respond to by upregulating their own defenses preemptively. Some plants — like the Lima bean — release VOCs that attract predatory mites to attack the spider mites feeding on the plant. Tannins in leaves bind to proteins, reducing digestibility for herbivores; alkaloids like caffeine (in coffee) and nicotine (in tobacco) are toxic at high concentrations. The arms race between plants and herbivores has driven enormous co-evolutionary diversification: approximately 25% of all plant compounds are estimated to be defensive chemicals.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0009-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Mutualism: Cooperation in Nature',
   'Mutualism — a relationship between species where both benefit — is as fundamental to ecology as predation and competition. The mycorrhizal network, where fungi colonize plant roots and exchange minerals for sugars, is the foundation of most terrestrial ecosystems.',
   'Over 90% of land plants have mycorrhizal associations: fungal hyphae extend the plant''s effective root system by orders of magnitude, accessing phosphorus, nitrogen, and water the plant cannot reach alone, in exchange for photosynthetically fixed carbon. The "wood wide web" — connections between trees through shared fungal networks — may allow carbon transfer between trees, though the extent and ecological significance is debated. The clownfish-anemone mutualism, fig trees and fig wasps (the only pollinators of each other), and acacia ants (which defend acacias against herbivores in exchange for food and shelter) are other well-documented mutualisms. Mutualism is so pervasive that most eukaryotic cells are themselves mutualisms — mitochondria began as bacterial endosymbionts.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0010-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Slime Molds: Intelligence Without a Brain',
   'Physarum polycephalum, the yellow slime mold, can solve mazes, find the shortest path between food sources, and recreate the layout of Tokyo''s rail network when food is placed at major city locations — without any neurons.',
   'Toshiyuki Nakagaki''s 2000 Science paper demonstrated P. polycephalum navigating a maze to connect two food sources via the shortest path. A 2010 study placed oat flakes at the locations of 36 cities around Tokyo and observed the organism forming a network almost identical to the actual railway system — one that balances efficiency with redundancy. The mechanism: the slime mold reinforces paths with greater flow (where more nutrients travel) and allows less-used paths to narrow and disappear — a dynamic optimization without any centralized control. This process mimics Ant Colony Optimization algorithms used in computer science for routing and logistics.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0011-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'How Birds Navigate Using Magnetic Fields',
   'Many migratory birds can detect Earth''s magnetic field and use it as a compass. The mechanism may involve a quantum-mechanical process in the eye involving cryptochrome proteins — potentially making bird navigation a macroscopic quantum phenomenon.',
   'The cryptochrome hypothesis proposes that light activates radical pairs in proteins in the bird''s eye, and the orientation of Earth''s magnetic field influences which of these radical pairs react — effectively giving the bird a visual representation of magnetic field direction. The magnetite hypothesis (iron-containing particles in the beak) was long favored but the cryptochrome mechanism is now considered more likely. Birds must calibrate their magnetic compass against the sun''s position at sunrise and sunset, explaining why migratory birds become confused in dense fog. Some birds also use star patterns: hand-reared birds raised without seeing a night sky cannot navigate south correctly.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0012-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Trophic Cascade: How Predators Shape Ecosystems',
   'A trophic cascade occurs when a predator''s presence or absence causes changes that ripple down through multiple levels of the food web, altering the abundance and behavior of species far removed from the predator''s direct prey.',
   'The classic demonstration: when sea otters were hunted to near-extinction off the Pacific coast, sea urchin populations exploded; urchins grazed kelp forests to bare rock; the loss of kelp eliminated habitat for hundreds of species. Otter recovery reversed the cascade. In freshwater lakes, adding largemouth bass causes cascading changes through smaller fish to zooplankton to algae — a cascade that has been experimentally manipulated many times. Trophic cascades have both top-down drivers (predators suppressing herbivores, allowing plants) and bottom-up drivers (nutrient availability affecting plant growth, propagating upward). The concept has important conservation implications: protecting apex predators is not just about charismatic megafauna but about maintaining the structural integrity of entire ecosystems.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0013-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'The Humongous Fungus',
   'The largest known organism on Earth by area is Armillaria ostoyae, a honey fungus in the Malheur National Forest in Oregon, covering approximately 9.6 square kilometers. It is estimated to be between 2,400 and 8,000 years old.',
   'The organism is mostly underground — its visible presence is clusters of honey-colored mushrooms on tree roots. The bulk is a network of white, root-like mycelium threading through soil and wood. Armillaria is also a pathogen: it kills conifers by colonizing their roots, then spreading vegetatively to neighboring trees. Its vast extent was discovered through DNA testing of fungal samples across the forest, showing genetically identical samples across the entire 9.6 km² area — meaning it is a single individual, not a colony. These organisms challenge conventional definitions of "individual" in biology: the Pando aspen grove in Utah (a clonal colony of quaking aspen sharing a root system) may be the world''s heaviest organism at approximately 6,000 tonnes.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0014-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Mangrove Forests: Carbon Powerhouses',
   'Mangrove forests — coastal trees adapted to saltwater — store carbon in their soils up to 10 times faster per hectare than tropical rainforests, making them among the most carbon-dense ecosystems on Earth. They also protect coastlines from storm surge, filter water, and provide nursery habitat for fish.',
   'The exceptional carbon storage comes from the mechanism: mangroves sequester carbon primarily in anaerobic, waterlogged soils where decomposition is extremely slow, building deep organic layers over centuries that persist long after individual trees die. Unlike above-ground forest carbon, which is released rapidly when trees burn, mangrove soil carbon can remain locked away for millennia. Global mangrove cover has declined roughly 35–50% since the 1980s — driven by shrimp aquaculture, coastal development, and logging — releasing this ancient stored carbon back into the atmosphere. Indonesia, which holds the world''s largest mangrove area, now includes mangrove protection in its national climate commitments. Restoration efforts have shown mixed results at scale, largely because of poor site selection and species matching rather than any fundamental biological barrier.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0015-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Whale Fall: Death as Ecosystem',
   'When a large whale dies and sinks to the ocean floor, its carcass sustains a succession of distinct ecological communities over decades — a phenomenon called whale fall. A single whale can support hundreds of species and thousands of individuals.',
   'Craig Smith and colleagues documented whale falls in detail after finding a gray whale carcass in the Santa Catalina Basin in 1987. The succession occurs in stages: mobile scavengers (sharks, hagfish, crabs) strip the blubber and soft tissue; enrichment opportunist communities colonize the lipid-rich bones; sulfophilic bacteria decompose the lipids through sulfate reduction, producing hydrogen sulfide that supports chemosynthetic communities — essentially a miniature cold seep. The deep-sea ecosystem is so food-poor that whale falls represent enormous windfalls. Some species are specialists — found almost exclusively on whale bones — and may have evolved to hop between whale falls as stepping stones across the deep ocean floor.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0016-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'How Ant Colonies Make Decisions',
   'Ant colonies make collective decisions — choosing nest sites, foraging routes, and allocating labor — without any central controller. The queen does not give instructions; decisions emerge from local interactions and pheromone feedback.',
   'Deborah Gordon''s decades of harvester ant research showed that foraging activity levels are regulated by the rate at which returning foragers encounter outgoing ones: if food is abundant and foragers return quickly, more ants leave to forage; if conditions are poor, fewer ants are recruited. This simple local rule produces sophisticated colony-level regulation. Nest site selection in emigrating ant colonies involves scouts finding potential sites, assessing quality independently, then recruiting for the best site through "tandem running" — leading a nestmate to the site for direct assessment. A quorum threshold is required: once enough scouts independently confirm a good site, recruitment shifts to mass transport.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0017-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Deep Sea Hydrothermal Vents: Life Without Sunlight',
   'Hydrothermal vents — discovered in 1977 near the Galápagos Rift — host thriving ecosystems powered by chemosynthesis rather than photosynthesis. Bacteria oxidize hydrogen sulfide from the vents, forming the base of a food web entirely independent of sunlight.',
   'Before 1977, biologists assumed sunlight was a prerequisite for productive ecosystems. The discovery of vent communities with tube worms up to 2 meters long, crabs, clams, and shrimp living in complete darkness at 2,500 meters depth challenged this fundamental assumption. The tube worms (Riftia pachyptila) have no mouth or digestive system as adults — nourished entirely by chemosynthetic bacteria living symbiotically in a specialized organ called the trophosome. This discovery reshaped astrobiology: if life can thrive without sunlight at extreme pressures and temperatures, subsurface oceans on Europa and Enceladus — heated by tidal flexing and potentially hosting hydrothermal vents — become plausible candidates for extraterrestrial life.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0018-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Coral Reefs: Built by Animals',
   'Coral reefs are built by tiny animals — coral polyps — that secrete calcium carbonate skeletons. The reef structure is the accumulated skeleton of billions of polyps over thousands of years. Coral bleaching occurs when stressed corals expel the symbiotic algae that provide 90% of their nutrition.',
   'Corals are animals (cnidarians) that live in symbiosis with photosynthetic algae called zooxanthellae, which live inside the coral tissue and provide up to 90% of the coral''s energy through photosynthesis. When water temperatures rise above normal (typically by just 1–2°C above summer maximum for 4+ weeks), corals expel their zooxanthellae in a stress response — revealing the white calcium carbonate skeleton beneath. Bleached corals can recover if temperatures return to normal, but prolonged bleaching is fatal. The Great Barrier Reef has experienced five mass bleaching events since 1998. Reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor but support approximately 25% of all marine species.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0019-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Animal Migration: Navigation Across Thousands of Miles',
   'The bar-tailed godwit flies 11,000 km nonstop from Alaska to New Zealand — the longest known nonstop migration by any animal — without eating, drinking, or sleeping. The bird''s organs shrink during the flight to reduce weight.',
   'Before departure, bar-tailed godwits double their body weight through hyperphagia (extreme eating). Their digestive organs then atrophy — stomach, liver, and intestines shrink — freeing metabolic resources and reducing weight for the flight. They navigate using a combination of sun compass, star patterns, Earth''s magnetic field, and possibly infrasound. Monarch butterflies migrate 4,000 km from Canada to specific overwintering sites in Mexico that no individual butterfly has ever seen — a multi-generational migration using an innate sun compass. Arctic terns have the longest annual migration: approximately 70,000 km round trip from Arctic breeding grounds to Antarctic feeding areas.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0020-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'The Nitrogen Cycle',
   'Nitrogen makes up 78% of Earth''s atmosphere but is inaccessible to most organisms in its gaseous form. The nitrogen cycle — converting N₂ into biologically usable forms and back — is fundamental to all life and is largely controlled by bacteria.',
   'Nitrogen fixation — converting N₂ into ammonia — is performed by specialized bacteria (Rhizobium species in root nodules of legumes; free-living Azotobacter; cyanobacteria). Without nitrogen fixation, the biosphere would rapidly exhaust its supply of bioavailable nitrogen. The Haber-Bosch process (1909) industrially fixes nitrogen for fertilizer, feeding approximately half the world''s population but at enormous energy cost and with severe environmental consequences: nitrogen runoff causes algal blooms and ocean dead zones. Denitrifying bacteria complete the cycle by converting nitrates back to N₂. Humans have roughly doubled the rate of nitrogen fixation on Earth, with cascading effects on air quality, water quality, and biodiversity.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0021-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Epigenetics: Environment Meets Inheritance',
   'Epigenetics refers to heritable changes in gene expression that don''t alter the DNA sequence itself. Environmental factors — diet, stress, toxins — can switch genes on or off, and some of these changes can be passed to offspring.',
   'DNA methylation is the best-studied epigenetic mechanism: methyl groups attach to DNA at CpG sites, typically silencing genes in that region. Histone modification is another: the proteins around which DNA is wound can be chemically modified, affecting how accessible it is for transcription. The Dutch Hunger Winter (1944–45) is a key human study: children conceived during the famine showed altered methylation patterns throughout their lives, with elevated rates of metabolic disease — and their children showed similar patterns, suggesting transgenerational epigenetic inheritance. Identical twins start with identical DNA but diverge epigenetically over decades, explaining why they develop different diseases.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0022-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Why Wolves Change Rivers',
   'The reintroduction of wolves to Yellowstone in 1995 triggered one of the most dramatic documented trophic cascades — a chain of effects that ultimately changed the physical course of rivers through predator-driven vegetation recovery.',
   'Fourteen wolves were released in 1995; the population grew to roughly 100. Elk numbers declined and their behavior changed — they stopped grazing in open riverbeds and valleys where wolf ambush was possible. Willows, aspens, and cottonwoods regenerated along waterways for the first time in decades. Beaver populations recovered, and beaver dams created wetland ponds, slowing water flow and allowing riverbanks to stabilize and revegetate further. The stabilized banks changed the rivers'' meandering patterns. Ripple and Beschta published the compelling account of this cascade, though the wolf effect interacts with drought, elk hunting, and other variables.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0023-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Bioluminescence: Living Light',
   'Bioluminescence — the production of light by living organisms — has evolved independently over 40 times in animals alone. In the deep ocean, 75–90% of organisms produce their own light, using it for communication, predation, camouflage, and defense.',
   'The chemical mechanism is broadly conserved: a light-emitting molecule (luciferin) is oxidized by an enzyme (luciferase) to produce light with remarkable efficiency — nearly zero heat. Different organisms use chemically distinct luciferins, reflecting independent evolution. Firefly luciferase has become a ubiquitous research tool — inserted into cells alongside genes of interest, it lights up wherever the gene is expressed. Deep-sea anglerfish dangle a bioluminescent lure to attract prey. Dinoflagellates produce flashes when disturbed — the "phosphorescent sea." Counter-illumination camouflage — producing light on a ventral surface to match the dim glow from above — makes animals invisible from below in the twilight zone (200–1000 m depth).',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Food ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('dddddddd-000d-0004-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Capsaicin: Spicy Food Is Technically Pain',
   'Capsaicin — the compound that makes chili peppers hot — activates the TRPV1 receptor, a pain sensor that normally responds to damaging heat above 43°C. Eating spicy food is not a taste sensation but a pain response. The brain compensates by releasing endorphins.',
   'TRPV1 (Transient Receptor Potential Vanilloid 1) is a protein channel in nerve endings that evolved to detect actual heat damage. Capsaicin binds to it and triggers the same neural signal as touching a hot surface — without actual tissue damage. The brain interprets this as both pain and heat, which is why spicy food makes you sweat and feel flushed. Repeated exposure desensitizes TRPV1 receptors, explaining why regular hot food eaters develop tolerance. The endorphin release explains the "spicy food high" and why some people seek progressively hotter peppers. Capsaicin is used in pain management: topical capsaicin cream depletes substance P from nerve terminals, reducing chronic pain signals. Birds lack functional TRPV1 receptors and thus cannot taste capsaicin — an evolutionary arrangement that lets birds disperse chili seeds without the deterrent affecting the primary seed disperser.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0005-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Fermented Foods and the Gut-Brain Axis',
   'Fermented foods — yogurt, kimchi, sauerkraut, kefir — contain live bacteria that may influence brain function through the gut-brain axis. The gut contains approximately 100 million neurons and communicates bidirectionally with the brain via the vagus nerve.',
   'The gut microbiome produces neurotransmitters including about 95% of the body''s serotonin, significant amounts of GABA, and short-chain fatty acids that cross the blood-brain barrier. A 2021 Stanford study by Wastyk et al. found that a high-fermented-food diet increased microbiome diversity and decreased inflammatory markers more effectively than a high-fiber diet over 10 weeks. Bidirectional communication means gut bacteria influence mood, anxiety, and cognition — and stress alters gut microbiome composition in return. The vagus nerve (running from brainstem to gut) is the major communication highway: 80% of its fibers carry signals from the gut to the brain, not the reverse. Psychobiotics — probiotics with mood-modulating effects — are an active research area, though clinical evidence remains preliminary.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0006-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'The Maillard Reaction: Why Cooked Food Is Brown and Delicious',
   'The Maillard reaction — a chemical reaction between amino acids and reducing sugars — is responsible for the brown color and complex flavors of seared meat, toasted bread, roasted coffee, and baked cookies. It is not caramelization, which is a different process involving only sugars.',
   'Louis-Camille Maillard first described the reaction in 1912. It occurs at temperatures above approximately 140–165°C and produces hundreds of different flavor compounds simultaneously — the specific mixture depends on the amino acid composition of the food and the temperature and duration of cooking. Caramelization, by contrast, involves sugar decomposing alone (without protein) at even higher temperatures (160–180°C depending on the sugar). The Maillard reaction is pH-sensitive: alkaline conditions (such as treating pretzels with lye before baking) accelerate it, producing the characteristic dark, glossy crust. High-moisture cooking (boiling, steaming) prevents the reaction because water keeps surface temperatures at 100°C — why boiled chicken never browns.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0007-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Sugar Has Only Been Widely Affordable for 200 Years',
   'For most of human history, sugar was an expensive luxury. In medieval Europe it was sold by the gram at apothecaries as a medicine or spice. The industrialization of sugar production in the 18th–19th centuries collapsed its price and transformed the global diet.',
   'The price collapse came in two phases. First, Caribbean plantation agriculture — powered by enslaved labor — massively expanded cane sugar supply in the 17th and 18th centuries, dropping prices enough for European middle-class use. Second, Napoleon''s promotion of beet sugar (to break the British naval blockade on cane imports) and subsequent mechanization extended sugar access to the poor. By 1900 sugar was a pantry staple; by 1950 the processed food industry was engineering product palatability around it. Global per-capita sugar consumption rose from roughly 2 kg per year in 1800 to over 20 kg today. The obesity and metabolic disease epidemics of the late 20th century are inseparable from this transformation — a 200-year economic shift that changed what humans eat more profoundly than any culinary trend.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0008-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Why Fast Food Tastes the Same Everywhere',
   'Major fast food chains achieve flavor consistency across thousands of locations through industrialized food science: standardized ingredient formulations, precisely calibrated processing, and added flavoring compounds that override natural variability.',
   'Natural ingredients — potatoes, beef, chicken — vary enormously in flavor depending on variety, season, farming conditions, and freshness. Fast food supply chains eliminate this variability through commodity standardization (specific potato varieties for specific fry textures) and by adding flavor compounds to restore the intended taste profile regardless of ingredient quality. Eric Schlosser''s "Fast Food Nation" documented how a small number of flavor companies (located in New Jersey''s "Flavor Corridor") design the majority of fast food taste experiences. Chicken nuggets, for example, are designed to a target flavor specification using added chicken flavors, salt, and fat ratios. The consistency is a competitive advantage — consumers know exactly what they are getting — but it also means the flavor is optimized for palatability rather than nutrition.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0009-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Emulsification: Why Oil and Water Can Mix',
   'Emulsification — stabilizing a mixture of oil and water using an emulsifier — is fundamental to foods like mayonnaise, vinaigrette, butter, ice cream, and milk. Without emulsifiers, oil and water separate immediately due to the hydrophobic effect.',
   'Emulsifier molecules have a hydrophilic (water-loving) head and a hydrophobic (oil-loving) tail. They position themselves at the interface between oil and water droplets, with the tail in the oil droplet and the head facing the water, preventing droplets from coalescing. Lecithin (found in egg yolks) is the emulsifier in mayonnaise and hollandaise sauce. Mono- and diglycerides are added to commercial ice cream to improve texture. Milk is a natural emulsion of fat globules in water, stabilized by casein proteins. The stability of an emulsion depends on droplet size (smaller = more stable), emulsifier concentration, and temperature. High shear mixing (vigorous whisking, blending) creates smaller droplets; this is why well-emulsified vinaigrette requires energetic whisking.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0010-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Coffee: The World''s Second Most Traded Commodity',
   'Coffee is one of the world''s most valuable traded commodities — behind only crude oil. Over 2.25 billion cups are consumed daily worldwide. The global supply chain spans more than 50 producing countries and supports the livelihoods of approximately 125 million people.',
   'The journey from Ethiopian highlands to global commodity happened in distinct steps. Coffee spread from Ethiopia to Yemen, where Sufi monks used it to sustain nighttime prayers; Ottoman coffeehouses became centers of intellectual and political life by the 16th century. European coffeehouses — nicknamed "penny universities" because anyone could enter for the price of a cup — incubated major institutions: Lloyd''s of London started in a coffeehouse; the London Stock Exchange emerged from Jonathan''s Coffee House. Today two species dominate: Arabica (Coffea arabica) commands the specialty market for its complex flavor, while Robusta (C. canephora) is cheaper, higher in caffeine, and used in espresso blends and instant coffee. Despite the industry''s scale, commodity prices on the ICE exchange are notoriously volatile — smallholder farmers, who grow roughly 70% of the world''s coffee, frequently receive a fraction of the retail price regardless of which country they''re in.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0011-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Color Affects Taste Perception',
   'Food color significantly influences perceived flavor, sweetness, and quality. Studies consistently show that people taste and rate food differently based on color — even when the actual taste is identical.',
   'Charles Spence''s crossmodal research at Oxford has documented extensive color-flavor interactions. In one classic study, participants given white wine that had been colored red with a flavorless dye described it using red wine flavor vocabulary — the visual cue overrode the gustatory signal. Pink drinks are perceived as sweeter than clear ones of identical composition. Green food is associated with freshness; brown food with ripeness or decay. McDonald''s and Starbucks both design cup and packaging colors to influence perceived flavor of their beverages. The food industry exploits this extensively: salmon is often dyed to achieve the expected pink color (wild salmon get their color from astaxanthin in their diet; farmed salmon do not naturally produce this pigment at sufficient levels).',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0012-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Umami Synergy: Why Some Flavors Are Intensely Savory',
   'Umami — the fifth basic taste, discovered by Kikunae Ikeda in 1908 — is produced by glutamate and nucleotides (IMP and GMP). When glutamate-rich and nucleotide-rich foods are combined, umami intensity multiplies dramatically through synergy.',
   'Kikunae Ikeda identified glutamic acid (as monosodium glutamate, MSG) as the active compound producing the distinctive savory taste of kombu dashi. He named it umami (roughly: delicious taste). Inosinate (IMP) in meat and fish and guanylate (GMP) in dried mushrooms synergize with glutamate: a combination of MSG and IMP produces umami intensity roughly 8 times greater than either alone at the same concentration. This explains why classic flavor combinations work so well: tomatoes (high glutamate) + Parmesan (high glutamate and IMP) = intensely savory; anchovy + tomato paste; soy sauce + dashi; miso + seaweed. The synergy is an evolutionary signal for protein-dense foods — foods containing both amino acids and nucleotides are typically highly nutritious.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0013-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Salt Was Once Used as Currency',
   'The word "salary" derives from the Latin salarium, believed to reference salt payments given to Roman soldiers. Salt was one of the first traded commodities in human history — scarce, essential for food preservation, and valuable enough to anchor trade routes, tax systems, and empires.',
   'Because salt was the primary means of food preservation before refrigeration, cities and trade routes were established near salt sources or coastlines. The Saharan salt trade saw slabs of Saharan rock salt exchanged weight-for-weight with gold across sub-Saharan Africa — one of medieval Africa''s most important commercial networks. Salt taxes have triggered major historical confrontations: the French gabelle was one of the most despised elements of the ancien régime, fueling revolutionary grievances; Gandhi''s Salt March in 1930 was a deliberately chosen symbolic assault on the British colonial salt monopoly — a tax that hit the poor hardest. The industrial era made salt so cheap that its monetary significance vanished entirely; it is now one of the least expensive food commodities by weight, a complete reversal of its historical status.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0014-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'How Bread Rises: Yeast, Gluten, and Gas',
   'Bread rises because yeast ferments carbohydrates and produces carbon dioxide gas, which inflates pockets in a stretchy gluten network formed by wheat proteins. Without either component — the yeast or the gluten — bread cannot rise properly.',
   'Gluten is formed when two wheat proteins (glutenin and gliadin) hydrate and cross-link during kneading, creating a viscoelastic network that can stretch around CO₂ bubbles without breaking. Yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) ferments sugars in the dough, producing CO₂ and ethanol; the ethanol evaporates during baking. Sourdough bread uses wild yeasts and lactobacilli: the bacteria produce lactic and acetic acids that give sourdough its characteristic tang and longer shelf life. The Maillard reaction and caramelization occurring at the surface during baking produce the crust''s color and flavor. Gluten-free bread uses alternative proteins (from eggs, psyllium husk) to mimic gluten''s structural role — but these alternatives cannot fully replicate gluten''s extensibility, which is why gluten-free bread has a different texture.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0015-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'The Spice Trade: When Spices Were Worth More Than Gold',
   'For centuries, spices — pepper, nutmeg, cloves, cinnamon — were among the most valuable commodities in the world, worth more by weight than gold in some transactions. Control of the spice trade motivated European exploration, colonialism, and war.',
   'Medieval European demand for Eastern spices (for food preservation, medicine, and flavor) was enormous, but the supply chain passed through multiple Arab, Persian, and Venetian middlemen, inflating prices enormously. A pound of nutmeg was reportedly worth seven fat oxen in 14th-century Germany. The search for a direct sea route to the "Spice Islands" (the Maluku archipelago in present-day Indonesia) motivated Portuguese and Spanish exploration: Vasco da Gama reached India in 1498; Magellan''s circumnavigation was partly about finding a western route. The Dutch VOC (founded 1602) achieved near-monopoly control of nutmeg production by enforcing brutal restrictions on cultivation elsewhere and destroying competitor crops. The spice trade''s profitability collapsed in the 19th century as colonial powers spread cultivation globally, demonstrating how geographic monopoly, not inherent value, had sustained spice prices.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0016-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Food Safety and the Cold Chain',
   'The cold chain — the continuous refrigeration of food from production to consumption — transformed what humans can safely eat, where food can be produced relative to where it is consumed, and how long food remains safe. It is one of the most significant food technology innovations in history.',
   'Before mechanical refrigeration (commercially viable by the 1880s), protein foods (meat, fish, dairy) had to be consumed locally and quickly, preserved through salt, smoke, drying, fermentation, or pickling. Mechanical refrigeration allowed transcontinental and then international food trade: Argentine beef to Britain, New Zealand lamb to Europe. The global food system today depends entirely on an unbroken cold chain: a single break in refrigeration allows bacteria (particularly Salmonella, Listeria, and E. coli O157:H7) to multiply exponentially. The "danger zone" (4–60°C) is where bacterial growth is fastest. HACCP (Hazard Analysis Critical Control Points), developed for NASA''s space food program, is now the standard food safety framework globally. Cold chain infrastructure is a significant economic barrier for developing countries seeking to access global food markets.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0017-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Why We Like Sweet and Fatty Foods',
   'Humans have a strong innate preference for sweet and fatty foods that evolved over millions of years when calories were scarce and energy-dense foods were rare and valuable. In a modern environment of caloric abundance, these preferences work against health.',
   'Sweetness signals the presence of simple carbohydrates — quick energy. Fat signals caloric density — concentrated stored energy. Salt signals minerals. Bitterness signals potential toxins — a protective aversion. These preferences are not cultural choices but hardwired neural responses: newborns prefer sweet tastes before any food experience. The mismatch between evolutionary food preferences and modern food environments is the core explanation for the obesity epidemic: our bodies respond to processed food engineered for palatability as though it were a rare caloric windfall, driving overconsumption. The food industry invests heavily in identifying the "bliss point" — the precise combination of sugar, fat, and salt that maximizes palatability and consumption, documented by Michael Moss in "Salt Sugar Fat."',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0018-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'How Alcohol Is Made: Fermentation Without Oxygen',
   'Alcohol (ethanol) is produced by yeast fermenting sugars in the absence of oxygen — anaerobic fermentation. This process has been practiced for at least 9,000 years and is the basis for beer, wine, sake, and distilled spirits.',
   'In anaerobic conditions, yeast (primarily Saccharomyces cerevisiae) cannot use oxygen to fully oxidize glucose through respiration. Instead it ferments, converting glucose to pyruvate, then to acetaldehyde, then to ethanol — yielding 2 ATP per glucose rather than the 36–38 ATP from aerobic respiration. Natural yeast tolerance for alcohol varies but typically caps at 12–15% before the yeast is killed by its own waste product — which is why naturally fermented wines rarely exceed 15% ABV. Distillation (exploiting the different boiling points of ethanol and water) concentrates alcohol beyond this limit, enabling spirits of 40–95% ABV. Beer fermentation uses malted barley (grain treated to activate amylase enzymes that convert starch to fermentable sugar before yeast is added); wine uses the natural sugars already present in grape juice.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0019-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'The History of Chocolate',
   'Chocolate was consumed for approximately 3,000 years before Europeans added sugar to it. The Olmec, Maya, and Aztec drank cacao as a bitter, often spiced beverage used in ritual, medicine, and elite consumption — the sweet solid bar was an industrial invention that came nearly 3,500 years later.',
   'The Spanish brought cacao to Europe in the 16th century, initially as a medicine; sugar was gradually added to make it palatable, and chocolate houses became fashionable in 17th-century England. The transformation into modern chocolate required two industrial breakthroughs in close succession: Coenraad van Houten''s 1828 hydraulic press separated cocoa butter from cocoa solids, creating both Dutch-process cocoa powder and the precondition for solid chocolate; J.S. Fry & Sons created the first moldable chocolate bar in 1847 by recombining the press''s outputs with sugar. Henri Nestlé and Daniel Peter then added condensed milk in 1875, creating milk chocolate and establishing the flavor profile that dominates the market today. The modern industry depends heavily on West African cacao — roughly 70% of world supply comes from Côte d''Ivoire and Ghana — with persistent documentation of child labor and deforestation in those supply chains.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0020-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'How Tea Became a Global Commodity',
   'Tea was drunk in China for thousands of years before becoming a global commodity that shaped empires, wars, and cultural rituals. The British addiction to tea in the 18th century drove the Opium Wars, the Boston Tea Party, and the transformation of Indian and Sri Lankan agriculture.',
   'Tea was well established in China by the Tang Dynasty (618–907 CE) and reached Japan via Buddhist monks. European trade companies began importing Chinese tea in the 17th century; by the 18th century it was Britain''s defining beverage. The British East India Company''s tea trade created a massive trade deficit with China, which the company addressed by selling Indian-grown opium to China — triggering the Opium Wars (1839–1842, 1856–1860). Britain also covertly transferred Chinese tea plants and expertise to India (Robert Fortune''s 1848–1851 industrial espionage mission) to break China''s tea monopoly — establishing the Assam and Darjeeling tea industries. The American colonies'' rejection of tea taxation ("no taxation without representation") and the Boston Tea Party of 1773 contributed directly to the Revolutionary War. Today tea is the second most consumed beverage in the world after water.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0021-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Factory Farming: Cost, Scale, and Ethics',
   'Factory farming — the intensive confinement of animals at industrial scale — reduced the real price of meat by roughly 75% over the 20th century. It also concentrates animals at densities that create systematic animal welfare concerns, antibiotic resistance risks, and environmental costs.',
   'The intensification of livestock production began in earnest in the 1950s, driven by post-war demand, cheap grain, and the discovery that antibiotics given to animals as growth promoters dramatically accelerated growth rates. Today approximately 80% of US land animals raised for food live in factory farms. Concentrated Animal Feeding Operations (CAFOs) create several systemic problems: antibiotic use at growth-promotion doses accelerates evolution of resistant bacteria (the WHO considers this a top global health threat); manure lagoons at scale create significant methane and nitrous oxide emissions; water use is enormous. The ethical debate centers on whether the cognitive and social capacities of pigs, chickens, and cattle are sufficient to make confinement a form of significant suffering. Peter Singer''s "Animal Liberation" (1975) and subsequent philosophical work have pushed these questions into mainstream bioethics.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0022-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'The French Paradox',
   'The French paradox — the observation that French people have relatively low rates of cardiovascular disease despite a diet high in saturated fat — puzzled researchers for decades. The explanation involves red wine, portion sizes, meal culture, and the limits of the saturated fat hypothesis itself.',
   'Serge Renaud introduced the term in a 1991 60 Minutes segment, attributing the French advantage to red wine''s resveratrol content. Resveratrol subsequently failed to show benefits in human clinical trials. More likely explanations: the French eat smaller portions; they eat more slowly in longer, more formal meals with less snacking; the saturated fat hypothesis (that dietary saturated fat directly causes heart disease) is itself more nuanced than once believed — type of fat, overall diet quality, and refined carbohydrate consumption all matter. The French also eat more fermented dairy (cheese, yogurt), which may have different metabolic effects than other saturated fat sources. Additionally, the "paradox" may partly reflect data artifacts — French mortality coding historically undercounted cardiovascular deaths. The case remains instructive about the limitations of single-nutrient thinking in nutrition science.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0023-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'How MSG Became Falsely Demonized',
   'Monosodium glutamate (MSG) is one of the most tested food additives in history and consistently found safe. The idea that it causes "Chinese Restaurant Syndrome" — headaches, palpitations, and numbness — originated from a 1968 letter to a medical journal and has never been confirmed in blinded clinical trials.',
   'Robert Ho Man Kwok published a letter in the New England Journal of Medicine in 1968 describing symptoms after eating at Chinese restaurants, speculating MSG might be responsible. The term "Chinese Restaurant Syndrome" entered popular consciousness. Subsequent double-blind studies (where neither participant nor researcher knew who received MSG) consistently failed to reproduce the effect: people who claimed MSG sensitivity could not distinguish MSG from placebo at typical food concentrations. The FDA classifies MSG as "generally recognized as safe" (GRAS). Glutamate is one of the most abundant amino acids in the human body and in the diet — present in high quantities in Parmesan, tomatoes, soy sauce, and mushrooms. The MSG scare has elements of xenophobia — Chinese food was specifically stigmatized while Italian, Japanese, and American foods containing equivalent glutamate levels were not. It is a textbook case of nocebo effect and media-amplified health misinformation.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Philosophy ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0004-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Trolley Problem: Why It Matters Beyond the Obvious',
   'The trolley problem — divert a runaway trolley to kill one person instead of five — is not really about trolleys. It is a tool for isolating our intuitions about the difference between killing and letting die, and between intended and foreseen harms.',
   'Philippa Foot introduced the basic trolley scenario in 1967; Judith Jarvis Thomson developed the "footbridge" variant in 1985 — where instead of diverting the trolley, you must push a large man off a bridge to stop it with his body. Most people accept diverting (5 saved, 1 killed) but reject pushing (same arithmetic, different action). This asymmetry reveals a real moral distinction that theories must explain: deontologists say pushing uses the man as a means; consequentialists struggle to explain why the numbers are the same but intuitions differ. Neuroscientific research (Joshua Greene) found that "personal" violence (like pushing) activates emotional brain regions more than "impersonal" harm (like diverting), suggesting moral intuitions blend emotional responses with reasoning. The trolley problem''s value is not in answering what to do but in revealing the structure of our moral thinking.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0005-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Plato''s Cave and Epistemic Humility',
   'Plato''s Allegory of the Cave — prisoners in a cave who mistake shadows on the wall for reality — is a thought experiment about the gap between appearance and reality, and about the philosopher''s duty to return to the cave after seeing the light.',
   'In Book VII of the Republic, Socrates describes prisoners chained in a cave, able only to see shadows cast by objects passing before a fire behind them. Released, a prisoner would initially be blinded by the sun (representing the Form of the Good); eventually understanding it is the source of all light and truth. The allegory addresses multiple philosophical problems simultaneously: epistemology (how we distinguish knowledge from belief); metaphysics (the Platonic theory that Forms, not physical objects, are most real); and political philosophy (the philosopher-king who returns to govern the cave despite personal preference for the realm of Forms). The cave metaphor has had remarkable staying power: The Matrix, virtual reality, and social media filter bubbles are all modern cave analogies.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0006-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Epistemology: How Do We Know What We Know?',
   'Epistemology — the branch of philosophy concerned with the nature, scope, and limits of knowledge — asks what distinguishes knowledge from mere belief, what justifies beliefs, and whether knowledge of the external world is even possible.',
   'The traditional analysis of knowledge is "justified true belief": you know P if P is true, you believe P, and you are justified in believing P. Edmund Gettier destroyed this definition in a famous 3-page 1963 paper with counterexamples: cases where you have justified true belief but clearly not knowledge (e.g., you look at a stopped clock that happens to show the correct time). The Gettier problem has never been fully solved, leading to decades of attempted revisions. Descartes'' foundationalist project — building certain knowledge from indubitable foundations ("I think, therefore I am") — faced skeptical challenges: the evil demon scenario asks whether anything beyond my own existence can be known. Contemporary epistemology grapples with social dimensions of knowledge: testimony, peer disagreement, and the reliability of scientific consensus.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0007-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Hume''s Problem of Induction',
   'David Hume demonstrated in the 1730s that inductive reasoning — inferring general principles from observed cases — cannot be rationally justified. The sun has risen every day in recorded history, but this gives us no logical guarantee it will rise tomorrow.',
   'The problem exposes a circularity at the heart of empirical knowledge. Justifying induction requires the "uniformity of nature" assumption — that the future will resemble the past. But the only evidence for that assumption is itself inductive (the future has resembled the past so far). Every attempt to justify induction non-circularly runs into this wall. Kant found the threat serious enough to restructure his entire epistemology around it, arguing that the mind imposes causal structure on experience rather than deriving it inductively. Karl Popper''s response was to abandon induction altogether: science does not confirm theories, it attempts to falsify them — a deductive rather than inductive process. But Popper''s falsificationism faces the Duhem-Quine problem: no experiment targets a single hypothesis in isolation; auxiliary assumptions are always involved, making clean falsification impossible. The problem remains genuinely unsolved, which is philosophically significant because the entire edifice of empirical science rests on inferential moves that cannot be formally justified.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0008-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Simulation Hypothesis',
   'Nick Bostrom''s 2003 trilemma argues that at least one of these must be true: civilizations go extinct before developing simulation technology; advanced civilizations choose not to run simulations of their ancestors; we are almost certainly living in a simulation.',
   'Bostrom''s argument is probabilistic: if any civilization ever achieves the capacity to run large-scale ancestor simulations and chooses to do so, the number of simulated beings would vastly outnumber biological beings — making it overwhelmingly likely that any randomly selected conscious being is simulated. Philosophers take the argument seriously not because it proves we are simulated, but because it exposes how little we know about the metaphysical status of our experience. Elon Musk and others have publicly claimed the probability we are not in a simulation is very low. Critics (Zoë Signorelli, Scott Aaronson) point out that the computational requirements for a high-fidelity simulation of physics may be literally infinite, making the scenario physically impossible regardless of technological development.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0009-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Moral Relativism vs. Moral Realism',
   'Moral relativism holds that moral claims are only true relative to individuals or cultures — there are no universal moral facts. Moral realism holds that some moral claims are objectively true regardless of what anyone believes. Both positions have serious philosophical problems.',
   'Moral relativism faces the tolerance paradox: if morality is relative, then the claim "tolerance is good" is itself just a cultural preference — and there is no basis for criticizing intolerant cultures. It also struggles with moral progress: if slave societies were not objectively wrong, only different, then abolition was not progress. Moral realism must explain what kind of thing moral facts are and how we come to know them — moral facts are not observable like physical facts, raising questions about their metaphysical status (G.E. Moore''s "non-natural properties"). Constructivism (Rawls, Korsgaard) offers a middle path: moral truths are neither discovered in the fabric of the universe nor mere preferences, but are constructed through procedures of rational agreement.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0010-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Gödel''s Incompleteness Theorems',
   'Kurt Gödel proved in 1931 that in any consistent formal system powerful enough to express arithmetic, there exist true statements that cannot be proven within that system. Mathematics cannot prove its own completeness.',
   'Gödel''s First Incompleteness Theorem: for any consistent formal system F strong enough to encode arithmetic, there is a statement G (Gödel''s sentence) that is true but unprovable in F. G essentially says "this statement is not provable" — if G were provable, F would be inconsistent; if G is unprovable, it is true but unprovable. The Second Incompleteness Theorem: F cannot prove its own consistency. This devastated Hilbert''s program — the project of establishing mathematics on a complete and consistent axiomatic foundation. It also has deep implications for computation: Alan Turing''s halting problem (the impossibility of a program that determines whether any given program will halt) is closely related. Gödel''s results show there are fundamental limits to what formal systems can prove — not due to technical limitations, but to the nature of formal systems themselves.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0011-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Socratic Method and Why Socrates Was Dangerous',
   'Socrates wrote nothing. We know him through Plato''s dialogues, where he relentlessly questions Athenian citizens, exposing the contradictions in their beliefs about justice, virtue, and knowledge. In 399 BCE he was tried and executed for corrupting the youth and impiety.',
   'The Socratic method — elenchus — involves asking a series of questions that draw out the interlocutor''s assumptions, expose contradictions, and gradually push toward clearer thinking. It is not Socratic to lecture; it is Socratic to question. Socrates claimed to know nothing ("the wisest is he who knows he knows nothing"), but his questioning consistently left confident experts unable to define the concepts they worked with daily (generals who couldn''t define courage; politicians who couldn''t define justice). This was profoundly destabilizing to Athenian society — Socrates undermined the authority of those whose status depended on appearing knowledgeable. The charge of "corrupting the youth" reflected genuine political anxiety about his influence on young aristocrats like Alcibiades and Critias, who later proved disastrous for Athens.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0012-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Pragmatism: Truth as What Works',
   'Pragmatism — developed by Charles Sanders Peirce, William James, and John Dewey in America in the late 19th century — holds that the meaning and truth of an idea is determined by its practical consequences. A belief is true if it works, if it helps us navigate experience successfully.',
   'Peirce''s original version was about meaning: the meaning of a concept is exhausted by its practical effects — there is no meaningful difference that makes no practical difference. James extended this to truth: true beliefs are those that are useful, that allow us to predict and control experience successfully. Dewey developed it into a philosophy of education and democracy: knowledge is a tool for solving problems, not a mirror of an independent reality. Pragmatism is often misunderstood as crude "whatever works, goes" relativism. More carefully, it means that truth is not a property ideas have independently of human experience and inquiry — it is something that happens to ideas when they are confirmed in experience. Richard Rorty revived pragmatism in the late 20th century, connecting it to critiques of foundationalism and the philosophical tradition''s "mirror of nature" metaphor.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0013-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Compatibilism: Free Will and Determinism Can Coexist',
   'Compatibilism is the position that free will and determinism are not contradictory. Most contemporary philosophers are compatibilists: what matters for free will is not whether our actions are causally determined, but whether they flow from the right kind of internal process.',
   'The compatibilist move is to redefine what "free" means in a way that survives causal determination. Acting freely, on this view, means acting without coercion, acting from one''s own desires and considered reasons, and being responsive to evidence and argument — not acting from causes that originate entirely outside yourself (compulsion, manipulation, addiction). A person who acts from their reflectively endorsed values is "free" even if their character was shaped by genetics and upbringing. Harry Frankfurt refined this with his account of second-order desires: we are free when our second-order desire (what we want to want) aligns with our first-order motivation. P.F. Strawson argued from a different angle: concepts like praise, blame, and moral responsibility are grounded in our reactive attitudes toward each other, and these practices are not undermined by determinism — they operate in a different register from causal explanation. Incompatibilists counter that compatibilism simply redefines "freedom" without addressing whether anyone could ever have done otherwise, which is the question that matters for genuine moral responsibility.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0014-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Problem of Evil',
   'The problem of evil is the challenge of reconciling the existence of God (omniscient, omnipotent, omnibenevolent) with the existence of evil and suffering. It remains the most discussed challenge to theism in philosophy of religion.',
   'The logical problem of evil (J.L. Mackie): an omnipotent God could create a world without evil; an omnibenevolent God would want to; an omniscient God would know how to. Since evil exists, God does not. The free will defense (Alvin Plantinga): God could not create free beings and guarantee they never choose evil — freedom requires the possibility of choosing evil. Natural evil (earthquakes, disease) is harder to explain via free will; some theodicies appeal to "soul-making" (Irenaeus, John Hick) — suffering is necessary for moral and spiritual development. The evidential problem of evil (William Rowe) is weaker: gratuitous evil (the suffering of animals before humans existed; the death of innocent children) is evidence against a perfect God, even if not a logical disproof. Skeptical theism responds: we cannot judge whether apparent gratuitous evil is truly gratuitous, given our cognitive limitations.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0015-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Utilitarianism and Its Counterintuitive Implications',
   'Utilitarianism — the view that the right action is the one that maximizes overall well-being — is both intuitively compelling (of course we should care about consequences) and generates results that most people find deeply counterintuitive.',
   'Jeremy Bentham and John Stuart Mill founded classical utilitarianism. Its appeal: it is impartial (everyone''s wellbeing counts equally), consequentialist (outcomes matter), and provides a decision procedure. Its problems: utility monster objection (if someone gets vastly more pleasure than everyone else from resources, they should get all resources); organ harvesting objection (if killing one healthy person to harvest organs would save five, utilitarianism seems to require it); the experience machine objection (Nozick: if a machine could give you perfect simulated happiness, should you plug in? Most say no — suggesting we care about more than just experiences). Preference utilitarianism (Peter Singer) substitutes preference satisfaction for pleasure; rule utilitarianism follows the rules that tend to maximize utility rather than individual calculations.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0016-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Deontology: Why Intentions Matter More Than Outcomes',
   'Deontological ethics holds that some actions are intrinsically right or wrong regardless of their consequences. Immanuel Kant''s categorical imperative — "act only according to that maxim whereby you can at the same time will that it should become a universal law" — is the most influential deontological framework.',
   'Kant held that morality derives from reason, not consequences. The categorical imperative has multiple formulations: (1) Universalizability: act only on maxims you could will to be universal laws — lying is wrong because universal lying would make communication impossible and thus self-defeating. (2) Humanity formula: treat humanity, whether in your own person or that of another, always as an end, never merely as a means. This forbids using people as tools for our purposes. Deontology''s strengths: it captures the intuition that some things are wrong regardless of consequences (torture, slavery); it grounds human dignity. Weaknesses: it can generate absurd results when rules conflict (Kant notoriously argued you must not lie to a murderer asking where your friend is hiding); consequentialists argue that in the end, what makes rules good is their good effects.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0017-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Hard Problem of Consciousness',
   'The "hard problem" of consciousness — coined by David Chalmers in 1995 — asks why physical processes in the brain give rise to subjective experience at all. Why is there something it is like to see red, feel pain, or taste coffee? Science explains mechanism, not experience.',
   'The "easy problems" of consciousness (Chalmers''s term — they are only "easy" by contrast) include explaining attention, memory, perception, and behavioral control — all functional questions science can in principle address. The hard problem is why any of this functioning is accompanied by experience — qualia, the subjective "what it''s like." Even a complete neuroscientific account of how the brain processes color information would not explain why processing is accompanied by the experience of seeing red. Physicalists respond that the hard problem will dissolve when neuroscience is complete — experience just is certain kinds of physical processing. Property dualists (Chalmers) hold that consciousness is a fundamental feature of reality not reducible to physics. Panpsychism — the view that consciousness is fundamental to the universe — has attracted renewed philosophical attention as an alternative to both materialism and substance dualism.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0018-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Virtue Ethics: The Good Life Over Good Acts',
   'Virtue ethics — originating with Aristotle — focuses not on what action is right but on what kind of person one should be. The central question is not "what should I do?" but "what kind of person should I become?" Virtues are stable character dispositions that constitute human flourishing (eudaimonia).',
   'Aristotle listed virtues as the mean between extremes: courage is the mean between cowardice and recklessness; generosity between miserliness and profligacy. Virtue is not innate but acquired through practice — you become courageous by repeatedly doing courageous acts. The Aristotelian tradition was revived in 20th-century philosophy by Elizabeth Anscombe, Philippa Foot, and Alasdair MacIntyre, partly as a reaction to the perceived sterility of utilitarian and Kantian frameworks. MacIntyre''s "After Virtue" (1981) argued that modern moral philosophy is incoherent because we have lost the teleological framework (human nature aimed at a telos, or end) in which virtue language originally made sense. Virtue ethics'' contemporary appeal lies in its attention to character, motivation, practical wisdom (phronesis), and the psychology of moral development — elements that rule-based theories neglect.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0019-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Veil of Ignorance: Rawls''s Thought Experiment',
   'John Rawls''s veil of ignorance asks: what principles of justice would rational people choose if they did not know their place in society — their class, race, talents, gender, or conception of the good? This "original position" is designed to generate fair principles.',
   'Rawls argues that behind the veil, rational people would choose two principles: first, equal basic liberties for all (freedom of speech, conscience, association); second, inequalities are only justified if they benefit the least well-off members of society (the difference principle). The difference principle implies that a society where the worst-off have more is preferable to one with higher average welfare but greater inequality — a significant departure from utilitarian thinking. Robert Nozick''s libertarian critique: justice in distribution depends on how holdings came about (entitlement theory), not their pattern; Rawls''s principles would require constant redistribution that violates individual rights. Communitarian critics (Michael Sandel, Michael Walzer) argued that the "unencumbered self" behind the veil is an abstraction that ignores how community and identity are constitutive of who we are.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0020-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Nihilism vs. Absurdism',
   'Nihilism holds that life has no inherent meaning or value. Absurdism — developed by Albert Camus — agrees that the universe offers no inherent meaning, but insists on living fully in defiance of this fact rather than accepting meaninglessness or inventing false meaning.',
   'Nietzsche''s nihilism was a diagnosis, not an endorsement: the "death of God" (the collapse of the Christian metaphysical framework that had provided Western values) threatens to pull values down with it — leading to passive nihilism (despair) or reactive nihilism (violence). Nietzsche''s response was the will to power and the revaluation of values — creating new values rather than inheriting them. Camus, in "The Myth of Sisyphus" (1942), posed the only serious philosophical question: given that life is absurd (the gap between human longing for meaning and the universe''s silence), why not commit suicide? His answer: one must imagine Sisyphus happy — embrace the struggle itself as sufficient. Existentialism (Sartre) adds: existence precedes essence; we are condemned to be free, to create our own meaning through radical choice.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0021-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Philosophy of Time: Is the Past Real?',
   'The philosophy of time asks whether past, present, and future exist equally (eternalism), whether only the present exists (presentism), or whether the past and present exist but the future does not yet (growing block). Physics complicates all these views.',
   'Eternalism (the block universe): all moments in time exist equally — there is no privileged "now," and the passage of time is an illusion. This view is suggested by special relativity: simultaneity is relative to reference frame, so there can be no absolute present moment shared by all observers. Presentism holds that only the present exists — past events have ceased to be, future events have not yet come to be. It faces severe challenges from relativity''s relativity of simultaneity. Growing block theory: past and present are real, but the future is open. McTaggart''s A-theory/B-theory distinction: A-theory holds that temporal properties (past, present, future) are fundamental; B-theory holds only temporal relations (earlier/later than) are fundamental. The A-theory better captures our experience of time''s passage; B-theory is better supported by physics.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0022-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Animal Rights: What Grounds Moral Consideration?',
   'The philosophical question of animal rights is not whether animals are exactly like humans, but whether the morally relevant properties — sentience, the capacity to suffer — are unique to humans. Peter Singer and Tom Regan argue they are not.',
   'Peter Singer''s utilitarian argument: since animals can suffer, their suffering counts equally in the utilitarian calculus. Causing animal suffering for human pleasure (factory farming, most animal experimentation) cannot be justified on utilitarian grounds. Tom Regan''s rights-based argument: animals who are "subjects of a life" — who have beliefs, desires, memory, an emotional life — have inherent value and thus rights that cannot be violated for aggregate benefit. The "marginal cases" argument challenges the consistency of those who grant rights to all humans regardless of cognitive capacity (infants, severely cognitively disabled individuals) but deny them to animals with equivalent or higher cognitive capacities. Traditional responses appeal to species membership, rational agency, or social contract — but each of these exclusions can be challenged on consistency grounds.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0023-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Self Is an Illusion',
   'Both Buddhist philosophy and contemporary neuroscience converge on the view that the unified, persistent self is a construction rather than a discovery. There is no homunculus — no little person in the brain experiencing experience. There is only the process.',
   'Buddhist anattā (non-self) doctrine holds that what we call "the self" is a conventional designation for a collection of constantly changing physical and mental processes — form, sensation, perception, mental formations, and consciousness. The self that seems to persist is a narrative construction, not a metaphysical entity. David Hume reached a similar conclusion empirically in the 18th century: introspecting for a "self" beyond perceptions and thoughts, he could not find one. Contemporary neuroscience corroborates this: the sense of unified conscious selfhood is a construction of the brain, arising from the integration of distributed processing streams. Antonio Damasio''s research shows that the "self" depends on continuous body-state representations; Thomas Metzinger''s "Being No One" argues the phenomenal self-model is a transparent simulation the brain constructs to regulate behavior — not a thing discovered but a tool deployed.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Culture ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('ffffffff-000f-0004-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Average Person Checks Their Phone 96 Times a Day',
   'Research by Asurion (2019) found Americans check their phones an average of 96 times per day — roughly once every 10 minutes. Smartphone use has fundamentally altered attention, social interaction, and the experience of boredom in a historically unprecedented way.',
   'The Pew Research Center documented that 31% of Americans report being "almost constantly" online as of 2021. Jean Twenge''s research (iGen, 2017) correlates smartphone adoption among teenagers with rising rates of depression, anxiety, and loneliness starting around 2012 — the year smartphone penetration hit 50%. The mechanism may be social comparison: social media enables constant upward comparison with curated presentations of others'' lives. Tristan Harris''s Center for Humane Technology argues that apps are designed with persuasive technology deliberately engineered to maximize engagement — variable reward schedules (like slot machines) through notifications, likes, and infinite scroll. The design features that maximize engagement are not accidental; they are the product of deliberate optimization for time-on-platform, which is correlated with advertising revenue.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0005-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Why Nostalgia Functions as a Psychological Buffer',
   'Nostalgia — a wistful longing for the past — is not mere sentimentality. Research shows it functions as a psychological resource: it raises self-esteem, increases social connectedness, and buffers against existential threats like loneliness and meaninglessness.',
   'Constantine Sedikides and colleagues have conducted extensive experimental research showing nostalgia is typically a positive experience — most nostalgic memories involve social events (holidays, friendships, family gatherings) and carry net positive affect despite elements of longing. Nostalgia is triggered by negative affect (loneliness, cold temperatures, meaninglessness) and counteracts these states by providing a sense of continuity and social connection. In a famous study, participants induced to feel lonely showed higher nostalgia; nostalgia increased their perception of social support. Commercially, nostalgia is aggressively exploited: reboot culture (reboots, remakes, sequels to beloved franchises) is partly driven by nostalgia''s reliable emotional response. The nostalgia industry is estimated to be worth hundreds of billions of dollars globally.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0006-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Cultural Capital: Bourdieu''s Theory of Social Status',
   'Pierre Bourdieu introduced the concept of cultural capital to explain how social inequality is reproduced through non-economic means. Cultural capital — familiarity with "legitimate" culture, educational credentials, refined tastes — is as powerful as economic capital in determining social outcomes.',
   'Bourdieu identified three forms of cultural capital: embodied (dispositions, ways of speaking, mannerisms — acquired in childhood and nearly impossible to fake); objectified (cultural goods: paintings, books, instruments); and institutionalized (educational credentials that formally recognize cultural competence). Cultural capital converts into social capital (networks, relationships) and back into economic capital. The concept explains why elite reproduction is so resilient: children of highly educated parents acquire embodied cultural capital through socialization at home, giving them systematic advantages in educational institutions that reward this familiarity — making meritocracy partly a mechanism for reproducing inherited cultural advantage. Bourdieu''s "Distinction" (1984) documented French society''s cultural hierarchies empirically, showing taste in music, food, art, and sport correlated strongly with class position.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0007-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Internet Created the First Global Youth Culture',
   'The internet has created the first genuinely global youth culture — where teenagers in Seoul, Lagos, and São Paulo share the same memes, music, fashion trends, and cultural references simultaneously. This is historically unprecedented.',
   'Before the internet, cultural diffusion was slow, partial, and mediated by national gatekeepers (record labels, TV networks, publishers). American cultural influence spread globally through Hollywood and pop music, but with significant delays and national adaptation. The internet collapsed these delays and disintermediated national gatekeepers. K-pop''s global success is paradigmatic: BTS achieved simultaneous fandom on every continent not through Western gatekeepers but through YouTube, Twitter, and fan networks that are inherently global. TikTok trends originate in any country and spread globally within days. This creates genuine cultural convergence — shared reference points across previously separate cultures — but also significant concerns about cultural homogenization and the dominance of platform algorithms (primarily designed in America and China) in determining which culture spreads.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0008-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'How Fashion Cycles Work',
   'Fashion trends historically moved in a "trickle-down" pattern — from elite designers to mass market. Today, street fashion, youth subcultures, and social media have largely inverted this: trends increasingly "trickle up" from the street to the runway, and cycle far faster than they used to.',
   'Georg Simmel described the trickle-down mechanism in 1904: elites adopt a fashion to distinguish themselves; lower classes imitate it; elites abandon it for new distinctions — an endless status arms race. The inversion began with 1960s youth culture (jeans, sneakers, band t-shirts) influencing high fashion and intensified as streetwear — rooted in skateboarding, hip-hop, and athletics — became dominant in luxury markets. Supreme''s collaboration with Louis Vuitton and Nike''s designer partnerships brought street aesthetics into the highest price tiers. Social media has compressed cycle times further: a trend that once took years to travel from underground to mainstream now takes months, sometimes weeks. The result is what critics call "fashion at the speed of culture" — and a growing sustainability crisis, with fast fashion companies producing 52 micro-collections per year and the industry responsible for roughly 10% of global carbon emissions.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0009-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Meme: Dawkins''s Original Concept',
   'Richard Dawkins coined "meme" in "The Selfish Gene" (1976) to describe the cultural equivalent of a gene — a unit of cultural transmission that replicates, varies, and competes for survival in the minds of a population. The internet meme is a specific realization of this broader concept.',
   'Dawkins''s meme was meant to illustrate that Darwinian selection is a general algorithm that can apply to any replicating information — not just biological genes. Cultural ideas, behaviors, phrases, tunes, fashions, and beliefs are memes if they can be transmitted, copied with variation, and differentially survive or die. The internet meme — image macros, formats, challenges — dramatically accelerated meme replication and mutation, making the evolutionary dynamics visible in real time: memes are remixed rapidly, with successful variants spreading virally and losing fitness as they become overexposed. Dawkins noted both pride and concern about the internet evolution of his concept: internet memes replicate with high fidelity (unlike genes, where mutation is random) because humans deliberately copy and vary them. Memetics as a formal academic discipline has not prospered, but the conceptual framework remains influential.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0010-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Printing Press Created Protestantism',
   'Martin Luther''s 95 Theses (1517) were not the first religious critique of Catholic corruption — but they were the first to benefit from the printing press. Within months, copies had spread across Germany. The Reformation is inseparable from the media technology that enabled it.',
   'Gutenberg''s printing press (c. 1440) reduced the cost of producing books by roughly 95% within decades. Luther''s Theses, originally intended for scholarly debate, were translated from Latin into German and printed in their thousands — unprecedented distribution for a theological document. Luther''s complete works were reprinted hundreds of times during his lifetime. The printing press enabled vernacular Bibles (Luther''s German Bible, Tyndale''s English Bible), which undermined the Catholic Church''s monopoly on scriptural interpretation. Elizabeth Eisenstein''s "The Printing Press as an Agent of Change" (1980) documented how print culture transformed not just religion but science (enabling cumulative, correctable knowledge), law, and politics. Elizabeth Eisenstein''s thesis has been qualified by Adrian Johns (who emphasizes how chaotic and untrustworthy early print culture was) but the causal connection between printing and Protestantism is not seriously disputed.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0011-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Why Humor Varies Across Cultures',
   'Humor is universal — all known human cultures laugh — but what is funny varies dramatically across cultures, languages, and historical periods. The mechanisms of humor (incongruity resolution, taboo violation, superiority) are universal; their content is culturally specific.',
   'The incongruity theory of humor (the most empirically supported) holds that humor arises when a mismatch between expectation and outcome is playfully resolved. The resolution must come in a safe, non-serious frame — distinguishing humor from threat. This mechanism is universal. But what counts as incongruous depends on cultural expectations; what counts as taboo depends on cultural norms. Self-deprecating humor is highly valued in British culture and often interpreted as honest modesty; in some other cultures it is read as sincere weakness or a fishing for reassurance. Sarcasm requires shared knowledge that the speaker does not mean what they say — a pragmatic competence that is culturally variable and creates frequent misunderstandings in cross-cultural communication. The "straight-faced" Finnish humor style — deadpan delivery of absurd content without any signal that it is funny — confuses many non-Finns who lack the cultural key.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0012-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Cultural Appropriation vs. Cultural Diffusion',
   'Cultural appropriation — adopting elements of a marginalized culture by members of a dominant culture, often without credit or understanding — is contested: critics see exploitation; defenders see normal cultural diffusion. The distinction involves power, context, and the nature of what is adopted.',
   'Cultural diffusion — the spread of cultural practices, ideas, and artifacts across groups — is universal and has driven much of human cultural development. The contested question is when diffusion becomes exploitative appropriation. Key factors scholars identify: whether the borrowing involves sacred or deeply meaningful practices adopted superficially; whether credit and compensation flow to the source culture; whether the appropriation occurs in a context of historical oppression or ongoing inequality. Native American headdresses worn as festival costumes are a frequently cited example: they are sacred objects with specific earned meaning in context; their Halloween use strips this meaning while the source culture receives nothing. Fashion, music, and food involve more complex cases where diffusion and exploitation blend — hip-hop''s global adoption has both spread African American culture internationally and generated revenue that often flows to non-Black producers.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0013-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Origin of the Handshake',
   'The handshake is often said to date to ancient times as a gesture of peace — showing the right hand empty, demonstrating no weapon. Its exact origin is uncertain, but it appears in ancient Greek and Roman art in contexts of treaty-making and oath-giving, and its modern equalizing form was spread by Quakers in the 17th century.',
   'The Quakers (Society of Friends) were particularly significant in popularizing the handshake as a consciously egalitarian greeting: unlike bowing, which encodes social rank, a handshake signals that two people stand as equals — a radical idea in early modern class-stratified Europe. This made it natural fit for the democratic and commercial cultures of the 18th and 19th centuries, where it became the standard greeting in business and civic life. The gesture varies considerably across cultures today: in many East Asian and South Asian contexts, bowing or pressing hands together (namaste) serves the same function; in some Middle Eastern cultures, same-gender handshakes are warm and extended while opposite-gender contact between non-intimates is avoided. COVID-19 prompted serious discussion of alternatives — the elbow bump, the nod, the Wuhan wave — but five years on, the handshake appears to be recovering its position as the default professional greeting in most Western contexts.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0014-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Liminality: How Festivals Invert Social Order',
   'Carnival, Mardi Gras, Holi, and similar festivals temporarily invert or suspend normal social hierarchies, rules, and identities. Anthropologists call this state "liminality" — a threshold condition where ordinary structure is dissolved, identities are unstable, and community bonds are renewed through shared transgression.',
   'Victor Turner, extending Arnold van Gennep''s three-phase model of rites of passage (separation → liminality → reincorporation), showed that liminal states produce "communitas" — a spontaneous, non-hierarchical solidarity that cannot exist within everyday structured social life. In carnival traditions, the specific mechanisms of inversion are consistent across cultures: masks dissolve identity and status; class hierarchies are satirized; normally forbidden behaviors (sexuality, mockery of authority) are temporarily licensed. Turner''s insight was that this inversion is structurally necessary — the experience of communitas, however brief, reminds participants that the hierarchies they live within are social constructions, not natural facts. The "safety valve" theory argues this is exactly why ruling classes historically tolerated carnival: by releasing social tension periodically, it made the return to hierarchy more tolerable. Critics (Stallybrass and White) counter that carnival spaces also generate genuine resistance and social critique that outlasts the festival — the permitted transgression creating a template for the impermissible one.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0015-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Global Spread of Jeans',
   'Blue jeans — invented by Levi Strauss and Jacob Davis in 1873 as working-class durable workwear — have become the most globally distributed garment in human history, worn by an estimated half of the world''s population daily. Their spread tracks 20th-century American cultural power.',
   'Originally designed for California gold miners needing durable trousers, jeans were workwear for decades. They became associated with youth rebellion through 1950s American culture — James Dean, Marlon Brando — and were subsequently banned in many schools and workplaces as subversive. The counterculture of the 1960s made them a symbol of anti-establishment values. Cold War Soviet youth coveted Western jeans as symbols of freedom; Levi''s were sold on Soviet black markets at enormous premiums. By the 1980s, designer jeans (Calvin Klein, Gloria Vanderbilt) had moved the garment upmarket while Levi''s retained working-class associations. Today jeans span all price points from fast fashion to $500 Japanese selvedge denim. Their global dominance illustrates how American cultural soft power — transmitted through film, music, and youth culture — can reshape clothing habits across radically different cultures.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0016-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Why Gift-Giving Norms Vary Across Cultures',
   'Gift-giving is universal across human cultures but the rules — what to give, when, how to present it, whether to open it immediately, and what giving and receiving mean — vary dramatically. Getting gift-giving wrong in a cross-cultural context can cause serious offense.',
   'Marcel Mauss''s "The Gift" (1925) argued that gift exchange is never purely voluntary or disinterested: gifts create obligations, establish hierarchies, and cement social bonds. The obligation to give, receive, and reciprocate is socially enforced. In Japan, gifts are typically wrapped elaborately (presentation matters as much as content), presented with two hands, and often not opened in the giver''s presence (to avoid expressions of disappointment or obligation). In Chinese contexts, gifts of clocks are inauspicious (the phrase "giving a clock" sounds like "attending a funeral"). Cash gifts are common and acceptable in Chinese and Korean contexts but awkward in American and British ones. In many Middle Eastern and South Asian contexts, refusing a gift is deeply offensive; in Western business contexts, gift-giving can be misread as bribery. These variations mean gift-giving is a frequent source of cross-cultural misunderstanding in international business.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0017-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Face: The Concept of Mianzi in East Asian Social Life',
   'The Chinese concept of mianzi (face) — social prestige, reputation, and dignity in the eyes of others — is a fundamental organizer of social life in China, Japan, Korea, and much of East and Southeast Asia. Losing face is a serious social injury; giving face is a powerful social gift.',
   'Face has two dimensions in Chinese social theory: mianzi (social prestige — what others think of you) and lian (moral character — whether you are considered trustworthy and ethical). Face is not an East Asian peculiarity — all human societies have concepts of reputation and dignity — but the degree to which face considerations explicitly govern social interaction, business negotiation, and conflict resolution varies significantly. In business contexts, publicly criticizing or contradicting a senior person causes them to lose face — this must be avoided even when they are clearly wrong. Disagreements and bad news are communicated indirectly, through intermediaries or in private, to preserve face. Face concerns explain negotiating behaviors that Western counterparts find mystifying: agreements that everyone knows will not be honored, because saying "no" directly causes face loss. Richard Lewis and Geert Hofstede both document this dimension as a significant source of cross-cultural business failure.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0018-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'How Sports Became Secular Religion',
   'Sports — particularly soccer, American football, cricket, and baseball — function in modern societies with striking parallels to religion: communal gathering, sacred spaces, rituals, saints (great players), martyrs, pilgrimages, and transcendent experiences of group identity.',
   'Emile Durkheim''s theory of religion emphasized not beliefs but collective rituals that create "collective effervescence" — the heightened emotional state of being part of a group larger than oneself. Sports stadiums generate exactly this: tens of thousands of people simultaneously experiencing intense shared emotion. Sports scholars (Christian Bromberger, Nick Hornby) have documented the religious dimensions: the stadium as sacred space; the game as ritual with fixed ceremonial elements; fans as a congregation sharing a collective identity. Winning creates collective pride and belonging; losing creates collective grief. Teams can serve as proxies for broader identities — national, regional, ethnic, religious. British football clubs, Brazilian samba schools, and Catalan soccer (FC Barcelona under Franco) have all served as vehicles for suppressed cultural identity. Sports provide many non-religious people with the only regular experience of transcendent collective identity available in secular society.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0019-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The History of Taboo',
   'Taboo — from the Tongan word tapu, meaning forbidden or sacred — was introduced into English by Captain Cook in 1777. Taboos are prohibitions felt as visceral and absolute rather than merely conventional, often involving dirt, sex, death, and the sacred.',
   'Mary Douglas''s "Purity and Danger" (1966) is the foundational anthropological account: taboos mark boundaries between categories. Dirt is "matter out of place" — soil in a garden is not dirt, but soil on a dinner plate is. The disgust response that enforces taboo violations is an evolved system for avoiding pathogens, extended culturally to social and moral domains. Jonathan Haidt''s moral psychology research documents the "moral dumbfounding" that occurs when people encounter taboo violations that cause no harm: they feel certain it is wrong but cannot articulate why. Classic example: "Is it wrong to have consensual sex with a dead chicken before cooking it for dinner?" Most say yes; when pressed on the harm, they insist it is "just wrong." Taboos vary enormously across cultures (incest taboos are universal but differ in who counts as kin; food taboos vary widely) while sharing the characteristic of felt absolute prohibition rather than rational rule.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0020-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Individualism vs. Collectivism: Hofstede''s Dimensions',
   'Geert Hofstede''s cultural dimensions theory, derived from a survey of IBM employees in 70 countries in the late 1960s, identified individualism-collectivism as one of the most significant dimensions of cultural variation. It predicts differences in everything from organizational behavior to mental health to moral reasoning.',
   'In highly individualist cultures (USA, UK, Australia), individual goals, rights, and identity take precedence over group membership; people are expected to speak their minds directly and personal achievement is celebrated. In highly collectivist cultures (China, Indonesia, many Latin American and African societies), group membership, loyalty, and harmony take precedence; direct confrontation is avoided; identity is defined by group roles. These are statistical tendencies, not deterministic rules — massive within-culture variation exists. Critics of Hofstede''s framework (including McSweeney, Kirkman) point out that he measured IBM employees (a highly selected sample) in a specific era and that his categories homogenize enormously diverse societies. Nevertheless, the individualism-collectivism dimension predicts real behavioral differences in negotiation, leadership, conflict resolution, mental health treatment, and legal systems.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0021-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Why Status Symbols Keep Changing',
   'Thorstein Veblen introduced "conspicuous consumption" in "The Theory of the Leisure Class" (1899): elites signal status through visible, wasteful spending. But status symbols are unstable — as soon as lower classes adopt an item, elites abandon it for new distinctions.',
   'Veblen documented how American elites in the Gilded Age spent conspicuously on items valued not for their utility but for their cost and visibility. The dynamic of status symbol adoption and abandonment is well documented: once a luxury good becomes affordable to the middle class, it loses its status function and elites move on. Luxury automakers (BMW, Mercedes) historically derived status value from their exclusivity; as they became widespread in the 1980s and 1990s, ultra-luxury brands (Bentley, Ferrari) recaptured the exclusivity premium. The internet has complicated status signaling: knowledge, cultural taste, and early adoption of trends have become status markers alongside material goods. "Quiet luxury" — expensive goods without visible logos — has emerged as a status strategy of those wealthy enough not to need visible logos. Pierre Bourdieu''s complementary concept of "distinction" shows how cultural taste hierarchies parallel economic ones.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0022-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Rise of Fandom as Identity',
   'Fandom — intense, organized enthusiasm for cultural products — has shifted from a marginal activity to a primary source of identity, community, and meaning for hundreds of millions of people. This shift has had significant cultural, economic, and political consequences.',
   'Henry Jenkins''s "Textual Poachers" (1992) rehabilitated fandom from pathology (the obsessive "fanatic") to active cultural participation: fans are producers of meaning, not passive consumers. They write fan fiction, create art, organize communities, and engage in sophisticated textual analysis of beloved works. The internet transformed fan communities from isolated local groups to global networks with significant collective power: organized fandoms have influenced casting decisions, reversed cancellations (Firefly), and mobilized millions of followers for political causes (K-pop fans claiming tickets to Trump''s Tulsa rally in 2020). Identity fusion with a fandom — where the fan experiences the fan community''s success and failures as personal — creates both intense belonging and vulnerability to collective distress when beloved franchises are threatened or changed.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0023-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Why Languages and Cultures Converge and Diverge',
   'Human cultural diversity — 7,000 languages, thousands of distinct cultural practices — coexists with powerful forces of cultural convergence. Understanding why cultures both spread and differentiate requires thinking about contact, prestige, geography, and group identity.',
   'Languages and cultures diverge through isolation (geographically separated populations develop independently), social differentiation (in-group markers distinguish groups in contact), and drift (random change accumulates over generations). They converge through contact, conquest, trade, migration, and prestige (speakers voluntarily adopt features of a prestige language or culture). English''s global spread is a convergence story driven by British colonialism and American economic and cultural dominance. At the same time, minority languages and cultures actively resist convergence as expressions of group identity — Welsh, Basque, and Hawaiian language revival movements reflect the political and psychological importance of linguistic distinctiveness. Stuart Hall''s concept of cultural hybridity — that cultures in contact produce neither pure convergence nor pure divergence but new hybrid forms — captures the dynamic better than either pure globalization or pure resistance models.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Geography ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0004-a1b2-c3d4e5f60004', 'geography', 'Half the World''s Population Lives in a Surprisingly Small Area',
   'More than half of the world''s 8 billion people live within a circle centered roughly on Bangladesh — a region covering parts of South Asia, Southeast Asia, and East Asia. This concentration reflects the agricultural productivity of monsoon-fed river deltas.',
   'A 2013 map by Valeria Favorito visualized this striking geographic concentration: drawing a circle encompassing China, India, Bangladesh, and nearby countries covers an area representing about 17% of Earth''s land surface but over 50% of its population. The Ganges-Brahmaputra, Yangtze, and Pearl River deltas are among the most agriculturally productive land on Earth, capable of supporting dense populations through intensive rice cultivation for millennia before industrialization. South and East Asia''s population concentration is not simply a product of recent growth — it reflects thousands of years of agricultural civilization enabled by reliable monsoon rainfall and fertile alluvial soils. By contrast, enormous land areas (the Sahara, Australian interior, Amazon basin, Siberia) are nearly empty. The human species is not evenly distributed across the planet but clustered where the land could support settled agriculture.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0005-a1b2-c3d4e5f60005', 'geography', 'Why Landlocked Countries Tend to Have Lower GDP',
   'Landlocked countries — those with no access to the ocean — have on average significantly lower GDP per capita and economic growth rates than coastal nations. Access to ocean trade routes is one of geography''s most powerful determinants of economic development.',
   'Jeffrey Sachs and colleagues documented that landlocked countries face a systematic "landlocked penalty" in economic development: transport costs for landlocked countries are 50% higher than for coastal countries on average, and they are dependent on the infrastructure quality and political stability of neighboring transit countries over which they have no control. Of the world''s 44 landlocked nations, approximately 30 are classified as "least developed countries" by the UN — a disproportionate concentration. Exceptions exist: Switzerland and Austria are landlocked but wealthy, showing that geography is not destiny — institutions, human capital, and location within a wealthy region can overcome the disadvantage. Mongolia, Bolivia, and Chad represent the other end of the spectrum: landlocked, surrounded by countries with limited infrastructure, and economically marginalized. The UN has created a special category (Land-Locked Developing Countries, LLDCs) with targeted development programs.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0006-a1b2-c3d4e5f60006', 'geography', 'Karst Topography: Limestone Dissolved by Acid Rain',
   'Karst landscapes — characterized by sinkholes, caves, disappearing rivers, and dramatic rocky towers — form when slightly acidic rainwater dissolves limestone bedrock over thousands of years. The result is some of Earth''s most dramatic and ecologically unique terrain.',
   'Rainwater absorbs CO₂ from the atmosphere and soil to form dilute carbonic acid (H₂CO₃). This weak acid dissolves calcium carbonate (limestone and dolomite) along fractures and joints, gradually enlarging them into caves, conduits, and ultimately large caverns. The Mammoth Cave system in Kentucky (the world''s longest known cave at over 650 km of mapped passages) is a karst system. China''s karst towers (Guilin''s limestone pillars, the Stone Forest of Yunnan) are among the world''s most dramatic landscapes. Karst aquifers — water stored in cave networks and rock fractures — supply drinking water to approximately 25% of the world''s population, but are vulnerable to contamination because water moves rapidly through large conduits without the filtration that occurs in porous sandstone aquifers.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0007-a1b2-c3d4e5f60007', 'geography', 'Lake Baikal: One Lake Holds 20% of Earth''s Fresh Water',
   'Lake Baikal in Siberia is the world''s deepest lake (1,642 m), oldest lake (25–30 million years), and holds approximately 20% of Earth''s total surface fresh water — more than all five of North America''s Great Lakes combined.',
   'Baikal holds 23,615 cubic kilometers of water — a volume that reflects both its extraordinary depth and its large surface area (31,722 km²). It formed in an ancient rift valley where the Asian plate is slowly pulling apart; the same geological process that formed the East African Rift Valley. Because of its age, Baikal hosts extraordinary endemic biodiversity: approximately 80% of its approximately 3,700 species are found nowhere else, including the Baikal seal (the world''s only freshwater seal), the nerpa, and hundreds of endemic invertebrates. The Baikal omul, a type of whitefish, is a regional delicacy and cultural symbol. Industrial pollution and climate change (the lake''s ice cover is forming later and melting earlier) threaten this unique ecosystem. Russia''s decision to close the Baikal Pulp and Paper Mill (after decades of resistance) is one of the most significant industrial environmental victories in post-Soviet history.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0008-a1b2-c3d4e5f60008', 'geography', 'Why Rivers Meander',
   'Rivers do not flow in straight lines — they develop curves (meanders) even on perfectly flat land. Meandering is the natural, energy-efficient form of river flow: slight initial curves amplify over time through erosion on outer bends and deposition on inner bends.',
   'Water flowing around a curve is deflected by centrifugal force and the Coriolis effect, creating a helical secondary circulation (helicoidal flow) that erodes the outer bank and deposits sediment on the inner bank. This positive feedback amplifies the curve: the more curved a meander becomes, the faster it curves further. Eventually meanders become so extreme that the river cuts across the narrow neck of land during a flood, abandoning the old curve as an "oxbow lake." Leopold and Langbein (1960) showed mathematically that meandering is the form a river adopts to achieve minimum variance in energy expenditure along its length — essentially a thermodynamic optimization. Channelized (straightened) rivers consistently re-meander if given the opportunity; straightening increases water velocity, causing erosion both upstream and downstream.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0009-a1b2-c3d4e5f60009', 'geography', 'Fjords: Glacially Carved Inlets',
   'Fjords are long, narrow, deep inlets carved by glaciers during ice ages and subsequently flooded by seawater as glaciers retreated. They are disproportionately deep compared to the ocean floor — Norway''s Sognefjord reaches 1,308 m depth while the adjacent continental shelf is far shallower.',
   'Glaciers carve through bedrock far more effectively than rivers because of their enormous mass and the abrasive action of rock fragments embedded in the ice base. The U-shaped cross-section of glacial valleys (compared to the V-shape of river valleys) reflects the glacier''s width and the fact that ice erodes sidewalls as well as the valley floor. When glaciers retreat, the valleys fill with seawater — and because glaciers eroded below current sea level, fjords are deeper than the surrounding sea. Salmon aquaculture (Norway, Chile, Scotland) has developed primarily in fjords because the sheltered, cold, oxygenated water is ideal for fish farms. Norway''s fjords are a major tourist attraction; the Geirangerfjord and Nærøyfjord are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. New Zealand''s Milford Sound (technically a fjord despite its name) is among the most photographed in the southern hemisphere.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0010-a1b2-c3d4e5f60010', 'geography', 'The Green Sahara: When the Sahara Was Wet',
   'Between approximately 11,000 and 5,000 years ago, the Sahara — today the world''s largest hot desert — was a savanna with lakes, rivers, crocodiles, hippos, and human settlements. It was greener than most of Europe is today. This period is called the African Humid Period or Green Sahara.',
   'The Green Sahara was driven by orbital forcing: changes in Earth''s axial tilt and orbital precession shifted the peak of Northern Hemisphere summer to occur when Earth was closest to the sun (perihelion), intensifying summer heating of the African continent. This strengthened the West African monsoon, pushing rains deep into what is now the Sahara. Rock art and archaeological sites throughout the Sahara — in Algeria, Libya, Chad, and Mali — document cattle herding, fishing communities, and images of animals that now exist only in sub-Saharan Africa. Lake Chad was then a vast inland sea (Mega-Chad) roughly the size of the Caspian Sea. The abrupt end of the African Humid Period around 5,500 years ago — likely a threshold response to gradually changing orbital parameters — may have pushed populations toward the Nile Valley, contributing to the conditions for the emergence of Egyptian civilization.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0011-a1b2-c3d4e5f60011', 'geography', 'Island Biogeography: Why Islands Develop Unique Species',
   'Islands are natural experiments in evolution and ecology. Isolated from mainland gene pools, island species evolve in distinctive directions — often losing the ability to fly, growing larger or smaller than mainland relatives, and displaying remarkable tameness toward predators they have never encountered.',
   'MacArthur and Wilson''s "The Theory of Island Biogeography" (1967) predicted species richness on islands based on two variables: distance from the mainland (affecting immigration rate) and island area (affecting extinction rate). Their equilibrium model has been remarkably predictive and spawned an entire field (metapopulation biology). Island species evolve under different selective pressures: without large mammalian predators, birds lose flight (the dodo, New Zealand''s moa); without competition from mainland species, animals can exploit ecological niches unavailable elsewhere; island dwarfism produces miniature elephants (Flores island, Sicily) when large animals must survive on limited resources. Island species are disproportionately vulnerable to extinction when humans arrive with rats, cats, and pigs — approximately 75% of documented bird extinctions have been island species. Hawaii''s native bird fauna has been largely destroyed by introduced avian malaria, rats, and habitat destruction.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0012-a1b2-c3d4e5f60012', 'geography', 'The Isthmus of Panama and Its Global Consequences',
   'An isthmus is a narrow strip of land connecting two larger landmasses. The Isthmus of Panama — which formed approximately 3 million years ago — is one of the most consequential geological events in Earth''s recent history, separating the Atlantic and Pacific and triggering major evolutionary and climate changes.',
   'Before the isthmus formed, ocean currents flowed between the Atlantic and Pacific. The closure of this seaway redirected the Gulf Stream, strengthening it and warming the North Atlantic — a factor in enabling human habitation of northern Europe. The separation of Atlantic and Pacific marine populations triggered rapid speciation: closely related species on either side of the isthmus (geminate species pairs, like Atlantic and Pacific fish) diverged after being separated. The Great American Biotic Interchange — the exchange of species between North and South America across the newly formed land bridge — reshaped both continents'' faunas: North American horses, camels, and ground sloths moved south; South American armadillos, opossums, and ancestors of sloths moved north. The Panama Canal (1914) partially reversed this by creating a marine connection and is now managed to prevent species exchange between the two oceans.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0013-a1b2-c3d4e5f60013', 'geography', 'Manila: The Most Densely Populated City on Earth',
   'The city of Manila, Philippines, has a population density of approximately 111,000 people per square kilometer in some districts — making it the most densely populated city on Earth by area. The broader Metro Manila urban area has over 13 million people in one of Asia''s most compact megacities.',
   'Manila''s density reflects several intersecting factors: rapid rural-to-urban migration driven by agricultural poverty; a lack of affordable land for horizontal expansion; inadequate public transport encouraging settlement near employment centers; and historical urban form dating to the Spanish colonial period (1571–1898). The result is a city where thousands of informal settlement residents (barangays) live in extraordinary spatial compression, with serious consequences for public health, disaster vulnerability, and quality of life. Manila lies in a typhoon belt and on a major earthquake fault; its density makes disaster response enormously challenging. The 2009 Typhoon Ondoy (Ketsana) flooded large parts of Metro Manila, displacing hundreds of thousands. Bangladesh''s Dhaka competes for density in some metrics; in terms of consistently verified city-level density, Manila''s districts consistently rank highest globally.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0014-a1b2-c3d4e5f60014', 'geography', 'The International Date Line and Why It''s Not Straight',
   'The International Date Line — the meridian (approximately 180°) where one calendar day ends and the next begins — is not a straight line. It zigzags to keep island nations, countries, and territories on the same calendar day as their political or economic partners.',
   'If the date line followed the 180th meridian exactly, it would bisect Russia''s Chukotka region and Alaska''s Aleutian Islands, putting parts of each on different dates from the rest of their country. The line zigs to the east to keep eastern Russia on the same day as western Russia, and to the west to keep the western Aleutians on Alaskan time. Kiribati moved the date line in 1995 to keep all its widely scattered islands on the same calendar day — making it the easternmost nation in the world (geographically) to observe the earliest date (politically). Samoa and American Samoa are on opposite sides of the date line despite being only 70 km apart — a legacy of 19th-century trade alignments (Samoa switched sides in 2011). The date line has no fixed physical basis; it is a human convention that could be drawn differently.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0015-a1b2-c3d4e5f60015', 'geography', 'How Mountains Form: Plate Tectonics and Collision',
   'Mountains form primarily through tectonic plate collisions, where continental crust — too buoyant to subduct — crumples and thickens upward. The Himalayas are still rising because India continues to collide with Eurasia at roughly 4–5 cm per year.',
   'When two oceanic plates collide, one subducts beneath the other, creating a deep ocean trench and a chain of volcanic islands (island arc). When an oceanic plate collides with a continental plate, the oceanic plate subducts, and the continental margin experiences volcanic activity (the Andes were formed this way). When two continental plates collide, neither easily subducts — instead, the crust thickens and rises, forming fold mountains. The Himalayan-Tibetan plateau (the "roof of the world") formed this way: India, once part of Gondwana, broke away and collided with Asia beginning about 50 million years ago. The Tibetan Plateau averages over 4,500 m in elevation across an area the size of Western Europe — a consequence of doubled crustal thickness. Mountain erosion shapes global climate by affecting atmospheric circulation and by chemically weathering silicate rocks, which absorbs CO₂ over geological timescales.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-12'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0016-a1b2-c3d4e5f60016', 'geography', 'Watersheds: Every Raindrop Has a Destination',
   'A watershed (or drainage basin) is the area of land that drains into a particular river or body of water. Every point on Earth''s surface belongs to a watershed. Understanding watersheds is fundamental to water management, ecosystem health, and understanding how human activities in one place affect water quality elsewhere.',
   'The Mississippi River watershed drains approximately 3.2 million km² — about 40% of the contiguous United States — gathering water from as far west as Montana and as far east as New York before delivering it to the Gulf of Mexico. Anything applied to the land within a watershed (fertilizers, pesticides, road salt, motor oil) has a potential pathway to the watershed''s outlet. Nitrogen runoff from Midwestern agriculture travels the entire Mississippi system to create a hypoxic "dead zone" in the Gulf of Mexico each summer — one of the world''s largest. Watershed thinking has transformed environmental management: policies focused on point-source pollution (specific discharge pipes) were replaced by non-point-source frameworks that address diffuse runoff across entire watersheds. The concept is also fundamental to understanding how deforestation causes flooding — without forest root systems holding soil and regulating water release, rainfall rushes to rivers instead of slowly percolating.',
   'fact', '2026-07-13'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0017-a1b2-c3d4e5f60017', 'geography', 'Why Monsoons Occur',
   'Monsoons — seasonal reversals of wind direction that bring alternating wet and dry seasons — are driven by the differential heating of land and sea. They are not simply heavy rains but a fundamental circulation pattern that governs agriculture, water supply, and the lives of billions across South Asia, Southeast Asia, West Africa, and Australia.',
   'Land heats and cools faster than water. In summer, the Asian landmass heats rapidly, creating a low-pressure zone. Moist air from the Indian Ocean flows inland toward this low, rising as it encounters land, cooling, and releasing enormous amounts of rain — the South Asian summer monsoon. In winter, the land cools faster, creating a high-pressure zone, and dry air flows outward to the sea — the dry season. The South Asian monsoon delivers 70–90% of India''s annual rainfall in 4 months (June–September). Agricultural and economic calendars across South Asia are organized around the monsoon''s onset and withdrawal. A delayed or weak monsoon causes drought and food insecurity; an unusually intense monsoon causes catastrophic flooding. The monsoon is sensitive to sea surface temperatures (El Niño weakens it; La Niña strengthens it) and is one of the climate systems most consequential for human welfare.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0018-a1b2-c3d4e5f60018', 'geography', 'Permafrost: The Frozen Carbon Bomb',
   'Permafrost — ground that remains at or below 0°C for at least two consecutive years — underlies approximately 25% of the Northern Hemisphere''s land surface. It stores an estimated 1.5 trillion tonnes of carbon — roughly twice the amount currently in the atmosphere — and it is thawing.',
   'Permafrost formed over thousands of years as organic matter (dead plants and animals) accumulated in frozen ground where decomposition is nearly impossible. Arctic warming (occurring at 2–4 times the global average rate) is thawing the active layer (the surface layer that thaws seasonally) and in some areas, deeper permafrost. As permafrost thaws, microbial decomposition resumes, releasing CO₂ and methane (a more potent but shorter-lived greenhouse gas). This creates a positive feedback loop: warming thaws permafrost, releasing greenhouse gases, causing further warming. The Arctic Monitoring and Assessment Programme (AMAP) estimates that permafrost thaw could release enough carbon to raise global temperatures by an additional 0.3–0.5°C by 2100 — on top of emissions from fossil fuel use. Thawing also causes ground subsidence (thermokarst), damaging buildings, roads, and pipelines across Siberia, Alaska, and Canada.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0019-a1b2-c3d4e5f60019', 'geography', 'The Ring of Fire: Volcanoes and Earthquakes Around the Pacific',
   'The Ring of Fire — a horseshoe-shaped zone around the Pacific Ocean — contains approximately 75% of the world''s active volcanoes and generates roughly 90% of the world''s earthquakes. It marks the boundaries of the Pacific Plate and several smaller plates.',
   'The Pacific Plate is the world''s largest oceanic plate and is subducting beneath surrounding continental and oceanic plates at multiple boundaries. Where the Pacific Plate dives beneath others, oceanic crust melts as it descends into the mantle, generating magma that rises to form volcanic arcs and explosive volcanoes (Krakatoa, Mount St. Helens, Pinatubo, Fuji). The Cascadia Subduction Zone (off Washington and Oregon) is capable of generating a magnitude 9+ megathrust earthquake that would devastate the Pacific Northwest. Japan sits on the intersection of four tectonic plates — the Pacific, Philippine, Eurasian, and North American plates — which is why it has the world''s most sophisticated earthquake preparedness infrastructure. The 2011 Tōhoku earthquake (M9.0) and resulting tsunami killed approximately 16,000 people and triggered the Fukushima nuclear disaster.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0020-a1b2-c3d4e5f60020', 'geography', 'How Ocean Currents Regulate Global Climate',
   'Ocean currents — driven by wind, temperature, salinity, and Earth''s rotation — redistribute heat around the planet, making otherwise uninhabitable regions livable. Without the Gulf Stream, northwestern Europe would be as cold as Labrador, Canada, which shares similar latitudes.',
   'The thermohaline circulation ("thermohaline" = heat + salt) is a global conveyor belt of ocean currents driven by density differences: cold, salty water is denser and sinks; warm, fresh water is lighter and floats. In the North Atlantic, warm surface water flows north (the Gulf Stream / North Atlantic Current), warming Western Europe; when it reaches the Arctic, it cools, becomes denser, and sinks to the deep ocean, flowing southward. This Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) is showing signs of weakening due to freshwater influx from melting Greenland ice, which reduces salinity and density in the North Atlantic, inhibiting sinking. A significant AMOC slowdown (unlikely but possible) would cool Western Europe substantially, offset warming there, and alter rainfall patterns globally. The 2004 film "The Day After Tomorrow" dramatized this scenario with characteristically scientific liberties.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0021-a1b2-c3d4e5f60021', 'geography', 'The Geography of Language',
   'Political borders and linguistic boundaries rarely align. Most of the world''s 7,000+ languages are not associated with a nation-state, and most nation-states contain speakers of multiple languages. The mismatch between political and linguistic geography is a source of persistent conflict and cultural tension.',
   'The ideal of one nation, one language — powerful in 19th-century European nationalism — rarely matches linguistic reality. Belgium is split between Dutch-speaking Flanders and French-speaking Wallonia (plus a German-speaking community), creating persistent constitutional crises. Nigeria has over 500 indigenous languages; its choice of English as the official language is a practical compromise that advantages those with access to English education. In post-colonial states across Africa, Asia, and the Pacific, the colonial language (English, French, Portuguese) often serves as the language of government and education because it is politically neutral between competing ethnic groups — no ethnic group can claim it as "their" language. Language geography is also a power map: the 23 languages spoken by 50 million or more people collectively serve as the first language of most of the world''s population, while the remaining 7,000+ languages are spoken by relatively small communities, many under severe assimilation pressure.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-18'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0022-a1b2-c3d4e5f60022', 'geography', 'Megacities: When Urban Areas Exceed 10 Million',
   'A megacity is an urban agglomeration with more than 10 million inhabitants. In 1950 there were two (New York and Tokyo). Today there are approximately 35, the majority in Asia. By 2030, there will be approximately 43, and they will house a growing share of humanity.',
   'Megacity growth is concentrated in the developing world: Delhi, Dhaka, Cairo, Lagos, Kinshasa, and Karachi are all growing rapidly, some by hundreds of thousands of residents per year. Tokyo remains the world''s largest megacity at approximately 37 million people — a functioning, relatively well-served city that is an existence proof that megacities need not be ungovernable. By contrast, many rapidly growing megacities in the global south lack adequate water, sanitation, transport, housing, and employment infrastructure for their populations. The informal sector (unregulated small businesses, informal settlements) absorbs much of this growth. UN projections show virtually all future world population growth occurring in cities, and virtually all of that in the less developed regions. The governance challenge of megacities — coordinating across multiple municipalities, balancing growth with services, managing climate vulnerability — is one of the defining policy problems of the 21st century.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0023-a1b2-c3d4e5f60023', 'geography', 'Why the Global South Is Larger Than Maps Suggest',
   'Standard world maps — based on the Mercator projection — dramatically inflate the size of landmasses in high latitudes (Europe, North America, Russia) and shrink equatorial regions (Africa, South America). Africa is actually 14 times the size of Greenland, not roughly comparable as Mercator maps suggest.',
   'The Mercator projection (1569) was designed for navigation: it preserves angles (so compass bearings are straight lines), which is essential for maritime navigation. As a side effect, it distorts area dramatically at high latitudes. On a Mercator map, Greenland appears similar in size to Africa; in reality, Africa (30.4 million km²) is approximately 14 times larger than Greenland (2.2 million km²). The Peters projection (now called the Gall-Peters projection) uses equal area but distorts shape. The Winkel Tripel projection minimizes both area and shape distortion and is preferred by National Geographic. The political dimension: many observers note that Mercator''s distortions disproportionately inflate the apparent size of wealthy Northern Hemisphere countries while shrinking Africa, South America, and South Asia — literally centering the Global North in the world''s mental map. Arno Peters explicitly framed his equal-area map as a political argument for equitable representation of the Global South.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Nature ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('cccccccc-000c-0004-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'The Honeybee Waggle Dance Is a Navigation System',
   'When a forager bee returns to the hive after finding food, it performs a precise figure-eight waggle dance that encodes the direction and distance to the source. Other bees decode the dance and fly directly to the location.',
   'The angle of the waggle run relative to vertical encodes the direction from the sun; the duration encodes distance (each second of waggling represents roughly 1 km). Karl von Frisch decoded the dance in the 1940s and received the Nobel Prize in 1973. The dance is accurate enough that bees can navigate to a food source 6 km away. Bees also perform tremble dances (to recruit processors) and shaking signals (to alert hive mates). The dance language is not instinctive in detail — bees raised in isolation cannot interpret it correctly, suggesting a cultural learning component.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0005-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Why Forests Need Fire to Stay Healthy',
   'Fire-dependent ecosystems — including much of the American West, Australian bush, and African savanna — evolved with regular low-intensity fires that clear undergrowth, recycle nutrients, and trigger seed germination. A century of fire suppression has made these ecosystems more vulnerable, not safer.',
   'Many tree species require fire for reproduction: lodgepole pine cones are serotinous — they remain sealed with resin and only open to release seeds when heated by fire. Without periodic burning, forest floors accumulate decades of fuel, turning low-intensity surface fires into catastrophic crown fires. The 1988 Yellowstone fires burned 36% of the park but recovered so quickly that ecologists now consider them beneficial. Indigenous land management in Australia used controlled burning (cultural burning) for 50,000+ years to maintain healthy mosaics of vegetation. Modern fire policy in many regions has shifted from pure suppression to prescribed burns, though the backlog of fuel buildup makes transition difficult.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0006-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Keystone Species',
   'A species whose impact on its ecosystem is disproportionately large relative to its abundance. Remove a keystone species and the entire ecosystem restructures — sometimes collapsing. The sea otter is the textbook example.',
   'Robert Paine coined the term in 1969 after removing ochre sea stars from a tidal pool and watching mussel populations explode, crowding out all other species. Sea otters are keystone predators of sea urchins: without otters, urchins overgraze kelp forests into "urchin barrens." With otters, kelp forests thrive, supporting hundreds of species and sequestering carbon. Wolves in Yellowstone are another keystone: their reintroduction in 1995 triggered a trophic cascade that changed river courses (via vegetation recovery on banks). The concept has practical conservation value — identifying keystones lets managers prioritize species protection for maximum ecosystem benefit.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0007-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Octopuses Have Three Hearts and Distributed Brains',
   'An octopus has three hearts (two pump blood to the gills, one to the body), blue blood (copper-based hemocyanin instead of iron-based hemoglobin), and two-thirds of its neurons in its arms — which can act semi-independently even when severed.',
   'The octopus nervous system is radically decentralized: each arm has its own neural cluster that can process sensory information and initiate movement without consulting the central brain. A severed arm continues to respond to stimuli for up to an hour. Octopuses solve puzzles, open childproof containers, use tools (carrying coconut shells for shelter), and recognize individual human faces. Their intelligence evolved independently from vertebrate intelligence — a striking example of convergent evolution. Octopus neurons are more similar in structure to mammalian neurons than to those of other mollusks. Their genome contains 33,000 genes (more than humans) and undergoes extensive RNA editing that may explain their behavioral flexibility.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0008-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Plants Actively Defend Against Herbivores',
   'When a caterpillar bites a tomato leaf, the plant releases airborne chemical signals that neighboring plants detect — triggering them to produce defensive compounds before they are attacked. Plants are not passive victims.',
   'Plants produce an arsenal of anti-herbivore chemicals: tannins that reduce protein digestibility, alkaloids (caffeine, nicotine, morphine) that are toxic to nervous systems, and protease inhibitors that block digestive enzymes. Willows and poplars release volatile methyl salicylate when attacked — neighboring trees absorb it through their leaves and upregulate their own defenses within hours. Some plants recruit the predators of their attackers: maize plants infested with beet armyworm caterpillars release volatiles that attract parasitic wasps that lay eggs in the caterpillars. The potato plant detects mechanical damage and can distinguish between damage by a caterpillar (which triggers defense) and mechanical wounding (which does not), suggesting something like herbivore recognition.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0009-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Mutualism',
   'A relationship between two species in which both benefit. Unlike competition (both lose something) or parasitism (one benefits at the other''s expense), mutualism is a form of biological cooperation that has driven major evolutionary innovations.',
   'Mutualism comes in obligate forms (neither partner can survive without the other) and facultative forms (both benefit but can survive alone). The mitochondria in every eukaryotic cell are the descendants of bacteria engulfed by ancient cells in a mutualistic merger 1.5 billion years ago. Mycorrhizal fungi colonize plant roots and exchange mineral nutrients (especially phosphorus) for plant sugars — a relationship that 90% of land plants depend on. Cleaner fish remove parasites from larger fish that could easily eat them; the relationship is stable because cheating destroys future cleaning opportunities. Clownfish live among anemone tentacles that kill other fish; they provide food scraps and aeration while the anemone provides protection.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0010-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Slime Molds Solve Mazes Without a Brain',
   'Physarum polycephalum, a single-celled organism (technically not a mold), can find the shortest path through a maze, optimize transport networks, and even anticipate regular events — all with no neurons whatsoever.',
   'In a famous 2010 experiment, Toshiyuki Nakagaki fed slime mold oat flakes placed in a map of the greater Tokyo area and found that the organism''s nutrient channels spontaneously recreated a network almost identical to the Tokyo rail network — optimized for efficiency, redundancy, and load distribution. The organism solves the shortest-path problem by expanding in all directions simultaneously, then withdrawing tubes that carry less flow, reinforcing those that carry more. Transport engineers have adapted the slime mold algorithm for designing fault-tolerant networks. The organism can also be conditioned: after experiencing regular shocks every 30 minutes, it begins to respond defensively before the next expected shock — a form of anticipatory behavior with no nerve cells.',
   'insight', '2026-07-07'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0011-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'How Birds Navigate by Earth''s Magnetic Field',
   'Many migratory birds have a magnetic sense that allows them to detect Earth''s magnetic field with remarkable precision. The mechanism involves quantum effects in eye proteins — one of the few confirmed examples of quantum biology in animal behavior.',
   'Birds likely use two systems: magnetite crystals in the beak (providing a map sense of absolute field strength) and cryptochrome proteins in the retina (providing a compass sense). The cryptochrome mechanism uses quantum entanglement: light activates electron pairs in cryptochrome molecules, creating "radical pairs" whose spin states are influenced by magnetic fields. The bird may literally see the magnetic field overlaid on its visual image. Experiments with European robins show that disrupting the magnetic sense with radio waves (at the Larmor frequency for radical pairs) eliminates their ability to orient — strong evidence for the quantum mechanism. Some species can also navigate by stars (learned as chicks by observing the rotation of the night sky) and by smell.',
   'fact', '2026-07-08'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0012-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Trophic Cascade',
   'The indirect effects that ripple through a food web when a top predator is added or removed. The classic example: wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone in 1995, and within years had changed the behavior of rivers.',
   'Without wolves, elk grazed heavily along riverbanks, preventing tree regrowth. Wolves changed elk behavior (elk avoided river valleys) more than elk numbers — "the ecology of fear." Within a decade, willows, aspens, and cottonwoods recovered along riverbanks. Beavers returned (they need willows), creating ponds that slowed erosion and raised water tables. Songbird diversity increased. River banks stabilized, changing channel geometry. The phrase "wolves change rivers" captures the cascade. Marine trophic cascades are even more dramatic: the depletion of large sharks from many ocean regions has caused cascading collapses of fish populations multiple trophic levels below them. Trophic cascades demonstrate that ecosystems have nonlinear dynamics — small changes at the top can restructure everything below.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0013-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'The Humongous Fungus Is the Largest Known Organism',
   'A honey fungus (Armillaria ostoyae) in Oregon''s Malheur National Forest covers 2,385 acres (965 hectares) and is estimated to be 8,650 years old — making it the largest known living organism by area and one of the oldest.',
   'The organism exists mostly underground as a network of root-like mycelium, with mushrooms (the reproductive fruiting bodies) appearing only seasonally at the surface. The fungus is a pathogen — it kills trees by strangling their roots — and has killed much of the forest above it. Genetic testing confirmed that the entire network shares the same DNA, proving it is a single organism rather than multiple related fungi. Across the world, fungi dominate the soil ecosystem: a teaspoon of healthy forest soil contains several miles of fungal mycelium. Mycelium networks are far older and more extensive than the forest above them — when a tree dies, the fungal network it was connected to can persist for centuries, potentially connecting to new trees.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0014-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Mangroves Store More Carbon Than Tropical Rainforests',
   'Mangrove forests store 3-5 times more carbon per hectare than tropical rainforests — mostly in deep, waterlogged soils where decomposition is extremely slow. They are among the most carbon-dense ecosystems on Earth.',
   'Mangroves grow at the interface of land and sea in tropical and subtropical regions, their roots holding the coastline together while filtering pollutants and providing nursery habitat for 80% of tropical fish species. Their carbon-dense soils accumulate organic matter that would decompose rapidly if exposed to air — when mangroves are cleared for shrimp farming or coastal development, centuries of stored carbon are released. Globally, about 50% of mangrove forests have been destroyed since 1940 — a loss rate faster than tropical deforestation. Mangrove restoration projects are increasingly recognized as among the most cost-effective carbon capture interventions available, with the added benefits of coastal protection and fisheries recovery.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0015-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Whale Fall Creates a Deep-Sea Ecosystem',
   'When a whale dies and sinks to the ocean floor, its carcass supports a succession of ecosystems for up to 100 years — feeding scavengers, then enrichers, then chemosynthetic bacteria that sustain life without sunlight.',
   'A large whale carcass (whale fall) first attracts mobile scavengers — sharks, hagfish, crabs — that strip the flesh within months. Then enrichment opportunists (bristle worms, crustaceans) colonize the bones and surrounding sediment enriched by whale fats. Finally, sulfophilic bacteria colonize the oil-rich bones, producing hydrogen sulfide through anaerobic decomposition. Chemosynthetic bacteria that use this sulfide to produce energy — the same process that powers deep-sea hydrothermal vent ecosystems — attract tubeworms, clams, and mussels in the final stage. The species found at whale falls overlap substantially with those at hydrothermal vents, supporting the "stepping-stone hypothesis" that whale falls provided dispersal corridors for vent species across the ocean floor over evolutionary time.',
   'fact', '2026-07-12'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0016-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Ant Colonies Make Collective Decisions Without a Leader',
   'An ant colony''s "intelligence" — finding food, navigating, regulating temperature, defending the nest — emerges from simple interactions between individual ants following local rules. No ant understands the colony''s goals; the colony understands them collectively.',
   'Ants communicate primarily through pheromones: a forager that finds food lays a pheromone trail on the way back to the nest. Other ants follow the trail and reinforce it if the food is still there. Shorter paths are reinforced more (ants return faster), so the colony converges on the shortest route — the same mechanism as ant colony optimization algorithms used in logistics. Temperature regulation in the nest is a collective behavior: no ant monitors the whole nest, but each responds to local temperature by moving eggs or fanning at the entrance. Army ant raids involve 200,000+ individuals acting as a coordinated unit with no communication beyond pheromone trails. The colony''s behavior emerges from the interactions of individually simple agents — a paradigmatic example of emergence.',
   'insight', '2026-07-13'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0017-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Deep-Sea Hydrothermal Vents Support Life Without Sunlight',
   'Discovered in 1977, deep-sea hydrothermal vents host thriving ecosystems powered entirely by chemosynthesis — bacteria converting sulfur compounds into energy, with no dependence on solar energy. This overturned the assumption that all life depends on the sun.',
   'Before 1977, biologists assumed all ecosystems ultimately traced their energy to photosynthesis. The discovery of tube worms, clams, shrimp, and crabs thriving around 350°C vents 2,500 meters deep — without sunlight — was revolutionary. The base of the food chain is chemosynthetic bacteria living inside the tissues of tube worms (which have no digestive system) and other animals in a symbiotic relationship. Some vent animals have evolved hemoglobins that can carry both oxygen and hydrogen sulfide simultaneously without the sulfide poisoning the oxygen-carrying function. The discovery expanded the definition of the habitable zone for life and immediately raised the possibility of life around hydrothermal vents on ocean moons like Europa and Enceladus.',
   'fact', '2026-07-14'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0018-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'How Coral Reefs Build Themselves — and Why Bleaching Kills Them',
   'Coral reefs are built by tiny animals (polyps) that secrete calcium carbonate skeletons. The polyps are only alive because of symbiotic algae (zooxanthellae) living in their tissues. When ocean temperatures rise, the algae are expelled — this is bleaching, and without the algae, the polyps starve.',
   'Zooxanthellae provide 90% of the coral''s energy through photosynthesis and give coral its color; stressed polyps expel them as a stress response, turning white (bleaching). If temperatures return to normal within weeks, zooxanthellae can be reacquired and the coral recovers. If bleaching is prolonged, the coral dies. The Great Barrier Reef experienced mass bleaching events in 2016, 2017, 2020, 2022, and 2024 — the bleaching events are coming faster than corals can recover. Coral reefs cover 0.1% of the ocean floor but support 25% of all marine species. Ocean acidification (from CO₂ absorption) dissolves the calcium carbonate skeleton even without bleaching. Scientists are working on heat-tolerant coral strains through selective breeding and assisted evolution — a potential lifeline for reefs under climate change.',
   'insight', '2026-07-15'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0019-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'The Nitrogen Cycle',
   'Nitrogen makes up 78% of Earth''s atmosphere but most organisms cannot use it directly. The nitrogen cycle converts atmospheric nitrogen into biologically usable forms through a cascade of microbial processes — and human fertilizer production has doubled the natural rate.',
   'The cycle: nitrogen-fixing bacteria (in soil and in root nodules of legumes) convert N₂ to ammonia (NH₃). Other bacteria convert ammonia to nitrites and nitrates (nitrification) — the forms plants can absorb. When organisms die, decomposers return nitrogen to the soil. Denitrifying bacteria complete the cycle by converting nitrates back to N₂. Fritz Haber and Carl Bosch''s 1913 invention of industrial nitrogen fixation (the Haber-Bosch process) allowed synthetic fertilizer production at scale — enabling the food production that supports about half of today''s global population. The process now fixes more nitrogen than all natural processes combined, causing nitrogen pollution: algal blooms, ocean dead zones, and nitrous oxide (a potent greenhouse gas). Half of the nitrogen atoms in your body were fixed by the Haber-Bosch process.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-16'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0020-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Epigenetics: How Environment Changes Gene Expression',
   'Epigenetics studies heritable changes in gene expression that do not involve changes to the DNA sequence itself. Environmental exposures can switch genes on or off — and some of these changes can persist across generations.',
   'The mechanisms include DNA methylation (adding a methyl group to silence a gene) and histone modification (changing how DNA is packaged, making it more or less accessible for transcription). The Dutch Hunger Winter of 1944-45 — a severe famine — has been studied for 70 years: children born to mothers who were pregnant during the famine had higher rates of obesity, diabetes, and mental illness. Some effects appeared in their children and grandchildren, suggesting epigenetic transmission. Exercise, stress, diet, and toxin exposure all modify epigenetic marks. The traditional view that acquired characteristics cannot be inherited (Weismann barrier) has been revised: while germline epigenetic inheritance is limited in mammals, it is real and meaningful. Epigenetics has complicated the gene-centric view of heredity without abandoning it.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0021-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Why Reintroducing Wolves Changed Yellowstone''s Rivers',
   'After wolves were exterminated from Yellowstone in the 1920s, elk populations exploded and overgrazed riverbanks bare. When 14 wolves were reintroduced in 1995, the resulting cascade of behavioral and ecological changes — including altered river flow — became one of ecology''s most documented natural experiments.',
   'Elk didn''t just decline in number — they changed their behavior, avoiding the river valleys where wolves had tactical advantage. This "ecology of fear" allowed willows, aspens, and cottonwoods to regenerate along riverbanks. Beavers returned because willows had regenerated (beavers'' primary food), creating ponds that raised water tables and slowed river erosion. Songbirds returned to the recovering riparian forests. Stabilized riverbanks changed the channels'' meandering patterns — the rivers actually moved. The wolf reintroduction demonstrated that predators affect ecosystems as much through behavioral modification of prey as through direct predation. The Yellowstone case is now the canonical example of a trophic cascade driven by a predator — though ecologists debate how much of the change was wolves versus other factors like elk hunting and drought.',
   'insight', '2026-07-18'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0022-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'Bioluminescence: Why Organisms Produce Light',
   'An estimated 76% of deep-sea species are bioluminescent — producing light through chemical reactions. Bioluminescence has evolved independently over 40 times in nature and serves functions ranging from predator evasion to courtship to predation.',
   'Bioluminescence works via the enzyme luciferase catalyzing the oxidation of a substrate (luciferin) to produce light — a near-perfect cold light with almost no heat waste. Fireflies use it for mate signaling (each species has a species-specific flash pattern). Anglerfish dangle a bioluminescent lure to attract prey in the deep sea. Some squid can match the dim bioluminescence of the water column above them to eliminate their silhouette from predators below — counterillumination camouflage. Dinoflagellates (marine plankton) flash when disturbed, producing the eerie blue glow of bioluminescent bays. The light is typically blue-green (460-490 nm) because these wavelengths propagate farthest in seawater. Bioluminescent proteins — especially green fluorescent protein (GFP) from jellyfish — have become transformative tools in cell biology and medical research.',
   'fact', '2026-07-19'),
  ('cccccccc-000c-0023-000c-00000000000c', 'nature', 'The Deep Ocean Is Earth''s Largest Biome',
   'The deep ocean (below 200 meters) covers 71% of Earth''s surface and represents 95% of its living space by volume. Yet it remains less explored than the surface of Mars, and new species are discovered on virtually every deep-sea expedition.',
   'The deep sea is defined by darkness (no photosynthesis below ~200 m), crushing pressure (increasing by 1 atmosphere every 10 meters), cold (2-4°C in most of the abyss), and food scarcity (the only food is marine snow — organic particles drifting down from surface waters). Despite these extremes, the deep sea is biodiverse: a single trawl in the deep Atlantic can yield dozens of previously unknown species. The global ocean conveyor belt (thermohaline circulation) transports oxygen and nutrients to the deep sea — without it, the deep ocean would become anoxic. Deep-sea mining for polymetallic nodules threatens ecosystems we have barely catalogued. The ocean''s role in carbon cycling — absorbing 25% of human CO₂ emissions — is partly mediated by deep-sea biological processes we don''t yet fully understand.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Food ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('dddddddd-000d-0004-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Spicy Food Is Technically Pain, Not Taste',
   'Capsaicin, the compound in chili peppers, binds to TRPV1 pain receptors in the mouth — the same receptors that respond to actual heat. The brain receives a signal identical to burning. There is no "spicy" taste receptor; spicy is a pain sensation that humans uniquely find pleasurable.',
   'TRPV1 (transient receptor potential vanilloid 1) is a nociceptor — a pain detector — that normally activates at temperatures above 43°C. Capsaicin activates it at room temperature, tricking the brain into perceiving heat and pain. The reward of eating spicy food is thought to involve the brain''s opioid system: the pain triggers endorphin release, creating a mild euphoria. Capsaicin desensitizes TRPV1 with repeated exposure — explaining how chili tolerance develops. Capsaicin cream is a legitimate pain treatment: applied topically, it depletes substance P (a pain neurotransmitter) from nerve endings. The heat unit for capsaicin is the Scoville scale, invented by pharmacist Wilbur Scoville in 1912. Pure capsaicin rates 16 million Scoville units; the current record-holder Carolina Reaper peaks at 2.2 million.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0005-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Fermented Foods and the Gut-Brain Axis',
   'The gut contains 100 million neurons and produces 90% of the body''s serotonin. The gut microbiome — shaped substantially by fermented foods — communicates bidirectionally with the brain via the vagus nerve, influencing mood, cognition, and behavior.',
   'The "gut-brain axis" is a bidirectional communication highway: the gut sends signals via the vagus nerve, enteric nervous system neurotransmitters, and immune signals; the brain modulates gut motility, secretion, and immunity in return. Studies in germ-free mice (raised without gut bacteria) show profound behavioral differences — high anxiety, abnormal stress responses — that are reversed by transplanting microbiota from normal mice. A 2022 Stanford study (Sonnenburg lab) found that a high-fermented-food diet (yogurt, kefir, kimchi, kombucha) significantly increased microbiome diversity and decreased inflammatory markers compared to a high-fiber diet alone. Specific strains of Lactobacillus produce GABA and other neuroactive compounds. The field is still young — most human studies are observational — but the evidence for microbiome influence on mental health is growing rapidly.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0006-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Maillard Reaction',
   'The chemical reaction between amino acids and sugars that occurs when food is heated above about 140°C, producing hundreds of flavor compounds and brown color. It is responsible for the taste of seared steak, toast, roasted coffee, and baked bread crust.',
   'The Maillard reaction (named for French chemist Louis-Camille Maillard who described it in 1912) is not a single reaction but a complex cascade of hundreds of reactions producing thousands of flavor molecules. It requires both reducing sugars and amino acids, and accelerates rapidly with heat — which is why dry-heat cooking (roasting, searing, baking) produces more flavor than steaming or boiling. The specific flavor compounds produced depend on which amino acids and sugars are present: beef, bread, and coffee all undergo Maillard reactions but produce completely different aromas. The browning is distinct from caramelization (which is pure sugar decomposition without amino acids). Controlling Maillard browning is central to industrial food manufacturing: color and flavor consistency in breakfast cereals, crackers, and processed meats are all carefully engineered.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0007-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Sugar Was a Luxury for Most of Human History',
   'For the vast majority of human history, sweet foods (fruits, honey) were rare and seasonal. Refined sugar only became widely affordable in the 19th century — after which human consumption exploded, and so did rates of tooth decay, obesity, and diabetes.',
   'Sugarcane originated in New Guinea c. 8000 BCE but was confined to South and Southeast Asia for millennia. Arab traders spread it to the Mediterranean in the 7th century CE; European crusaders encountered it and called it "sweet salt." Sugar only reached Europe in significant quantities in the 1500s via Atlantic slave trade plantations in the Caribbean and Brazil — it was expensive enough to be displayed as a status symbol. In England, per-capita sugar consumption rose from 4 pounds per year in 1700 to 90 pounds by 1900, following the industrialization of refining and the abolition of sugar tariffs. Tooth decay (dental caries) was rare before widespread sugar consumption — analysis of pre-agricultural human remains finds nearly zero cavities. The Columbian Exchange brought sugar to the Americas; it also triggered one of history''s largest forced migrations via the transatlantic slave trade.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0008-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Why Fast Food Tastes the Same Everywhere',
   'The consistent taste of global fast food chains is not magic — it is the result of industrial flavor science, standardized supply chains, and rigorous quality control. "Natural flavors" on an ingredient list often represent a laboratory achievement as sophisticated as pharmaceutical synthesis.',
   'Flavor houses (companies like Givaudan, Firmenich, IFF) synthesize the hundreds of volatile compounds that constitute food aromas. A "strawberry flavor" might contain 50+ individual chemical compounds in precisely calibrated ratios. Standardization runs deep: McDonald''s French fries use a specific potato variety (Russet Burbank), grown to specific starch content, cut to specific dimensions, blanched, par-fried at precise temperatures, frozen, shipped, and fried again — all to tolerances that eliminate the variability of farm-to-table cooking. The industrial food system''s ability to deliver consistent sensory experiences globally requires controlling the entire supply chain. Eric Schlosser''s "Fast Food Nation" (2001) documented this system in detail. The tradeoff: consistency at the cost of the microbiological complexity and nutritional variation of whole foods.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0009-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Emulsification',
   'The process of combining two immiscible liquids (like oil and water) into a stable mixture, using an emulsifier molecule whose one end is attracted to water and the other to oil. Mayonnaise, vinaigrette, and milk are all emulsions.',
   'An emulsifier is an amphiphilic molecule — it has a hydrophilic (water-loving) head and a hydrophobic (oil-loving) tail. Lecithin (found in egg yolks) is the most common food emulsifier: its tails embed in oil droplets while its heads face the surrounding water, stabilizing the dispersion. Mayonnaise is an oil-in-water emulsion (oil droplets suspended in water); butter is a water-in-oil emulsion. Homogenized milk is stabilized by milk proteins. Without emulsifiers, salad dressing separates immediately; with lecithin or mustard (another emulsifier), it stays mixed. Industrial food production relies heavily on synthetic emulsifiers (polysorbate 80, carrageenan) to maintain texture in products ranging from ice cream to processed cheese. Some research suggests high doses of certain emulsifiers alter gut microbiome composition, though the clinical significance at food-level doses is debated.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0010-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Coffee Is the World''s Second Most Traded Commodity',
   'After petroleum, coffee is the world''s most traded commodity by value. Over 2.25 billion cups are consumed daily globally. The crop supports the livelihoods of 125 million people — and its production is highly vulnerable to climate change.',
   'Coffee arabica (70% of global production) originated in the highlands of Ethiopia and was first cultivated in Yemen in the 15th century. Coffee reached Europe via Ottoman traders in the 1600s, where coffeehouses became centers of intellectual and political life — the London Stock Exchange, Lloyd''s of London, and the Oxford University Press all originated in coffeehouses. The caffeine in coffee works by binding to adenosine receptors in the brain, blocking the drowsiness signal. Unlike many stimulants, caffeine does not cause dependence in the pharmacological sense, though withdrawal headaches are real (adenosine receptors upregulate during chronic use). Specialty coffee — direct-trade, single-origin, meticulously processed — has transformed the industry, with some lots selling for over $1,000/kg. By 2050, climate change is projected to reduce suitable arabica growing land by 50%.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0011-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'We Eat With Our Eyes: Color Shapes Taste Perception',
   'Color is so central to flavor perception that off-color food tastes wrong — even to experts. When white wine is colored red with an odorless dye, trained sommeliers consistently use red wine descriptors to describe it.',
   'A 2001 study by Frédéric Brochet asked 54 wine experts to describe an artificially colored white wine: they used words like "jammy," "berry," and "chocolate" — terms from their red wine vocabulary — while the actual wine was white with an odorless red dye. The brain integrates color as a prior expectation that powerfully shapes taste processing in the orbitofrontal cortex. Pink drinks are rated as sweeter and perceived as containing more calories than identically flavored colorless drinks. Green ketchup (launched by Heinz in 2000) failed largely because the color violated expectations so strongly that taste was impaired. Food manufacturers carefully calibrate color to signal freshness, ripeness, and flavor intensity. The relationship is bidirectional: we associate certain colors with certain tastes (yellow-sweet, green-sour, red-intense) through a lifetime of learned associations.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0012-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Umami Synergy',
   'When two different umami compounds — glutamate and nucleotides (IMP or GMP) — are combined, their flavor impact multiplies far beyond their additive contributions. This synergy is why combining certain ingredients produces a depth of flavor that neither ingredient achieves alone.',
   'Glutamate (from tomatoes, soy sauce, aged cheese, anchovies) and nucleotides (from meat, dried mushrooms, bonito flakes) act on the same taste receptor (T1R1/T1R3) but at different binding sites. When both are present simultaneously, the receptor''s response amplifies 8-fold compared to glutamate alone. Traditional cuisines independently discovered this principle: Japanese dashi combines kombu (glutamate-rich) with katsuobushi (IMP-rich); French bouillon combines vegetables (glutamate) with meat (IMP); Italian cooking pairs parmesan (glutamate) with prosciutto (IMP). This is why MSG (pure glutamate) added to dishes that already contain glutamate-rich ingredients has a smaller effect than adding it to dishes with both glutamate and nucleotide sources. The synergy effect was formally described by Akira Kuninaka in 1960.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0013-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Salt Was Once as Valuable as Gold',
   'The word "salary" derives from the Latin "salarium" — believed to refer to salt rations paid to Roman soldiers. Salt was so valuable as a preservative, flavor enhancer, and mineral supplement that it functioned as currency across many ancient cultures.',
   'Before refrigeration, salt was the primary method of food preservation: fish, meat, and vegetables were salt-cured to prevent bacterial and fungal growth. Salt preservation made long-distance trade, long campaigns, and urban life possible. Saharan camel caravans traded salt from the Saharan salt mines (notably Timbuktu''s Taghaza mines) weight-for-weight against gold from sub-Saharan Africa — the famous "salt for gold" trade. Venice''s maritime empire was built partly on its salt monopoly in the Adriatic. The British "salt tax" (gabelle) was a major grievance fueling the French Revolution; Gandhi''s 1930 Salt March was a deliberate challenge to British salt taxation in India. Industrial extraction and the collapse of salt scarcity mean that table salt is now one of the cheapest commodities on Earth — a reversal of its historical centrality.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0014-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'How Bread Rises: Yeast, Gluten, and Gas',
   'Bread rises because yeast ferments sugars and releases CO₂, which inflates the gluten network in the dough. The texture of bread is a direct product of this interaction between biology (yeast) and chemistry (gluten structure).',
   'Gluten forms when glutenin and gliadin proteins in wheat flour hydrate and link together, creating an elastic, extensible network that traps CO₂ bubbles. Without gluten (or a substitute), the gas escapes and the bread does not rise. Yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) consume sugars (from flour''s starch, broken down by amylase enzymes) and produce CO₂ and ethanol; the ethanol evaporates during baking. Sourdough uses wild yeast and lactic acid bacteria in a symbiotic culture; the bacteria produce lactic and acetic acids that give sourdough its characteristic flavor and improve keeping quality. The "oven spring" — the last rapid rise when bread enters the oven — happens because CO₂ and water vapor expand rapidly before the gluten network sets. Bread baking has been practiced for at least 14,400 years, predating agriculture (wild grain bread found at Shubayqa 1 site in Jordan).',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0015-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Spices Were Worth More Than Gold in Medieval Europe',
   'In the 15th century, a pound of nutmeg was worth more than several cows. European demand for Asian spices (pepper, cloves, cinnamon, nutmeg) was so intense that it directly motivated the voyages of Columbus and da Gama — and shaped the modern world.',
   'Spices were valuable primarily as preservatives and flavor enhancers for meat that was often partly spoiled (the "spice masks bad meat" explanation is oversimplified — fresh meat was preferred, but spiced preserved meat was the practical staple). Black pepper was so prized that medieval contracts were sometimes settled in peppercorns. The Spice Islands (Maluku, Indonesia) were the only source of cloves and nutmeg; the Portuguese and later Dutch fought wars and established empires specifically to control them. The Dutch VOC (East India Company) held a nutmeg monopoly so complete that they massacred or enslaved the entire population of the Banda Islands rather than let others access the trees. The subsequent European colonialism of Southeast Asia, triggered largely by spice economics, reshaped the world. Today, nutmeg costs a few dollars per pound.',
   'fact', '2026-07-12'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0016-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'The Cold Chain Transformed What Humanity Eats',
   'Refrigeration — the ability to maintain food at low temperatures through the supply chain — has been called the most important invention in the history of food. It enabled year-round availability of perishables, global food trade, and the modern supermarket.',
   'Before mechanical refrigeration (Thaddeus Lowe''s ice machine, 1865; Carl von Linde''s ammonia compressor, 1876), food preservation relied on salt, sugar, smoking, fermentation, and natural ice harvested in winter. The ice trade — shipping natural ice from New England lakes to cities worldwide — was a major 19th-century industry before mechanical refrigeration. Refrigeration enabled the Australian and Argentine beef industries (refrigerated ships from the 1870s), California''s export fruit industry, and the globalization of dairy. The modern cold chain — refrigerated trucks, warehouses, and retail display — is why a consumer in Oslo can buy Chilean strawberries in January. The energy cost is significant: refrigeration accounts for about 7% of global electricity use. Cold chain gaps in developing countries contribute substantially to food waste — roughly 40% of food in low-income countries spoils before consumption.',
   'insight', '2026-07-13'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0017-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Why We Like Sweet and Fatty Foods',
   'The human preference for sweet and fatty foods is not a modern weakness — it is an evolutionary adaptation to scarce, calorie-unpredictable environments. The brain''s reward systems evolved to strongly signal "eat more of this" when encountering rare, high-energy foods.',
   'In ancestral environments, sweet foods (ripe fruit) signaled carbohydrates; fat signaled dense calories. Both were relatively rare. The brain''s dopamine reward system releases more dopamine in response to calorie-dense foods than to low-calorie foods, independent of conscious preference. The combination of fat and sugar is particularly potent: most natural foods are either sweet or fatty, but not both simultaneously. Processed foods (ice cream, cookies, chocolate) combine fat and sugar in ratios that do not occur in nature, maximizing palatability. This "hyperpalatable" quality is engineered deliberately by food scientists. The "bliss point" (Howard Moskowitz''s term) is the precise concentration of sugar, fat, and salt that maximizes consumption. Low-calorie foods have a disadvantage in this system — the brain downregulates reward in the absence of caloric content, making diet foods feel less satisfying even when they taste similar.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0018-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'Fermentation Without Oxygen: How Alcohol Is Made',
   'Alcoholic fermentation is anaerobic — it occurs without oxygen. When yeast cannot access oxygen, they switch from aerobic respiration (which produces CO₂ and water) to fermentation, which produces ethanol and CO₂. This accident of yeast metabolism gave humanity wine, beer, and spirits.',
   'The Pasteur point is the threshold oxygen level below which yeast switch to fermentation: below 0.1-1% oxygen, yeast produce ethanol rather than fully oxidizing sugars. Wine fermentation is aerobic initially (grape juice is oxygenated), then turns anaerobic as oxygen is consumed — the transition to anaerobic conditions is what produces the alcohol rather than simply more yeast. Spirits (whiskey, vodka, gin) are produced by distilling fermented beverages to concentrate the ethanol. Distillation concentrates to a maximum of about 96% ethanol (the ethanol-water azeotrope); absolute alcohol requires additional steps. The carbon dioxide from fermentation is used commercially: the CO₂ produced by beer and wine fermentation in closed tanks is collected, purified, and sold for carbonating soft drinks. The same anaerobic pathway in human muscle cells (lactic acid fermentation) produces the burn of intense exercise.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-15'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0019-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'How Chocolate Went from Bitter Aztec Drink to Modern Candy',
   'The cacao tree (Theobroma cacao — "food of the gods") was cultivated by the Maya and Aztecs, who drank a bitter, spiced, often chilled beverage from its fermented seeds. European colonizers added sugar and heat, transforming cacao into what we now recognize as chocolate.',
   'Hernán Cortés brought cacao to Spain around 1528; sweetened hot chocolate spread slowly across Europe over the following century, becoming fashionable at courts before coffeehouses popularized it as a public drink. The transformation into solid chocolate required two industrial inventions: Conrad van Houten''s 1828 hydraulic press separated cocoa butter from cocoa solids, and Joseph Fry used this technique in 1847 to recombine the outputs with sugar into a moldable solid. Henri Nestlé and Daniel Peter added condensed milk in 1876, creating milk chocolate and establishing the flavor profile that dominates the mass market today. Modern bean-to-bar producers have reversed this industrialization: sourcing from single origins, controlling fermentation, and highlighting terroir — bringing the craft back to where mass production began stripping it away.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0020-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'How Tea Became the World''s Second Most Consumed Beverage',
   'After water, tea is the world''s most consumed beverage — over 3 billion cups daily. All true teas (black, green, white, oolong) come from one plant (Camellia sinensis); the differences arise from processing. Britain''s 19th-century tea obsession reshaped the global economy and politics.',
   'Camellia sinensis originated in the region spanning Yunnan, Burma, and Assam. Chinese legend attributes its discovery to Emperor Shennong in 2737 BCE; documented evidence of tea consumption dates to the Han dynasty (206 BCE). Green tea is unoxidized (leaves are immediately heated); oolong is partially oxidized; black tea is fully oxidized (leaves are bruised and allowed to oxidize before drying). Britain''s East India Company controlled the China tea trade until the Opium Wars; when China restricted trade, the British transplanted tea cultivation to colonial India (Assam and Darjeeling) and Ceylon. The Clipper ship era was driven by the premium paid for the first teas of each season — ships raced from China to London. The Boston Tea Party (1773) was a political response to British tea taxation; the American preference for coffee over tea is partly a legacy of that anti-British association.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0021-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'How MSG Became Falsely Demonized',
   'Monosodium glutamate was for decades blamed for "Chinese Restaurant Syndrome" — a cluster of symptoms (headache, flushing, sweating) allegedly caused by MSG in Chinese food. Rigorous double-blind studies have consistently failed to find any effect of MSG on people who believe they are sensitive to it.',
   'The "Chinese Restaurant Syndrome" was coined in a 1968 letter to the New England Journal of Medicine by Dr. Robert Ho Man Kwok, describing his personal symptoms after Chinese food — purely anecdotal, not a study. Subsequent controlled trials gave subjects either MSG-containing food or placebos in blind conditions and found no consistent effect of MSG on "sensitive" individuals. The nocebo effect (expectation of harm causing harm) explains most reported symptoms. MSG contains 12% sodium by weight versus 39% in table salt — it is actually a low-sodium alternative for adding savory flavor. The FDA classifies MSG as "generally recognized as safe." The demonization of MSG has clear racial dimensions (Chinese food was singled out while parmesan, soy sauce, and tomatoes — all glutamate-rich — were never blamed). MSG use has continued unabated in processed foods under different names (hydrolyzed protein, yeast extract).',
   'fact', '2026-07-18'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0022-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'The French Paradox: Why Saturated Fat Didn''t Kill the French',
   'France has high dietary saturated fat consumption (cheese, butter, duck fat, foie gras) but historically low rates of cardiovascular disease compared to northern Europe and the US — a puzzle called the French Paradox that challenged prevailing diet-heart theory.',
   'Serge Renaud''s 1991 "60 Minutes" documentary popularized the term and suggested red wine (resveratrol, polyphenols) was protective. Subsequent research has largely failed to confirm resveratrol as the mechanism — the doses required are implausibly high. More likely explanations: the French food culture involves smaller portions, longer meal times, less snacking, lower ultra-processed food consumption (at least historically), and perhaps time-restricted eating patterns. The paradox also reflects a statistical artifact: France''s cardiovascular data in the 1980s lagged dietary change by decades (the diet that caused current disease was 20 years ago). As French diets have shifted toward ultra-processed foods, their cardiovascular disease rates have risen toward European norms. The paradox has been used to sell both red wine and low-carb diets — often overinterpreting data to support a preferred conclusion.',
   'insight', '2026-07-19'),
  ('dddddddd-000d-0023-000d-00000000000d', 'food', 'The Columbian Exchange: How the Americas Fed the World',
   'The biological exchange following 1492 — plants, animals, and pathogens moving between the Old and New Worlds — produced the single most rapid and permanent change in global food supply in human history. Foods now considered quintessentially European or Asian mostly arrived from the Americas.',
   'American crops transferred to the Old World include: potatoes (which enabled Irish famine when monoculture failed, but also fed Europe through the 17th-19th century population boom), tomatoes (now central to Italian, Spanish, and Greek cuisine), maize (became the staple of sub-Saharan Africa), chili peppers (transformed Indian, Thai, Korean, and Chinese cooking), chocolate, vanilla, tobacco, and rubber. The Old World sent to the Americas: wheat, rice, sugar, coffee, horses, cattle, pigs, sheep — and smallpox, measles, and typhus that killed an estimated 90% of the indigenous American population, one of history''s greatest demographic catastrophes. Alfred Crosby, who documented the exchange, argued it was more consequential than any political event of the era. The potato alone is credited with enabling the industrial revolution by feeding Europe''s swelling urban workforce affordably.',
   'fact', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Philosophy ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0004-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Trolley Problem Reveals Competing Moral Frameworks',
   'The trolley problem — pull a lever to divert a runaway trolley, killing one person to save five — is interesting not for its answer but because it exposes a fault line between utilitarianism and deontological ethics. Most people switch the lever but won''t push someone off a bridge to achieve the same result.',
   'Judith Jarvis Thomson''s "footbridge" variant (pushing a fat man off a bridge to stop the trolley) produces the opposite intuition despite identical arithmetic (one death saves five). The divergence reveals that our moral psychology is not purely consequentialist: how an outcome is brought about matters, not just the outcome itself. Deontologists explain the difference via the doctrine of double effect (killing as a side effect of saving is different from killing as a means to saving). Utilitarians struggle to explain why the footbridge variant feels wrong. Joshua Greene''s neuroimaging research shows that the push variant activates emotional (medial prefrontal cortex) regions while the lever variant activates more cognitive regions — suggesting the divergence is partly the difference between two neural systems, not a single rational calculus.',
   'insight', '2026-07-01'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0005-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Plato''s Cave and the Nature of Reality',
   'In Plato''s allegory of the cave, prisoners chained in a cave mistake shadows on the wall for reality. The allegory argues that most people mistake their perceptions and opinions for knowledge — and that true understanding requires the painful effort of turning toward the light.',
   'The allegory appears in Book VII of the Republic. The cave represents the world of appearances (the material world accessible to the senses); the sunlit world outside represents the realm of Forms (eternal, unchanging truths accessible only to reason). The freed prisoner who returns to the cave to guide others is treated with contempt — an allegory for the philosopher''s political fate (Socrates''s execution being the obvious reference). Modern resonances: Baudrillard''s "simulacra" (media images replacing reality), Nozick''s experience machine (would you plug into a simulation of perfect happiness?), and the simulation hypothesis all revisit Plato''s central question: how do we know our perceptual experience corresponds to reality? Epistemic humility — acknowledging the limits of our perception — remains one of philosophy''s most practical insights.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0006-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Epistemology',
   'The branch of philosophy studying the nature, sources, and limits of knowledge. Its central questions: What is knowledge? What can we know? How do we know it? What distinguishes belief from knowledge?',
   'The classical definition of knowledge — justified true belief (JTB) — was dominant from Plato through 1963, when Edmund Gettier published a two-page paper demonstrating that justified true belief is not sufficient for knowledge. His counterexamples: a person with a stopped clock looks at it at exactly the right moment (true justified belief without genuine knowledge). Gettier problems spawned a large literature of proposed refinements. Foundationalism holds that knowledge rests on a set of basic, self-justifying beliefs (Descartes: cogito ergo sum). Coherentism holds that beliefs are justified by their coherence within a network of beliefs. Pragmatism holds that truth is what works. Externalism (Goldman) holds that beliefs can be knowledge if they are caused by reliable cognitive processes, regardless of the believer''s awareness of those processes.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0007-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Hume''s Problem of Induction Has Never Been Solved',
   'David Hume demonstrated in 1739 that there is no logical justification for inferring general laws from specific observations. All scientific induction — concluding from many observed sunrises that the sun will rise tomorrow — rests on an assumption that cannot itself be justified by observation.',
   'Hume''s argument: the uniformity of nature (that future cases will resemble past cases) is the assumption that makes induction valid. But this assumption cannot be justified by induction (that would be circular) or by logic alone (it is a contingent fact about the world, not a logical necessity). Kant tried to solve this by making the uniformity of nature a precondition of possible experience (a synthetic a priori truth). Popper argued that science avoids induction by using deduction: science proceeds not by confirming theories but by attempting to falsify them (hypothetico-deductivism). Goodman''s "new riddle of induction" (1955) deepened the problem: even if induction is valid, any set of observations is consistent with infinitely many generalizations — which one do we choose? The problem remains genuinely unsolved.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0008-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Simulation Hypothesis Is Philosophically Serious',
   'If it is possible to simulate conscious minds in a computer, and if civilizations tend to run many such simulations, then we are almost certainly simulated beings — because simulated minds would vastly outnumber non-simulated ones. Nick Bostrom''s trilemma formalizes this argument.',
   'Bostrom (2003) argues that at least one of three propositions is true: (1) virtually all civilizations go extinct before reaching the capability to run ancestor simulations; (2) virtually no civilization that reaches that capability runs such simulations; or (3) we are almost certainly living in a simulation. The argument''s force: if simulations are possible and common, the number of simulated minds dwarfs non-simulated minds, making it statistically likely we are simulated. Physicists have noted that the universe''s discrete structure (Planck length, quantized energy) resembles a computational grid. Counterarguments: consciousness might not be computationally implementable (philosophical zombies objection); energy requirements for a simulated universe might be prohibitive; the argument assumes a naive view of probability over infinite nested simulations. Elon Musk and Stephen Hawking have both commented on the hypothesis — it has moved from philosophy departments to mainstream discourse.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0009-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Moral Relativism',
   'The view that moral truths are relative to individuals, cultures, or historical periods — there are no universal moral facts. Contrasted with moral realism, which holds that some moral claims are objectively true or false.',
   'Descriptive moral relativism (an empirical claim) observes that different cultures have different moral beliefs — this is uncontroversial. Metaethical moral relativism (a philosophical claim) holds that moral claims are true or false only relative to a cultural framework — there is no standpoint-independent moral fact. Normative moral relativism holds that we should not judge other cultures'' moral practices — this is the most commonly debated form. Critics: moral relativism seems to prevent condemning slavery, genocide, or female genital mutilation in any culture that endorses them. It also seems self-refuting (is the claim "morality is relative" itself relative?). Error theory (Mackie) holds that all moral claims are objectively false, because there are no moral facts — a position that preserves the logical structure of moral claims while denying their truth.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0010-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Gödel''s Incompleteness Theorems Changed Philosophy',
   'In 1931, Kurt Gödel proved that any consistent formal system powerful enough to express arithmetic must contain true statements that cannot be proven within the system. Mathematics cannot fully prove its own consistency — a result that shook the foundations of logic.',
   'Gödel''s first incompleteness theorem: any consistent formal system containing basic arithmetic is incomplete — there will always be true statements expressible in the system that cannot be proven within it. The second theorem: such a system cannot prove its own consistency. The proof uses Gödel numbering — encoding mathematical statements as numbers — to construct a sentence that says "this sentence is unprovable," creating an arithmetic version of the liar''s paradox. The results destroyed Hilbert''s program: the hope of formalizing all mathematics in a complete, consistent, decidable system. Philosophical implications are debated: Lucas (1961) argued Gödel showed human minds transcend formal systems (Penrose extended this). Most logicians reject this interpretation. More practically, Gödel showed that mathematical truth outstrips provability — there are more mathematical truths than any system can capture.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0011-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Socratic Method and Why Socrates Was Dangerous',
   'Socrates claimed to know nothing and proceeded to demonstrate — through relentless questioning — that those who believed they knew were confused. This "elenchus" (refutation) was profoundly disruptive: it undermined authority and showed that conventional wisdom dissolved under examination.',
   'Socrates left no writings; we know him through Plato''s dialogues and Xenophon''s memoirs. His method: ask someone to define a concept (justice, courage, piety), then find counterexamples that show the definition is inadequate, then refine — ad infinitum, with the typical result that neither Socrates nor his interlocutor can define the concept satisfactorily. The method worked as a political tool: it showed that Athenian generals didn''t know what courage was, that politicians didn''t know what justice was. The Athenian jury convicted him of impiety and corrupting youth — charges that amounted to "making the established order look foolish." His willingness to die for his principles (refusing to flee Athens) rather than live inconsistently made his execution the founding event of Western philosophy.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0012-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Pragmatism',
   'The American philosophical tradition (Peirce, James, Dewey) that holds that the meaning of a concept is determined by its practical consequences, and that truth is whatever "works" — whatever proves useful in guiding action and inquiry.',
   'Charles Sanders Peirce founded pragmatism in 1878 with the maxim: to determine a concept''s meaning, consider what practical effects its object would have. William James extended this: "truth" is not a static correspondence between idea and reality but a property ideas acquire as they are verified in experience. John Dewey applied pragmatism to democracy, education, and ethics: ideas are tools for problem-solving; philosophy should address real human problems. Critics (Bertrand Russell) argued that calling "whatever works" true makes truth relative to human purposes and allows convenient falsehoods. Richard Rorty''s neo-pragmatism radicalized this: there is no view from nowhere — all descriptions are made from within some contingent vocabulary, and philosophy should give up the correspondence theory of truth entirely.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0013-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Compatibilism: Free Will and Determinism Can Coexist',
   'The hard incompatibilist view says that if the universe is deterministic, free will is impossible. Compatibilists — the majority position among professional philosophers — argue that free will (properly defined) is compatible with determinism: the kind of freedom we need for moral responsibility exists even in a deterministic universe.',
   'The compatibilist insight: "free will" does not require the ability to have done otherwise (libertarian free will) — it requires only that action flows from one''s own desires, reasons, and character rather than from external compulsion or internal defect (addiction, coercion, mania). Daniel Dennett''s version: an agent is free when its actions are caused by its rational deliberation rather than bypassing it. Harry Frankfurt''s version: freedom consists in acting on desires you reflectively endorse (second-order desires). Hard incompatibilists (Saul Smilansky, Derk Pereboom) argue that even the compatibilist sense of freedom is insufficient for moral responsibility — because our characters were themselves determined by factors outside our control. The debate matters practically: it affects how we think about punishment, reward, and the criminal justice system.',
   'insight', '2026-07-10'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0014-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Problem of Evil and Its Responses',
   'If God is all-powerful, all-knowing, and all-good, why does evil exist? This is the oldest and strongest argument against theism. The logical problem (evil disproves God''s existence) has largely been resolved; the evidential problem (evil makes God''s existence unlikely) remains the central challenge.',
   'Alvin Plantinga''s Free Will Defense resolved the logical problem: God could not create free creatures who always choose good, because freedom requires the genuine possibility of choosing evil. If free will is valuable enough to justify its risks, God''s permission of evil is logically consistent with omnipotence and goodness. The evidential problem is harder: the sheer quantity and severity of natural evil (cancer in children, the Lisbon earthquake that killed 100,000 people) seems disproportionate to any plausible benefit. Theodicy (justifying God''s ways to humans) responses include soul-making theodicy (suffering builds character, Hick), eschatological justice (evil will be redeemed in an afterlife), and skeptical theism (we cannot evaluate divine purposes). Rowe''s "fawn" argument: a fawn dies slowly in a forest fire caused by lightning, with no human observation — what possible good could justify this? The debate continues with no consensus.',
   'fact', '2026-07-11'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0015-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Utilitarianism and Its Counterintuitive Implications',
   'Utilitarianism (Bentham, Mill) holds that the right action maximizes aggregate happiness. This simple principle generates a framework that is simultaneously one of ethics'' most influential and most controversial — its implications often clash with deeply held moral intuitions.',
   'Act utilitarianism: each action should be evaluated by whether it maximizes total happiness. Rule utilitarianism: follow the rules that if generally followed would maximize happiness. Peter Singer''s utilitarian argument for global poverty: if you can prevent something bad without sacrificing anything of comparable moral importance, you ought to do it — implying that spending on luxuries while others die of preventable disease is morally equivalent to letting a child drown in a shallow pond. Counterintuitive implications: utilitarianism seems to endorse harvesting one healthy person''s organs to save five, punishing the innocent if it produces better outcomes, and demanding unlimited sacrifice from individuals for marginal gains in aggregate welfare. John Rawls argued that utilitarianism does not "take seriously the distinction between persons" — it treats individuals as locations of welfare to be aggregated rather than as ends in themselves.',
   'insight', '2026-07-12'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0016-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Deontology: Why Intentions Matter More Than Outcomes',
   'Deontological ethics (Kant) holds that some actions are intrinsically right or wrong, regardless of consequences. The rightness of an action is determined by whether it conforms to a duty or rule — not by the outcomes it produces.',
   'Kant''s categorical imperative has three formulations: (1) Act only on maxims you could will to become universal laws; (2) Always treat persons as ends in themselves, never merely as means; (3) Act as a legislating member of a kingdom of ends. The first formulation rules out lying: if everyone lied, trust would collapse and lying would become impossible (self-defeating). The second rules out using people instrumentally. Deontology''s strength: it explains the moral intuition that some things are just wrong regardless of consequences (torture, using someone as a non-consenting organ donor). Its weakness: it struggles with cases where following the rule produces catastrophic outcomes (lying to a murderer at the door about where your friend is hiding). W.D. Ross proposed "prima facie duties" (keep promises, don''t harm, help others) that can conflict and must be weighed situationally — a more flexible deontology.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-13'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0017-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Hard Problem of Consciousness',
   'Why does physical processing in the brain give rise to subjective experience — the redness of red, the pain of pain? Explaining how neurons produce consciousness is the "hard problem" (Chalmers, 1995). It remains the deepest unsolved problem in philosophy and science.',
   'The "easy problems" of consciousness (explaining attention, memory, access to information, reportability) are scientifically tractable — they explain how the brain processes information. The hard problem asks why there is something it is like to be a conscious organism at all — why any physical process should be accompanied by inner experience rather than occurring "in the dark." Physicalists hold that consciousness will eventually be explained by neuroscience, or that the hard problem is confused (Dennett: there is no "Cartesian theater" of experience, just functional processes). Dualists (Chalmers) hold that consciousness is not reducible to physical processes. Panpsychists (Philip Goff) hold that consciousness is a fundamental feature of reality, not emergent from matter. Illusionists (Frankish) hold that phenomenal consciousness is an introspective illusion — there is no qualitative experience, only the representation of one.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0018-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Veil of Ignorance: Rawls''s Thought Experiment',
   'John Rawls''s "original position" asks: what principles of justice would you choose if you didn''t know your place in society — your wealth, race, gender, or talents? Behind this "veil of ignorance," rational agents would choose principles that protect the worst-off.',
   'Rawls argued that agents behind the veil of ignorance would choose two principles: (1) equal basic liberties for all; (2) inequalities are permissible only if they benefit the least advantaged members of society (the "difference principle"). The thought experiment captures something important: principles chosen in ignorance of one''s position are more likely to be genuinely impartial. Libertarians (Nozick) objected that Rawlsian justice requires continuous redistribution that violates rights acquired through voluntary exchange. Communitarians (Sandel) objected that the "unencumbered self" behind the veil doesn''t represent real human beings, who are constituted by their communities and relationships and cannot abstract away from them. The original position remains one of the most influential thought experiments in political philosophy — a standard reference point for debates about distributive justice.',
   'insight', '2026-07-15'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0019-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Nihilism vs. Absurdism: Different Responses to Meaninglessness',
   'Nihilism holds that life has no meaning or value. Absurdism (Camus) holds that life has no inherent meaning but we should embrace it anyway — finding joy in the struggle itself rather than seeking meaning that doesn''t exist or fleeing into false comfort.',
   'Nietzsche diagnosed the "death of God" (the collapse of religious meaning-making in the modern world) as creating a crisis of nihilism — and called for a creative response (will to power, eternal recurrence, Übermensch). Sartre''s existentialism: existence precedes essence — we are not born with a purpose; we create one through radical freedom and commitment. Camus rejected both theistic consolation and atheistic meaning-making as forms of "philosophical suicide" — leaping to false solutions to avoid facing the absurd. His response: revolt, freedom, and passion in the face of meaninglessness. The absurd hero is Sisyphus, condemned to roll a boulder uphill forever; Camus says we must imagine him happy. The practical difference from nihilism: nihilism ends in paralysis or despair; absurdism ends in full engagement with life, precisely because it is not underwritten by any cosmic guarantee.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-16'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0020-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Philosophy of Time: Is the Past Real?',
   'Presentism holds that only the present moment exists — the past is gone and the future doesn''t yet exist. Eternalism holds that past, present, and future all equally exist; the "present" is just our current location in a four-dimensional spacetime block. Special relativity strongly supports eternalism.',
   'Presentism''s appeal: the intuition that only "now" is real. Its problem: special relativity shows that simultaneity is relative — two observers in relative motion disagree about which events are simultaneous. If "present" is defined as "simultaneous with now," different observers have different presents — undermining the idea of a single, universally present moment. Eternalism (block universe or four-dimensionalism) treats time as a fourth spatial dimension: all events exist in the four-dimensional block, and our experience of "now" moving forward is an illusion (or a feature of our psychology). The growing block view: the past and present exist but the future does not — the universe "grows" as new events become present and then past. The debate has practical implications for free will (the block universe seems to leave no room for genuine change) and for our attitudes toward death and the passage of time.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0021-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Animal Rights: What Grounds Moral Consideration?',
   'The philosophical argument for animal rights asks not "can they reason?" or "can they talk?" but "can they suffer?" — Peter Singer''s utilitarian argument and Tom Regan''s rights-based argument have fundamentally changed how both philosophy and law view the moral status of animals.',
   'Jeremy Bentham''s 18th-century insight: "The question is not, Can they reason? nor, Can they talk? but, Can they suffer?" Singer''s Animal Liberation (1975) applied utilitarian logic: if sentient beings'' suffering matters morally, then the suffering of animals must count equally with equivalent human suffering — anything less is "speciesism," a bias as arbitrary as racism. Tom Regan''s rights-based approach: animals are "subjects of a life" (they have beliefs, desires, a welfare) and therefore have inherent value that cannot be overridden by aggregate benefit. Contractarian responses (Carruthers): moral standing requires the ability to participate in the social contract — animals cannot, so they have no direct moral status (though indirect obligations arise from their owners'' interests). Practical legal developments: several countries now have constitutional animal protection; some jurisdictions have granted primates limited legal personhood.',
   'insight', '2026-07-18'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0022-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'The Self as Illusion: Buddhism and Neuroscience Converge',
   'Buddhist philosophy has argued for 2,500 years that the unified, persistent self is a cognitive illusion. Contemporary neuroscience, studying split-brain patients, autobiographical memory, and the default mode network, has reached similar conclusions through entirely different methods.',
   'The Buddhist doctrine of anatta (no-self) holds that what we call "self" is a collection of changing processes (skandhas) — physical form, sensation, perception, mental formations, and consciousness — with no persistent, unified entity underlying them. David Hume reached a similar conclusion through introspection in 1739: looking inside for a self, he found only "a bundle of perceptions." Michael Gazzaniga''s split-brain research: patients whose corpus callosum is severed have two quasi-independent conscious systems — the "interpreter" in the left hemisphere confabulates stories that unify experience post-hoc. Thomas Metzinger argues the self is a "self-model" — a representation the brain constructs for navigational and predictive purposes, not a metaphysical entity. The practical question: if the self is a construction, does this change how we should approach suffering (Buddhist therapeutics), responsibility, or identity over time?',
   'insight', '2026-07-19'),
  ('eeeeeeee-000e-0023-000e-00000000000e', 'philosophy', 'Virtue Ethics: The Good Life Over Good Acts',
   'Rather than asking "What is the right action?" (deontology) or "What produces the best outcomes?" (consequentialism), virtue ethics asks "What kind of person should I be?" The focus is on character — developing stable dispositions that make right action natural.',
   'Aristotle''s Nicomachean Ethics (350 BCE) is the foundational text. His key concepts: eudaimonia (flourishing, often translated as happiness but closer to "living well"), arête (virtue, excellence), and phronesis (practical wisdom — the ability to discern the right action in particular circumstances, which cannot be reduced to rules). Virtues are the mean between extremes: courage is the mean between cowardice and recklessness; generosity between miserliness and profligacy. Virtues are acquired through practice — we become just by doing just acts, brave by doing brave acts, until virtuous action becomes second nature. Alasdair MacIntyre''s "After Virtue" (1981) argued that modern moral philosophy''s problems stem from fragmenting virtue ethics into incompatible deontological and consequentialist frameworks stripped of their narrative and communal context. Contemporary virtue ethics has been applied to professional ethics, environmental ethics, and positive psychology.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Culture ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('ffffffff-000f-0004-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Average Person Checks Their Phone 96 Times Daily',
   'Research by app analytics firm Asurion found that Americans check their phones 96 times per day — once every 10 minutes. The smartphone has fundamentally restructured human attention, social interaction, and the experience of time.',
   'The compulsive phone-checking behavior is driven by variable-ratio reinforcement — the same psychological mechanism as slot machines. Each check might reward with a message, like, or piece of news; unpredictable reward schedules produce the most persistent behaviors. Sean Parker, Facebook''s founding president, publicly described the platform''s design goal: "How do we consume as much of your time and conscious attention as possible?" The attention economy treats human attention as a resource to be harvested for advertising. Microsoft''s 2015 research claimed human attention spans had dropped below that of goldfish (8 seconds) — a claim that was methodologically flawed and widely circulated. The genuine concern is attentional fragmentation: the ability to sustain focus on a single task for extended periods has measurably declined in populations with heavy smartphone use.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0005-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Nostalgia Functions as a Psychological Buffer',
   'Nostalgia — a bittersweet longing for the past — is a near-universal experience that serves specific psychological functions: buffering loneliness, providing continuity of self, and increasing feelings of meaning and social connectedness. It is protective, not pathological.',
   'The term was coined in 1688 by Swiss physician Johannes Hofer from the Greek nostos (homecoming) and algos (pain) to describe a medical condition — intense homesickness — that he believed could be fatal. For two centuries it was treated as a disease; Swiss soldiers were prohibited from playing folk music that triggered it. 20th-century psychology reclassified it as a normal emotion. Constantine Sedikides''s research at the University of Southampton showed that nostalgic people report higher self-esteem, greater social connectedness, and more meaning in life. Critically, nostalgia functions as a regulatory resource: when people feel lonely or their sense of self is threatened, they spontaneously turn to nostalgic memories to restore equilibrium. The "nostalgia trap" — using the past to avoid confronting present problems — is real, but it is not nostalgia''s primary function.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0006-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Cultural Capital',
   'Pierre Bourdieu''s concept (1979) for the non-financial social assets — knowledge, tastes, skills, credentials, and cultural knowledge — that provide social advantage. Like economic capital, cultural capital is distributed unequally and reproduces class distinctions across generations.',
   'Bourdieu identified three forms: embodied (cultivated dispositions, tastes, and ways of speaking incorporated into the body through upbringing), objectified (cultural goods like books, art, instruments), and institutionalized (educational credentials). Cultural capital explains why two children with identical test scores from different class backgrounds have different educational and career outcomes: the middle-class child speaks the institutional language, navigates its norms, and has social connections that the working-class child lacks. The concept sparked critiques: Bourdieu''s analysis of "legitimate culture" (which tastes are valued) reflects existing power relations rather than objective aesthetic merit. The concept has been applied beyond class to explain gender, racial, and generational differences in social outcomes. "Cultural fit" hiring in corporations is cultural capital in action — privileging candidates whose habitus matches the organizational culture.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0007-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Internet Created the First Global Youth Culture',
   'For most of human history, youth culture was local or at most national — shaped by geographic proximity. For the first time in history, teenagers in Seoul, São Paulo, and Stockholm share memes, music, slang, and references in near real-time, creating a genuinely global cultural cohort.',
   'Previous attempts at global culture (Hollywood films, MTV, McDonald''s) were broadcast from centers of power to peripheries. Internet culture is more bidirectional: K-pop spread from Seoul; meme formats emerge from 4chan, Reddit, and TikTok in unpredictable places; "AAVE-derived slang" (from African American Vernacular English) has been adopted globally via social media. The "born digital" generational divide (roughly Gen Z born after 1997) is the first cohort to grow up with smartphones from childhood, giving them a shared technological mediation of experience that crosses national boundaries. This creates both convergence (shared references, aesthetic preferences, concerns about climate) and paradox: global youth culture is also characterized by intense micro-niche fragmentation — the TikTok algorithm creates personalized culture bubbles within the global stream.',
   'insight', '2026-07-04'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0008-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'How Fashion Cycles Work',
   'Fashion operates on cycles that are simultaneously driven by cultural trickle-down (elite styles diffusing to masses) and trickle-up (street styles adopted by high fashion). What was "ugly" 20 years ago reliably becomes fashionable again — a pattern called the 20-year rule.',
   'Thorstein Veblen''s "Theory of the Leisure Class" (1899) described fashion as conspicuous consumption: the wealthy adopt new styles to signal status; the middle class imitates; the wealthy then adopt new styles to re-differentiate. Georg Simmel described the simultaneous drives in fashion: imitation (conformity, belonging to a group) and differentiation (standing out). The 20-year cycle occurs because the generation that finds a style embarrassing (teenagers during its peak) grows up and rediscovers it as ironic or nostalgic. Currently: 1990s and early-2000s aesthetics are dominant. The luxury secondhand market (Vestiaire, The RealReal) has complicated traditional fashion cycles by extending garment lifespans and making luxury accessible. Fast fashion (Zara, H&M, Shein) has accelerated the cycle from seasonal to weekly.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0009-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Meme: Dawkins''s Original Concept',
   'Richard Dawkins coined "meme" in "The Selfish Gene" (1976) to describe a unit of cultural transmission analogous to the gene — an idea, behavior, or style that spreads from person to person through imitation and undergoes selection. Internet memes are a specific case of this broader concept.',
   'Dawkins''s definition: a meme is any information pattern that can be replicated — melodies, catchphrases, fashions, architectural styles, scientific theories. Like genes, memes are "selfish" — they replicate to the extent that they are well-suited to their environment (human minds and culture) regardless of whether they benefit their hosts. Memes that are catchy, emotionally resonant, or tied to memorable stories replicate more than those that are dull or complex. Susan Blackmore''s "The Meme Machine" (1999) extended Dawkins: the self itself is a memeplex — a collection of memes that replicate together. Critics argue the meme concept is too vague to have scientific explanatory power; it is not clear what the "unit" of a meme is or how to count replications. The internet meme — an image with variable captions following a format — is the most visible cultural instance of the original concept.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0010-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Printing Press Created Protestantism',
   'Martin Luther''s 95 Theses (1517) were not the first theological challenge to the Catholic Church — but they were the first to spread in weeks across Europe, amplified by the 50-year-old printing press. Without the printing press, the Reformation would likely have been suppressed like its predecessors.',
   'Jan Hus in Bohemia (burned for heresy, 1415) and John Wycliffe in England (posthumously condemned, 1415) made similar challenges to Catholic authority but had no effective means of mass distribution. Luther''s theses were translated from Latin to German and printed in editions of thousands within weeks — the pamphlet war that followed involved thousands of titles. Elizabeth Eisenstein''s "The Printing Press as an Agent of Change" (1980) argues that the press didn''t just spread Luther''s ideas; it restructured the entire epistemological environment. Literacy expanded; readers could compare Bible translations independently of priestly mediation; the norm of checking primary sources emerged. The same printing press that spread Protestant reform also spread the Copernican heliocentric theory, alchemical and occult texts, and eventually the scientific revolution''s norms of publication and peer review.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0011-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Why Humor Varies So Dramatically Across Cultures',
   'What is funny varies profoundly across cultures, generations, and contexts. The mechanisms of humor (incongruity, superiority, release of tension) are universal, but what triggers them depends entirely on shared knowledge, social context, and who holds power over whom.',
   'Benign violation theory (McGraw and Warren, 2010) argues that humor arises when something is simultaneously a violation (of norms, expectations, dignity) and benign (safe, distanced, or consensual). This explains why the same joke can be funny in one context and offensive in another — whether the violation feels benign depends on social context. Aristophanes'' comedies relied on political satire targeting Athenian public figures; audiences needed to know those figures. British irony depends on shared cultural understandings of understatement. Japanese boke-tsukkomi comedy (the fool-straight man dynamic) reflects social norms around deference and face. Slapstick (physical humor) travels across cultures more easily because it requires less shared knowledge. What can be mocked safely signals social hierarchies: humor punching down (mocking the less powerful) is more culturally variable in acceptability than self-deprecating or power-targeting humor.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0012-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Cultural Appropriation vs. Cultural Diffusion',
   'Cultural appropriation — the adoption of elements of a marginalized culture by members of a dominant culture, often without understanding or credit — is distinguished from cultural diffusion, the natural spread of cultural practices across groups. The line between them is contested but meaningful.',
   'Cultural diffusion has always occurred: the spread of blues to rock to global pop, of yoga from Hinduism to global wellness, of Japanese aesthetics to Western design. Critics of appropriation distinguish: borrowing with understanding, credit, and context differs from borrowing that extracts profits while the source culture is exploited. The specific concern is power asymmetry — when a dominant group profits from cultural elements of a marginalized group that was penalized for the same practices. Sacred or spiritually significant elements (Native American headdresses, Hindu deities as fashion) are more clearly appropriative when removed from context than broadly shared aesthetic influences. Proponents of unrestricted diffusion argue that cultural exchange is always benign and that policing it essentializes culture as static and ethnically owned. The debate reflects deeper questions about property, credit, identity, and historical power.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0013-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Origin of the Handshake',
   'The handshake is ancient — depicted in Mesopotamian, Greek, and Roman art as a gesture of contract, peace, or greeting. One common theory is that it developed to demonstrate that neither party carried a weapon; its current form in Western business culture was standardized in the 18th century.',
   'The weapon-check theory: clasping right hands (the sword hand) prevented reaching for a weapon; the up-down shaking dislodged anything hidden in the sleeve. This origin story is plausible but unverified historically. In ancient Greece, the dexiosis (right-hand clasp) was a formal gesture accompanying oath-taking and treaty ratification; it appears on coins depicting alliances. In medieval Europe, knights would clasp hands to signal peaceful intent. Quakers popularized the handshake in the 17th-18th centuries as a democratic alternative to bowing (which implied social hierarchy). American and European business culture adopted it as the standard formal greeting by the 19th century. The COVID-19 pandemic sparked debate about whether handshakes would survive — they largely have, though fist bumps and elbow bumps gained some currency. Different cultures use different greeting gestures (bowing in Japan, cheek kisses in France, pressing noses in Māori culture), reflecting divergent histories.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0014-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'How Carnivals Invert Social Order',
   'Carnivals, festivals, and other liminal events have historically functioned as controlled inversions of social hierarchy — spaces where the poor mock the powerful, the sacred is profaned, and normal rules are suspended. Victor Turner called these periods "liminality."',
   'Mikhail Bakhtin''s analysis of carnival in "Rabelais and His World" (1965) emphasized its subversive quality: during carnival, ordinary hierarchies are inverted, the body is celebrated over the mind, and collective laughter at power is permitted. Medieval European carnivals allowed commoners to mock priests and nobles in ways that would otherwise be dangerous. Turner''s liminality concept: in ritual transitions (rites of passage, carnival), participants exist "betwixt and between" social categories — outside the normal structure. This in-between state creates "communitas" (intense social bonding without hierarchy). The political function of carnival: it may release pressure from social inequality (a safety valve), or it may subvert the order it appears to endorse. Bakhtin: the laughter of carnival is not merely entertainment but a genuine challenge to authority that power cannot fully co-opt.',
   'insight', '2026-07-11'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0015-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'How Jeans Became a Global Cultural Symbol',
   'Blue denim jeans, invented for California gold miners in 1873 by Levi Strauss and Jacob Davis, became the 20th century''s most globally recognized garment — crossing class, nationality, and ideology to become simultaneously workwear, youth rebellion, and universal casual wear.',
   'The denim riveted trouser was patented in 1873 as durable workwear for miners and laborers. Hollywood films of the 1930s-50s associated jeans with the American West''s romantic frontier mythology. James Dean and Marlon Brando wore jeans in films depicting youth rebellion (Rebel Without a Cause, 1955; The Wild One, 1953), cementing their association with counter-cultural identity. In the Soviet Union, jeans were banned as decadent capitalism; Soviet youth paid black-market prices for smuggled Levi''s as a symbol of Western freedom. In Mao''s China, jeans were "spiritual pollution." The irony: jeans became a symbol of anti-establishment individualism even as they became the most mass-produced garment in history. Designer jeans (1970s), the global denim market (now $64 billion annually), and fast-fashion distressed denim have completed the transition from labor garment to fashion item.',
   'fact', '2026-07-12'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0016-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Why Gift-Giving Norms Vary Dramatically Across Cultures',
   'Gift-giving is universal but governed by radically different rules across cultures: what to give, how to give it, whether to open it immediately, and the proper response to a gift vary enormously. Violating these rules can cause offense as severe as refusing the gift entirely.',
   'Marcel Mauss''s "The Gift" (1925) argued that gift exchange is never economically neutral — it creates obligations, establishes social bonds, and expresses power relationships. The "gift economy" (where exchange is governed by generosity and reciprocity rather than price) contrasts with market exchange. In Japan, gift-wrapping is as important as the gift itself; gifts are not opened in front of the giver (to avoid showing disappointment). In China, refusing a gift initially is polite (the giver should insist); giving clocks (symbolizes death) or pears (separation) is taboo. In American culture, gifts are typically opened immediately and effusively thanked. The potlatch of the Pacific Northwest native peoples was competitive gift-giving designed to demonstrate wealth through generosity — Canadian authorities banned it in 1885 as economically disruptive. Christmas gift exchange has been transformed by commercialization into a quasi-economic obligation tied to social relationship maintenance.',
   'insight', '2026-07-13'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0017-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Concept of Face in East Asian Social Life',
   'Mianzi (面子, social face) and lian (廉, moral face) are complementary concepts in Chinese social life — mianzi refers to social status and prestige; lian to moral character and trustworthiness. "Losing face" is a social injury severe enough to damage relationships, business, and public standing.',
   'The concept of face operates across East Asian cultures (Japan: mentsu; Korea: chemyeon) and profoundly shapes business interactions, communication style, and conflict resolution. To cause someone to lose face publicly is a serious social transgression — hence the preference for indirect communication, intermediaries, and private rather than public correction of errors. "Giving face" (gei mianzi) means publicly honoring someone, recognizing their status or achievements. Business negotiations in China often involve extensive relationship-building (guanxi) to establish mutual face before substantive discussion. Western businesspeople frequently misread this dynamic as evasion or inefficiency. Psychologist Geert Hofstede''s cross-cultural research identifies "long-term orientation" and "collectivism" as the dimensions that underlie face concerns. Face dynamics also explain certain political behaviors: leaders in high-face cultures are more constrained by the need to avoid public capitulation.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0018-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'How Sports Became Secular Religion',
   'Sociologists of religion have noted that sports function analogously to religion in modern secular societies: they provide community, ritual, sacred times and spaces, heroes and villains, transcendent experiences, and meaning in suffering. "Sports as religion" is more than metaphor.',
   'Émile Durkheim''s sociology of religion focused on the function of religion in social cohesion (the "collective effervescence" of shared ritual) rather than its truth claims — a framework that applies directly to sports spectatorship. The packed stadium, the synchronized chanting, the collective ecstasy of a championship win — these produce the same neurological and social responses as religious ritual. Joseph Price''s "From Season to Season" documents sports as a "civil religion." Sacred spaces (Wembley, Fenway, Augusta), sacred calendar (Super Bowl Sunday, the World Cup), pilgrimage (traveling fans), relics (famous jerseys), saints (legends), and martyrs (athletes who sacrificed for the game) map onto religious structures. Psychologists find that team fans gain self-esteem from wins (BIRGing — basking in reflected glory) and experience genuine bereavement after losses. For many people, particularly men in secular societies, sports communities provide the primary source of shared ritual, belonging, and collective identity.',
   'insight', '2026-07-15'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0019-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The History of Taboo',
   'Taboo (from Tongan "tapu" — set apart, forbidden) refers to a cultural prohibition whose violation feels viscerally wrong, not merely incorrect. Taboos are enforced by disgust and social sanction rather than explicit rationale — and their content varies enormously across cultures while the psychological mechanism is universal.',
   'The word entered English through Captain Cook''s accounts of Polynesian practice (1777): tapu objects or persons were set apart as sacred or dangerous; contact was forbidden. Anthropologists (Mary Douglas, "Purity and Danger," 1966) argue that taboos serve to organize social reality by establishing conceptual boundaries — what is "out of place" relative to a cultural classification system is dangerous and taboo. Incest taboo is often cited as universal — but its content varies (cousin marriage is taboo in some cultures, preferred in others). Food taboos (pork in Islam and Judaism, beef in Hinduism) are among the most culturally specific. Sigmund Freud linked taboo to ambivalence (the forbidden is both attractive and prohibited). Jonathan Haidt''s moral foundations theory identifies "purity" as a moral foundation that generates taboos — violations produce disgust regardless of harm.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-16'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0020-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Individualism vs. Collectivism Across Cultures',
   'The single most consequential dimension in Geert Hofstede''s cross-cultural framework is individualism-collectivism: whether people define themselves primarily as individuals or as members of groups. This shapes everything from how praise is delivered to how healthcare is organized.',
   'Hofstede''s survey of IBM employees across 72 countries (1967-1973) found individualism-collectivism to be the most consistently differentiated cultural dimension. The United States scores highest on individualism; Guatemala and South Korea score lowest. In high-individualism cultures: identity is defined by personal attributes; direct disagreement is valued; privacy is a strong norm; hiring and promotion are based on merit rather than in-group membership. In high-collectivism cultures: identity is defined by group membership; harmony is valued over directness; loyalty to the in-group extends to business decisions; criticism is delivered indirectly. The dimension affects health outcomes, organizational effectiveness, legal systems (individual rights vs. community harmony), and responses to collective challenges like pandemics — collectivist cultures showed faster and more consistent compliance with public health measures during COVID-19.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0021-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Why Status Symbols Keep Changing',
   'The logic of status signaling is self-defeating: once a status symbol becomes affordable to the masses, it loses its signal value and the elite must adopt new symbols. Thorstein Veblen called this "conspicuous consumption" — purchasing not for utility but for social signaling.',
   'Veblen''s "Theory of the Leisure Class" (1899) described how the wealthy signal status through wasteful consumption — objects whose high price is their primary feature, since utility could be achieved more cheaply. The cycle: elite adopts expensive good → mass production makes it affordable → good loses status signal → elite adopts new expensive good. Logo-heavy luxury goods (LV monograms, Gucci patterns) have gone through multiple cycles of peak status and subsequent mainstream adoption. Current status signaling has shifted in some elite circles toward experiential wealth (rare travel, access to exclusive events), invisible luxury (quality without logos), and increasingly, time wealth (the ability to not work, to be healthy, to have slow mornings). Tyler Cowen and others argue that the internet has disrupted status hierarchies by making information goods (books, music, knowledge) effectively free, shifting status competition to experiences and access.',
   'insight', '2026-07-18'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0022-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'The Rise of Fandom as Identity',
   'Fandom — intense engagement with a cultural object (sports team, band, TV show, fictional universe) to the point where it becomes a significant component of personal identity — has expanded from a niche phenomenon to a mainstream mode of cultural participation.',
   'Henry Jenkins''s "Textual Poachers" (1992) reframed fans not as passive consumers but as active cultural producers: fan fiction, fan art, conventions, and critical analysis create participatory culture. The internet enabled fan communities to organize at scale, producing phenomena like K-pop fandoms mobilizing millions of coordinated actions, Hamilton''s fan community creating enormous secondary content, and sports team fandom functioning as quasi-tribal identity. Social identity theory (Tajfel) explains why fandom produces real emotional stakes: the team or artist is part of the fan''s social identity, and threats to the object feel like threats to the self. "Stan culture" — named after Eminem''s 2000 song about a disturbed superfan — describes extreme fan devotion that can turn toxic. The parasocial relationship (feeling personal connection to a media figure who doesn''t know you exist) is a normal human cognitive tendency, not pathology, though it can become problematic at extremes.',
   'insight', '2026-07-19'),
  ('ffffffff-000f-0023-000f-00000000000f', 'culture', 'Why Languages and Cultures Both Converge and Diverge',
   'Globalization has accelerated both cultural convergence (global brands, English spread, shared media) and cultural divergence (assertion of local identity in response to homogenization). This dynamic plays out simultaneously — the same forces drive both.',
   'Samuel Huntington''s "Clash of Civilizations" (1996) predicted that cultural and religious differences would be the primary source of post-Cold War conflict — a hypothesis that has been partially but not fully validated. Benjamin Barber''s "Jihad vs. McWorld" (1995) captured the paradox: global capitalism homogenizes culture (McWorld) while triggering reactive assertion of local, ethnic, or religious identity (Jihad — his term for tribal resistance, not specifically Islamic). The Welsh language is spoken by more people today than in 1960, despite globalization — because Welsh identity politics revived it. K-pop''s global spread did not Westernize Korean culture; it globalized Korean cultural exports. The Hebrew language was revived from liturgical to vernacular in a generation through deliberate political will. Language shift (communities abandoning indigenous languages for dominant ones) and language revival occur simultaneously in different communities.',
   'insight', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ---- Geography ----
INSERT INTO entries (id, category_id, headline, body, read_more, type, published_date) VALUES
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0004-a1b2-c3d4e5f60004', 'geography', 'Half the World''s Population Lives Inside a Tiny Circle',
   'A circle centered near Bangladesh and encompassing much of South and East Asia contains over 50% of Earth''s entire population. The other half of the world — most of the Americas, Africa, Europe, Australia, and Oceania — holds the remaining 50%.',
   'The most-population circle (often cited as centered near the Bay of Bengal) fits the extreme population density of South Asia and East Asia: Bangladesh (1,200 people/km²), India, and China each have populations exceeding a billion. The comparison is stark: Russia is the world''s largest country by area but has fewer people than Bangladesh. Population density reflects both agricultural productivity (monsoon-fed river deltas support dense farming) and historical depth of settlement. The UN projects that Africa will be home to 4 billion people by 2100, shifting global population distribution significantly. The concept was popularized by the "Valeriepieris circle" — the most-population circle demonstrating how concentrated global population is geographically.',
   'fact', '2026-07-01'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0005-a1b2-c3d4e5f60005', 'geography', 'Why Landlocked Countries Tend to Be Poorer',
   'The 44 landlocked countries in the world have average GDP per capita roughly 40% lower than their coastal neighbors. Without ocean access, trade costs are higher, foreign investment is harder to attract, and military security is more complex — geography shapes development.',
   'Ocean access enables cheap bulk trade: maritime shipping is 10-30 times cheaper than land transport per ton-kilometer. Landlocked countries must negotiate transit rights with neighbors, adding political and logistical complexity. Jeffrey Sachs''s "Geography and Economic Development" documented the strong correlation between coastal access and prosperity. There are exceptions: Switzerland, Luxembourg, and Austria are wealthy despite being landlocked — but they are surrounded by prosperous neighbors with excellent infrastructure and are deeply integrated into the EU. Landlocked developing countries (LLDCs) like Chad, Central African Republic, and Niger face compounded disadvantages: poor neighbors, inadequate transit infrastructure, and dependence on single export commodities. The UN has a specific development program for LLDCs. The Baltic Sea''s historically critical role in European commerce explains why control of its coastline was a recurring cause of war.',
   'insight', '2026-07-02'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0006-a1b2-c3d4e5f60006', 'geography', 'Karst Topography',
   'A landscape formed by the dissolution of soluble rocks (limestone, dolomite, gypsum) by slightly acidic water. Karst features include sinkholes, caves, underground rivers, and dramatic limestone pinnacles — covering about 15% of Earth''s land surface.',
   'Rainwater absorbs CO₂ from the atmosphere and soil to form carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), which dissolves calcium carbonate in limestone. Over millions of years, this creates extensive cave systems, disappearing streams (rivers that flow into caves underground), and sinkholes. The karst regions of southern China (Guangxi and Yunnan) feature iconic "tower karst" — isolated limestone pinnacles rising from flat plains, depicted in classical Chinese landscape painting. Carlsbad Caverns (New Mexico), the Waitomo Caves (New Zealand), and the Škocjan Caves (Slovenia) are karst systems. Florida is built largely on karst limestone — sinkholes there open suddenly, sometimes swallowing buildings. The Yucatan Peninsula''s cenotes (sinkholes filled with groundwater) were sacred to the Maya and served as water sources in a landscape with no surface rivers. Karst aquifers provide drinking water to 25% of the global population.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-03'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0007-a1b2-c3d4e5f60007', 'geography', 'Lake Baikal Holds 20% of Earth''s Surface Fresh Water',
   'Lake Baikal in Siberia is the world''s oldest (25 million years), deepest (1,642 meters), and most voluminous freshwater lake — containing more fresh water than all five North American Great Lakes combined. It is a UNESCO World Heritage site and home to 2,500 endemic species.',
   'Baikal formed as a continental rift valley — a site where Earth''s crust is slowly pulling apart. It sits in a seismically active zone and is widening by 2 cm per year. Its extraordinary depth results from this geological origin. The lake is home to the Baikal seal (nerpa) — the world''s only exclusively freshwater seal, which evolved after the lake became isolated approximately 500,000 years ago. The Baikal oilfish (golomyanka) is a remarkable endemic species: transparent, nearly all fat, it lives at depths of 500-1,600 meters and gives live birth to larvae. Baikal''s water is exceptionally clear (visibility to 40 meters) because of its unusual purifying ecosystem: Epischura baikalensis, a tiny crustacean that comprises 80% of the zooplankton biomass, filters the entire lake volume roughly once a year. Industrial pollution from the Baikal Paper Pulp Mill (operating 1966-2013) caused damage that scientists are still assessing.',
   'fact', '2026-07-04'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0008-a1b2-c3d4e5f60008', 'geography', 'Why Rivers Meander',
   'Rivers rarely flow in straight lines — even when their channels are artificially straightened, they curve again. The meandering of rivers is a consequence of fluid dynamics: even tiny irregularities amplify into curves that grow and migrate over time.',
   'The mechanism: as water flows around a slight bend, centrifugal force pushes water outward, eroding the outer bank. The slower inner bank accumulates sediment (point bar). The bend deepens and migrates laterally. Meanders grow until the river cuts across a loop at flood stage, creating an "oxbow lake" — the abandoned meander. The length of a river as the crow flies divided by its actual length is the sinuosity index; most rivers have sinuosity >1.5. The Mississippi River is 2,350 km long (straight line) but flows 3,730 km — and was once even more sinuous before the Army Corps of Engineers straightened it for navigation, paradoxically causing faster flows, more flooding downstream, and land loss in the delta. Leopold and Wolman (1960) showed that meandering is the river''s energy-minimizing configuration — it allows the river to dissipate the same energy over a longer distance, reducing velocity and erosion.',
   'insight', '2026-07-05'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0009-a1b2-c3d4e5f60009', 'geography', 'Fjord',
   'A long, narrow, deep inlet of the sea between steep cliffs, formed by glacial erosion and subsequently flooded by rising sea levels at the end of the last ice age. Fjords can reach depths exceeding 1,300 meters — deeper than most ocean shelves.',
   'Fjords form when glaciers advance to the sea, carving U-shaped valleys through their immense erosive power. When glaciers retreat and sea levels rise, the sea floods the valley. The deepest fjords occur where glaciers were thickest and moved fastest — the Sognefjord in Norway reaches 1,308 meters depth and extends 204 km inland. The threshold (sill) at the fjord''s mouth (where the glacier''s terminus was) is often shallow, limiting water circulation and creating anoxic (oxygen-depleted) bottom waters in some fjords. Norwegian fjords are the most dramatic and numerous, but they also exist in Scotland, Chile (Patagonia), Alaska, New Zealand, Greenland, and Canada''s British Columbia. The Norwegian coastline''s total length, measured precisely including all fjords, is approximately 100,000 km — longer than twice Earth''s circumference. Fjords are major carbon sinks: slow bottom-water circulation buries organic carbon in anoxic sediments.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-06'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0010-a1b2-c3d4e5f60010', 'geography', 'The Sahara Was Green 10,000 Years Ago',
   'During the "Green Sahara" or African Humid Period (approximately 11,000-5,000 years ago), the world''s largest hot desert was covered by savanna, grasslands, and lakes. Hippos and crocodiles lived where Cairo is today. Rock art from this period shows hunting scenes that would be impossible in today''s desert.',
   'The Green Sahara was caused by changes in Earth''s orbital precession — the wobble of Earth''s axis that shifts the timing and intensity of seasons. During the African Humid Period, summer insolation was stronger, intensifying the West African monsoon and pushing rainfall deep into the Sahara. Lakes formed across the region, including the largest known lake in Earth''s history: Mega-Lake Chad, which covered 400,000 km² (larger than the Caspian Sea). Cave paintings in the Tassili n''Ajjer (Algeria) and Fezzan (Libya) depict giraffes, elephants, cattle herds, and swimming figures in what is now waterless desert. The transition back to desert conditions around 5,500 years ago may have concentrated human populations in the Nile Valley — contributing to the emergence of Egyptian civilization. Climate models suggest a future wetter Sahara is possible under certain warming scenarios.',
   'fact', '2026-07-07'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0011-a1b2-c3d4e5f60011', 'geography', 'Island Biogeography: Why Islands Develop Unique Species',
   'Islands have disproportionately high rates of endemic species (found nowhere else) and high extinction rates. The theory of island biogeography (MacArthur and Wilson, 1967) explains species diversity on islands through the balance of immigration from the mainland and extinction of established species.',
   'The key variables: island size (larger islands support more species because habitats are more diverse and populations are less prone to extinction) and distance from the mainland (closer islands have higher immigration rates). The equilibrium number of species on an island is where immigration rate equals extinction rate. Islands are evolution''s natural laboratories: isolated populations diverge rapidly. The Galápagos (Darwin''s finches), Hawaii (honeycreepers), and Madagascar (lemurs) all display extreme adaptive radiation — one colonizing species diversifying into many ecological niches. Island species are often naive about predators — making them catastrophically vulnerable to introduced species (rats, cats, goats). Islands hold 20% of bird, reptile, and plant species despite covering only 5% of land area, and are home to 40% of critically endangered species. Fragmented habitats on continents (forest patches in agricultural landscapes) function as ecological islands, with similar dynamics.',
   'insight', '2026-07-08'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0012-a1b2-c3d4e5f60012', 'geography', 'Isthmus',
   'A narrow strip of land connecting two larger landmasses, with water on both sides. The Isthmus of Panama is the most consequential — its formation 3 million years ago separated the Pacific and Atlantic Oceans, altered global ocean circulation, and may have triggered the Ice Ages.',
   'An isthmus forms when volcanic activity, tectonic uplift, or sediment accumulation creates a land bridge. Panama formed as the North American and South American plates converged and volcanic islands accreted. The closure of the Central American Seaway (the sea passage between North and South America) redirected warm Atlantic water northward, strengthening the Gulf Stream and increasing evaporation over the North Atlantic — sending more precipitation to the Arctic, building ice sheets, and triggering the Pleistocene glaciations. It also created the "Great American Biotic Interchange" — the mixing of North and South American fauna when previously isolated species could cross the land bridge. The Panama Canal (1914) effectively reopened the isthmus to ocean traffic, though biosecurity measures prevent marine species from crossing between oceans. Other notable isthmuses: Suez (Egypt), Karelian (Finland), Corinth (Greece).',
   'vocab', '2026-07-09'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0013-a1b2-c3d4e5f60013', 'geography', 'Manila Is the World''s Most Densely Populated City',
   'Manila, the capital of the Philippines, has an official population density of approximately 111,000 people per km² — making it the world''s most densely populated city. For comparison, New York City averages about 11,000 per km². This density creates both resilience and extreme vulnerability.',
   'Manila''s density is not uniform: while overall figures are high, specific districts (Tondo, Binondo) reach even higher densities. The density reflects rapid rural-to-urban migration driven by economic opportunity in the metropolis, lack of affordable housing, and the informal settlement sector (barangays). High density enables efficient public transit, reduces per-capita carbon emissions for transport, and creates vibrant street economies. It also concentrates vulnerability: the 2013 Typhoon Hainan devastated Tacloban (smaller but similarly densely settled coastal city); Manila''s location on Manila Bay and in a region of intense typhoon activity makes it highly exposed to storm surge and flooding. Dhaka (Bangladesh), Kolkata, and Lagos are among other extreme-density megacities. Urban density is increasingly recognized as a climate-adaptive strategy — dense cities are more energy-efficient than sprawl — but only when paired with adequate infrastructure.',
   'fact', '2026-07-10'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0014-a1b2-c3d4e5f60014', 'geography', 'The International Date Line Is Not Straight',
   'The International Date Line (IDL) follows the 180° meridian in the Pacific but deviates significantly in several places to keep island nations and their territories on the same calendar day — creating some of the world''s most dramatic time anomalies.',
   'The 180° meridian runs through the Pacific Ocean, but if the IDL were perfectly straight, it would split Kiribati, Fiji, and Russia''s Chukotka across two calendar days — creating administrative and commercial chaos. The IDL curves eastward around Russia''s far east, westward to keep Fiji and its dependencies together, and dramatically eastward to include all of Kiribati on the western side (Kiribati shifted the entire line 2,600 km eastward in 1995, so it now has the world''s most advanced time zone, UTC+14). The result: some Pacific islands that are geographically close are a full calendar day apart (American Samoa UTC-11 and Samoa UTC+13 are 23 hours apart but less than 100 km apart). Samoa switched sides of the IDL in 2011 to align with Australia and New Zealand — its major trading partners — losing the "twice-lived" Saturday.',
   'fact', '2026-07-11'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0015-a1b2-c3d4e5f60015', 'geography', 'How Mountains Form',
   'Mountains form through three primary mechanisms: volcanic activity, tectonic collision (fold mountains), and fault-block uplifting. Most of Earth''s major mountain ranges were formed by the collision of tectonic plates over millions of years.',
   'Fold mountains (Himalayas, Alps, Appalachians) form when two continental plates collide: the oceanic or continental crust crumples upward since it cannot subduct. The Himalayas formed and continue to form from the Indian plate colliding with the Eurasian plate at 5 cm/year — Mount Everest grows about 4 mm annually. The Appalachians were once as tall as the Himalayas but have been eroded over 480 million years to their current modest heights. Volcanic mountains (Kilimanjaro, Fuji, Cascades) form over hot spots or subduction zones where magma breaches the crust. Fault-block mountains (Sierra Nevada, Basin and Range) form when blocks of crust are uplifted along normal faults. The Basin and Range province of the American Southwest shows this pattern dramatically: north-south parallel mountain ranges separated by broad valleys, all formed by east-west crustal extension over the past 30 million years.',
   'insight', '2026-07-12'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0016-a1b2-c3d4e5f60016', 'geography', 'Every Raindrop Has a Watershed Destination',
   'A watershed (also called a drainage basin or catchment) is the area of land that drains to a single point — a river, lake, or ocean. Every raindrop that falls on Earth eventually reaches one of these basins. Watershed boundaries are topographic ridgelines.',
   'The continental divide is the master watershed boundary: in North America, rain falling east of the Continental Divide ultimately reaches the Atlantic or Gulf of Mexico; rain falling west reaches the Pacific. In the Western U.S., some rain falls into "closed basins" — areas that drain to internal lakes with no outlet, where water leaves only by evaporation (Great Salt Lake, Nevada''s basins). The Amazon watershed covers 7 million km² — 40% of South America. The Mississippi-Missouri watershed covers 3.2 million km². Understanding watersheds is critical for water management: pollution in any part of a watershed affects the entire drainage system downstream. Deforestation in Himalayan watersheds contributes to flooding in Bangladesh''s Ganges-Brahmaputra delta. City planners use watershed analysis to manage stormwater, prevent flooding, and protect drinking water supplies.',
   'vocab', '2026-07-13'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0017-a1b2-c3d4e5f60017', 'geography', 'Why Monsoons Shape Billions of Lives',
   'The monsoon is not just rain — it is a seasonal reversal of winds that brings the annual rainfall cycle to 70% of the world''s population. The South Asian monsoon (India, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Myanmar) is the most consequential climate system for human civilization after the hydrological cycle itself.',
   'The monsoon mechanism: land heats faster than ocean in summer, creating a low-pressure system over the heated continent that draws in moisture-laden ocean air. When this air rises and cools over land, it drops its moisture as torrential rain. In India, 70-90% of annual rainfall falls during the June-September monsoon. Indian agriculture, water supply, and hydroelectric power all depend critically on monsoon timing and intensity. The El Niño Southern Oscillation (ENSO) is the primary predictor of monsoon variability: El Niño years typically bring weaker monsoons (drought risk); La Niña years bring stronger monsoons (flood risk). The 2002 Indian monsoon failure caused a 19% deficit that created severe food insecurity. West Africa, East Africa, Southeast Asia, and northern Australia also have monsoon seasons shaped by the same ocean-land heating differential. Climate change is altering monsoon timing and intensity in ways that remain difficult to predict with certainty.',
   'insight', '2026-07-14'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0018-a1b2-c3d4e5f60018', 'geography', 'Permafrost Holds Twice as Much Carbon as the Atmosphere',
   'Permafrost — ground that stays frozen for at least two years — underlies 25% of the Northern Hemisphere''s land area. It stores an estimated 1.5 trillion tons of carbon in frozen organic matter. As it thaws under warming temperatures, it releases methane and CO₂ in a feedback loop that could significantly accelerate climate change.',
   'Permafrost formed over thousands of years as dead plant matter accumulated in frozen ground without decomposing — the frozen conditions prevented the microbial breakdown that normally releases carbon back to the atmosphere. As Arctic temperatures rise at 2-4 times the global average rate ("Arctic amplification"), the active layer (the seasonally thawed surface) deepens, and the permafrost below begins to thaw. Thawing releases CO₂ under aerobic conditions and methane (28x more potent as a greenhouse gas) under anaerobic (waterlogged) conditions. The Siberian shelf — shallow Arctic Ocean water — stores frozen methane hydrates that are beginning to destabilize. "Zombie fires" (peat fires that smolder underground through winter and re-emerge in spring) are increasing as permafrost thaws. The IPCC''s 2022 report estimates that permafrost thaw could add 0.1-0.4°C of warming by 2100 beyond current projections — a feedback loop not fully captured in most climate models.',
   'insight', '2026-07-15'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0019-a1b2-c3d4e5f60019', 'geography', 'The Ring of Fire Explains Most Earthquakes and Volcanoes',
   'The Ring of Fire — a 40,000 km arc around the Pacific Ocean — is home to 75% of the world''s active volcanoes and 90% of the world''s earthquakes. It marks the boundaries of the Pacific Plate, where subduction drives both volcanic activity and seismic stress.',
   'Subduction — where one tectonic plate slides under another — is the primary driver of Ring of Fire activity. The dense oceanic Pacific Plate subducts beneath lighter continental plates around its perimeter. As the oceanic plate descends, it releases water into the mantle, lowering the melting point of mantle rock and generating magma that rises to the surface as volcanoes. The subducting plate also creates megathrust faults — the source of the world''s largest earthquakes. The 1960 Chilean earthquake (magnitude 9.5 — the largest ever recorded), the 2004 Indian Ocean earthquake (9.1), and the 2011 Tōhoku earthquake and tsunami (9.0) were all megathrust events on subduction zones. Japan sits at the junction of four tectonic plates and experiences 1,500 earthquakes annually, including numerous significant ones. The Pacific Northwest''s Cascadia Subduction Zone has not produced a major earthquake since 1700 but is overdue for a magnitude 9.0 event.',
   'fact', '2026-07-16'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0020-a1b2-c3d4e5f60020', 'geography', 'Ocean Currents Regulate Global Climate',
   'The thermohaline circulation — the ocean''s global conveyor belt — moves warm water from the tropics to the poles and cold deep water from the poles to the tropics, regulating global temperature far beyond what latitude alone would predict. Without it, northwestern Europe would be as cold as Hudson Bay.',
   'Thermohaline circulation (thermo = temperature, haline = salt) is driven by density differences: cold, salty water is denser and sinks; warm, fresh water is less dense and rises. In the North Atlantic, warm surface water from the Gulf Stream cools and sinks near Greenland and Iceland, driving the "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation" (AMOC). This brings warm water northward, keeping London 5-10°C warmer than its latitude would predict. Climate models suggest that melting of the Greenland ice sheet could inject so much fresh water into the North Atlantic that it disrupts AMOC — potentially causing dramatic cooling in Europe even as the world warms overall. Proxy evidence suggests AMOC has slowed by about 15% since 1950. The 2004 film "The Day After Tomorrow" dramatized this mechanism implausibly fast, but the underlying physics is real. AMOC collapse is one of several potential tipping points in the climate system.',
   'insight', '2026-07-17'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0021-a1b2-c3d4e5f60021', 'geography', 'Geography Rarely Matches Political Borders',
   'Most political borders in the world do not align with geographic, ecological, or linguistic boundaries — they reflect historical accidents, colonial decisions, and military outcomes. This mismatch creates persistent tensions between political maps and natural or cultural reality.',
   'Rivers seem like natural borders but are actually poor dividers: they are shared resources, their courses change, and they connect rather than separate the communities on their banks. The Rio Grande (US-Mexico border) shifts course periodically; the Oder-Neisse line (Germany-Poland) was a post-WWII political decision, not a geographic inevitability. The Sahara Desert is not a political barrier — its trade routes connected sub-Saharan Africa to the Mediterranean for millennia. Mountain ranges (Pyrenees, Alps, Himalayas) do function as partial barriers but are crossed by passes that have historically channeled trade and military movement. The most arbitrary borders are the straight lines drawn by European colonizers in Africa and the Middle East: the Berlin Conference of 1884-85 partitioned Africa with rulers on maps, ignoring existing kingdoms, ethnic distributions, and ecological zones. The consequences — ethnic conflict, resource disputes, difficult governance — persist 140 years later.',
   'insight', '2026-07-18'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0022-a1b2-c3d4e5f60022', 'geography', 'Megacities: When Urban Areas Exceed 10 Million',
   'In 1950, only New York exceeded 10 million people; today there are 34 megacities. By 2050, 68% of the world''s population is projected to live in urban areas, and Asia and Africa will host most of the new megacities — a transformation without historical precedent in scale or speed.',
   'The UN defines a megacity as a city with over 10 million inhabitants. Tokyo is the world''s largest (37 million in the greater metropolitan area), followed by Delhi (32 million) and Shanghai (28 million). Growth is fastest in Africa: Lagos is projected to exceed 30 million by 2035. The megacity era is historically unprecedented: as recently as 1800, only London and Beijing exceeded 1 million people. Megacity governance is challenging: no clear jurisdiction covers the full metropolitan area; services (water, sewage, transport) must be delivered at extraordinary scale; informality (slums, informal economies) is normal. Jakarta (22 million) is sinking faster than sea level rises due to groundwater extraction, forcing Indonesia''s capital to move to Borneo (Nusantara). São Paulo''s Paulista Avenue, which concentrates Brazil''s financial power, sits in a city of 22 million that regularly faces water shortages because its reservoirs were built for a fraction of its current population.',
   'insight', '2026-07-19'),
  ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-0023-a1b2-c3d4e5f60023', 'geography', 'Why the Global South Is Geographically Larger Than Maps Suggest',
   'The Mercator projection — the basis for most world maps people grew up with — systematically distorts the size of countries near the poles upward and shrinks those near the equator. Africa, South America, and South Asia are all dramatically larger than they appear.',
   'The Mercator projection (1569) was designed for maritime navigation: it preserves compass bearings (rhumb lines appear as straight lines) but massively distorts area. Greenland appears similar in size to Africa on a Mercator map; Africa is actually 14 times larger (30.4 million km² vs. 2.2 million km²). Europe appears similar in size to South America; South America is twice as large. The Global South''s apparent smallness on standard maps is not politically neutral: it contributed to colonial-era perceptions of Africa and Asia as peripheral and manageable. Arno Peters''s "Peters Projection" (1973) uses equal area but distorts shape — all flat maps involve a tradeoff between area fidelity and shape fidelity (proven by Gauss in the 19th century). The Winkel Tripel projection (used by National Geographic since 1998) and the Robinson projection minimize overall distortion without claiming to solve it. Tools like "The True Size of..." website allow users to drag countries to their actual comparative sizes.',
   'fact', '2026-07-20')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
